   `GExp hmet (α t) (s • curveVelocity (I := I) α t) = α (t + s)` (and the backward version with
   `-curveVelocity`).
   **Do not** try to prove that `s ↦ GExp z (s • w)` is `C^∞` at `0` on both sides — instead work
   with the *chosen geodesic segments* `geodCurve`, which are two-sided smooth by construction:
   fix `T_max ≥ t + s` and set `Gp := geodCurve hmet T_max _ p u hu` (a unit-speed geodesic on
   `Ioo (-δ) (T_max + δ)`).  Then `α r = Gp r` for `r ∈ [0, T_max]`
   (by `GExp_apply`/`GExp_smul` + `geodCurve_agree`).  Put `z := Gp t`, `w := curveVelocity Gp t`
   and `Gz := geodCurve hmet T_max _ z w hw`; both `s ↦ Gz s` and `s ↦ Gp (t + s)` are smooth
   geodesics on an open interval around `0` with the same value `z` and the same velocity `w` at
   `0`, so `eqOn_Ioo_of_isGeodesicAt_of_eq` gives `Gz s = Gp (t + s)`.  Finally
   `GExp z (s • w) = (geodCurve (T := s) z (unitOf w) _) s = Gz s` (again `geodCurve_agree`),
   whence `GExp z (s • w) = α (t + s)`.  The backward version is the same statement applied to
   the unit direction `-w` (note `GExp z (s • (-w)) = GExp z ((-s) • w)`).  This also yields the
   **extended ray property**: `t ↦ GExp p (t • u)` is `C^∞` and geodesic on every `(0, T)`
   (it agrees there with the single `geodCurve`).
2. **The recursion / closed + right-open argument.**  Let `ρ` be the ball-surjectivity radius of
   `exists_eq_expMap_of_riemannianEDist_lt` at `p`, truncated by the GExp-agreement radius
   `exists_pos_GExp_eq_expMap`.  If `d(p,q) < ofReal ρ` we are done (ball surjectivity +
   `GExp = expMap`).  Otherwise `L := (d(p,q)).toReal ≥ ρ > 0`; take `ε := ρ/2 < L` and the
   minimiser `y` of `d(·,q)` on the sphere `S_ε(p)` (`Commons.Geometry.Manifold.riemannianEDist_min_on_sphere`,
   using the nonemptiness and finiteness lemmas above and continuity of the distance); then
   `d(p,q) = ofReal ε + d(y,q)` and `y = GExp hmet p v₀` with `‖v₀‖ = ε`; let
   `u := ε⁻¹ • v₀`, `α t := GExp hmet p (t • u)`.
   Let `S := {t ∈ [0,L] : d(p, α t) = ofReal t ∧ d(α t, q) = ofReal (L - t)}`.  Then
   `0 ∈ S`, `ε ∈ S`, `S` is closed (continuity), and `S` is *right-open*: for `t ∈ S`, `t < L`
   and `r < min(ρ, L - t)` apply the sphere-minimiser lemma at the point `α t` to get `y'` with
   `d(α t, y') = ofReal r`, `d(y',q) = ofReal (L - t - r)`; the telescoping gives
   `d(p,y') = ofReal (t + r)` (so the broken path `p → α t → y'` is minimising), and the corner
   cut (`ξ := α'(t)`, `η := r⁻¹ • (φ y')`) shows that the two outer directions agree, i.e.
   `y' = GExp hmet (α t) (r • α'(t)) = α (t + r)` by `GExp_ray_continue`.  Hence
   `[t, t+r] ⊆ S` and the standard closed + right-open argument gives `[0,L] ⊆ S`, in particular
   `d(α L, q) = 0`, so `α L = q` and `v := L • u` does the job.
   (The corner-cut use needs `d(p, GExp (α t) ((-r) • α'(t))) = ofReal (t - r)`, i.e. the
   *backward* ray property of item 1.)
3. Then, in the application, `M` compact + `SimplyConnectedSpace M` gives `ConnectedSpace M` and
   `PathConnectedSpace M` (the latter is an instance from `SimplyConnectedSpace`), so Hopf–Rinow
