### 20.3 STRATEGIC REDUCTION (read this before planning the next steps)

The classical proof of the theorem splits into three inputs:

1. **Myers** `diam ≤ π/√K_min` — DONE (`GlobalBM.riemannianEDist_le_pi`).
2. **Klingenberg's injectivity radius estimate**.  In the normalisation `sec ∈ [1,4)`
   (i.e. `K_max < 4`), for a **closed simply connected** manifold it states `inj > π/2`
   (Wikipedia "Berger's sphere", citing Cheeger–Ebin 2008 Ch. 5 and Petersen 2016 §6.5; the Berger
   spheres show that the curvature bound cannot be dropped).  The even-dimensional companion
   (`sec > 0`, closed orientable, even dimension ⟹ `inj ≥ π/√K_max`) is Klingenberg 1959 and fails
   in odd dimensions.  Structurally (Xu, arXiv:1401.5549, Thm. 1.3; Klingenberg 1959/1995):
   `inj(p) = min{ econj(p), (half the length of the shortest geodesic loop at p) }` and
   `inj(M) = min{ econj(M), (half the length of the shortest closed geodesic in M) }`, where `econj`
   is the *essential* conjugate radius.  Klingenberg's lemma: the nearest cut point is either
   conjugate (along every minimal geodesic), or it is the meeting point of exactly two minimal
   geodesics, which form a geodesic loop at `p` that is smooth at the cut point.  So `inj ≥ π/2`
   needs (a) `conj ≥ π/2` — **DONE this session** (`RauchGlobal.eqOn_zero_of_jacobi`) — and (b) *no
   closed geodesic loop at `p` of length `< π`*.  Part (b) is the hard part; in even dimensions it
   follows from the second variation with a *periodic parallel normal field* (the holonomy around a
   null-homotopic loop lies in `SO(n-1)`, `n-1` odd, hence has a fixed vector —
   `Commons.LinearAlgebra.InnerProductSpace.exists_fixed_vector_of_odd_finrank_det_one` exists), and
   in odd dimensions from Klingenberg 1961 via **Morse theory on the free loop space**
   (the "long homotopy lemma": a non-trivial contractible closed geodesic of length `< 2conj(M)`
   forces every null homotopy to pass through a curve of length `≥ 2conj(M)`).
3. **Grove–Shiohama's diameter sphere theorem**: `sec > 1` and `diam > π/2` ⟹ `M ≅ S^n` (the
   sharper cited form: transverse curvature `> 1` and diameter `> π/2`, arXiv:2505.01378).  Its
   proof uses **Toponogov's hinge comparison** plus the **critical point theory of the distance
   function** (isotopy lemma).

**The reduction that avoids the strictness problem**: the hypotheses of the challenge are
`1 < K ≤ 4` pointwise, hence `K_min > 1` (the infimum is attained, by compactness of the
Grassmann bundle) and `K_min/K_max > 1/4` *strictly*.  Scaling the metric by `1/K_min` therefore
gives a metric with `sec ≥ 1` and `sec < 4` — exactly the hypotheses of Klingenberg's second
estimate.  Then `inj > π/2`, so `diam > π/2`, and Grove–Shiohama applies.  (With `sec ∈ [1,4]`
non-strictly this fails: the round sphere of curvature `4` has `diam = inj = π/2`, and `ℂP^n` with
the Fubini–Study metric is a non-sphere with `sec ∈ [1,4]` — but it is not simply connected.)

So the **only** genuinely open mathematical input is (2b), the absence of short closed geodesics;
everything else is either done or classical-but-long (Toponogov, the critical point theory, the
cut locus theory, the assembly — the last being already available through
`Commons.Topology.Sphere.two_disk_cover`).

