### 24.7 A shortcut route to the first variation: the CHORDAL BOUND for `exp`

There is a route to `ρ'(0) = -cos θ` (and hence to a first-order Toponogov) that avoids the cut
locus theory entirely and reuses already-proved material.  The key statement is

  **`chordal`**: for `x₀ ∈ M` there is `ρ > 0` such that for all `u w : E` with `‖u‖, ‖w‖ < ρ`,
  `d(expMap x₀ u, expMap x₀ w) ≤ ‖u - w‖`  (the "euclidean chordal" bound; equivalently,
  `exp_p` is `1`-Lipschitz for the *flat* metric on `T_pM`).

This is a **weaker** statement than the model-metric version, and it is exactly what the
first variation formula needs: with `u = -ε·c'(L)` and `w = t·σ'(0)` in `T_pM`
(`c` the minimizing geodesic from `q` to `p`), the triangle inequality
`d(q,σ t) ≤ d(q, c(L-ε)) + d(c(L-ε), σ t) = (L-ε) + d(exp_p u, exp_p w)`
gives `d(q,σ t) ≤ (L-ε) + ‖u - w‖`, and choosing `ε = √t` gives
`d(q,σ t) ≤ L + t⟨c'(L),σ'(0)⟩ + O(t^{3/2})`, i.e. the first variation inequality.

*Proof plan for `chordal`.*  Let `α(s) = u + s(w-u)`.  Then `s ↦ exp_p(α(s))` joins the two points,
so it suffices to bound its length by `∫‖α'‖ = ‖w-u‖`, i.e. to prove the **pointwise** bound
`‖(d exp_p)_v(x)‖ ≤ ‖x‖` for all `v, x`, at least for `‖v‖ < ρ`.
Decompose `x = x_∥ + x_⊥` w.r.t. `v` (`x_∥ = (⟨x,v⟩/‖v‖²) v`):
* by **`gauss_lemma_fderiv`** (`Submission/Gauss.lean`), `(d exp_p)_v(v)` is orthogonal to
  `(d exp_p)_v(X)` whenever `⟨v,X⟩ = 0`, and `‖(d exp_p)_v(v)‖ = ‖v‖`;
* hence `‖(d exp_p)_v(x)‖² = ‖(d exp_p)_v(x_∥)‖² + ‖(d exp_p)_v(x_⊥)‖² = ‖x_∥‖² + ‖(d exp)_v(x_⊥)‖²`;
* it remains to bound the normal part.  For this use **`exists_geodesicVariation`**
  (`Submission/Gauss.lean`): with base `x₀`, velocity `v` and direction `x_⊥` it gives a two-parameter
  family `Φ` with `expMap x₀ (s • (v + t • x_⊥)) = (extChartAt I x₀).symm (Φ (s,t)).1`; put
  `y_t(s) = (Φ (s,t)).1`.  Then `∂_t y_t(s)|_{t=0}` is the **variation field of a variation by
  geodesics**, hence a Jacobi field: it satisfies the chart Jacobi system
  (`Submission/JacobiTensor.lean`, `jacobiSys`) with initial data `y(0) = 0`, `D_s y(0) = x_⊥`
  (uniqueness of the linear ODE solution plus `Φ(0,t) = (coords x₀, v + t•x_⊥)`);
* therefore `(d exp_p)_v(x_⊥) = ∂_t y_t(1)|_{t=0} = (D.Y 1 x_⊥)` where `Y` is the Jacobi tensor of
  `Submission/RauchUpper.lean` built from `Φ(·,0)`, and **`JData.rauch_upper_linear`** gives
  `‖Y 1 x_⊥‖² ≤ 1²·‖x_⊥‖² = ‖x_⊥‖²`  (this is the "`exp` is `1`-Lipschitz in the euclidean sense"
  corollary: with unit chart speed, `‖J(1)‖ ≤ 1·‖D_sJ(0)‖`).
* Combining: `‖(d exp_p)_v(x)‖² ≤ ‖x_∥‖² + ‖x_⊥‖² = ‖x‖²`.

Everything needed is in the library *except* two identifications:
(a) the **variation field of a geodesic variation is a Jacobi field** (in the chart system
    `jacobiSys` of `Submission/JacobiTensor.lean`), and
(b) `(d exp_p)_v(x) = (D.Y 1 x)` — i.e. `mfderiv_expMap_comp` composed with the identification of
    `∂_t y_t(1)|_{t=0}` above.
Both are uniqueness-of-ODE statements, so (a)+(b) together are: prove that `t ↦ (∂_t y_t(s))` solves
`jacobiSys` and matches the initial data, then use uniqueness of the solution of the linear system
(the same uniqueness that makes `JacobiTensor.exists_jacobiSystem` well defined).

*Consequence*: `chordal` ⟹ first variation ⟹ `hinge_comparison_dist` becomes unconditional once the
Hessian bound `DistHessianBound` is available.

*Remark (avoiding Rauch II).*  One might think the normal-part bound needs **Rauch II** (the focal
comparison, `∇J(0) = 0 ⟹ |J(t)| ≤ |J(0)|`).  It does not.  There are two identifications of
`(d exp_p)_v(x)` with a Jacobi field along `t ↦ exp_p(t v)`:
* `C(s,t) = exp_p(t·(v + s x))` gives `J(0) = 0`, `∇J(0) = x` (**Rauch I** data), and
* `C(s,t) = exp_p(t v + s x)` gives `J(0) = x`, `∇J(0) = 0` (**Rauch II** data);
both have `J(1) = (d exp_p)_v(x)`.  Using the first, `Rauch`'s upper comparison theorem
(`JData.rauch_upper`, hence `rauch_upper_linear`) gives
`|J(1)| ≤ sin(‖v‖)·‖x‖/‖v‖ ≤ ‖x‖` **provided `x ⊥ v`**, and Gauss's lemma supplies both the radial
identity `|(d exp_p)_v(x_∥)| = ‖x_∥‖` and the orthogonality of the two images.  So Rauch II is *not*
needed — only the more elementary identification (a)/(b) above.  (Rauch II with the flat model is
still worth having for other purposes; it is the Riccati equation with the *focal* initial condition
`S(0) = 0` in place of `S ~ 1/t`, giving `S ≤ 0` from `S' + S² + R = 0` and `R ≥ 0`, and hence
`(|J|²)' ≤ 0` — the same `ShapeOperator` machinery of `Submission/RauchUpper.lean` with a different
initial condition, and much easier than the conjugate-point case since `Y` is invertible
everywhere.)

