### 27.2 Why the interval generalisation was unavoidable

`JData`'s `hunit` says the *chart* speed of the geodesic is `1`, i.e. the geodesic is unit speed.
The chart geodesic produced by `Gauss.exists_geodesicVariation` for a velocity `v` of *small*
ambient norm has chart speed `c = √⟨v,v⟩` (small, but not `1`), and it is defined on the fixed
interval `Ioo (-2) 2`.  Rescaling the parameter to unit speed (`s ↦ s/c`) shrinks the interval to
`(-2c, 2c)`, which does **not** contain `(-2,2)`; conversely the *speed-c* description keeps the
interval but fails `hunit = 1`.  Hence the chain had to be restated on an arbitrary interval.  The
`_gen` proofs of `RauchUpper` transfer verbatim because they only ever use `W.mem`.

The invertibility hypothesis of the `Window` (`hYinv`) is supplied by the **conjugate radius bound**
`π/2` in the unit-speed parametrisation; this is `RauchGen.isInvertible_Y_gen`, proved through the
*global* Rauch theorem `RauchGlobal.eqOn_zero_of_jacobi` (germ-level `IsJacobiAt`, no interval
restriction).  This is why `c < π/3` appears in `rauch_bound_rescaled`: the evaluation point is
`t = c` and the window is `(0, 5c/4)`, so `5c/4 < π/2` is needed.

