* `modelAngle w t s = arccos ((cos w - cos t cos s)/(sin t sin s))` — the model angle opposite the
  side `w`; **`modelDist1_modelAngle`** (the law of cosines: `modelDist1 (modelAngle w t s) t s = w`)
  and **`modelDist1_le_iff`** (the model distance is a strictly monotone-injective function of the
  enclosed angle, so comparisons of model distances are equivalent to comparisons of angles);
* **`geodCurve_sub_arc_minimising`** — sub-arcs of a minimising `geodCurve` are minimising (the tool
  that splits the sides of a hinge).

### 31.3 THE PLAN FOR THE GLOBALISATION (the *Key Lemma*)

Following the notes `arXiv:2404.09792` §4.1: for `0 < l ≤ π`,
`(∀ z ∈ B_l(p), CompHolds z ((2/3)l)) → CompHolds p l`.

*Proof structure (the notes' argument, in our language).*  Suppose a hinge at `z` with unit `v₁`,
`v₂`, lengths `t`, `s` and `(2/3)l ≤ t + s < l` violates the comparison (smaller hinges satisfy it by
the hypothesis).  Let `X = exp_z(t v₁)`, `Y = exp_z(s v₂)`, `X' = exp_z(s₁ v₂)` with `s₁` chosen so
that `t + 3s₁ = (2/3)l`, WLOG `t ≤ s`.

* *sub-hinge 1* (apex `z`, directions `v₁`, `v₂`, lengths `t`, `s₁`): its size is `< (2/3)l`, both
  directions are known, so the hypothesis gives `w := d(X', X) ≤ modelDist1 θ t s₁`;
* *sub-hinge 2* (apex `X'`, the direction towards `z` is the reversed side — this is where
  `Submission.GeoBack.geodCurve_sub_eq_expMap` (for `geodCurve`-based sides) or the `GExp` API is
  needed — and the direction towards `X` is a *chosen* minimising geodesic, from Hopf–Rinow): its
  size is `< (2/3)l` (using `w ≤ t + s₁`), so the hypothesis gives `t ≤ modelDist1 α' s₁ w` where
  `α'` is the angle at `X'` between the two directions;
* *the model computation* (pure trigonometry, see 31.4) turns these two inequalities into
  `modelDist1 (π - α') w (s - s₁) ≤ modelDist1 θ t s < d(X,Y)`, i.e. the comparison also fails at
  the *new* hinge (apex `X'`, lengths `s - s₁` and `w`, angle `π - α'`), whose size is `≤ t + s`.

Iterating produces a sequence of hinges with non-increasing size `rₙ ∈ [(2/3)l, l]`; either some
`r_N ≤ (2/3)l` (then the hypothesis gives the comparison, contradicting the failure), or the sizes
tend to a limit in `[(2/3)l, l]` with `rₙ - r_{n+1} → 0`, and a compactness argument (the sides stay
bounded away from `0`) produces a limiting configuration in which the model angles tend to `π`, so
that `lim dₙ ≥ d(X,Y)`, contradicting the strict decrease of `dₙ`.

### 31.4 THE PURE-TRIGONOMETRY INPUT (commons, in flight/committed)

* `Commons.Geometry.Sphere.modelDist_extension` (COMMITTED): for `sin s₁ ≠ 0` and
  `sin (arccos (cos t cos s₁ + sin t sin s₁ cos θ)) ≠ 0`,
