## 37. SESSION 44: THE CLASSICAL PROOF STRUCTURE OF THE SPHERE THEOREM (from the literature)

This section records the *classical* proof structure, read off from the primary sources this session.
It supersedes several earlier guesses and it is the reference for the remaining work.

### 37.1 THE SOURCES

* **Berger 1960**, *Les variétés riemanniennes (1/4)-pincées*, Ann. Scuola Norm. Sup. Pisa (3) **14**
  (1960) 161–170, available at `http://www.numdam.org/item/ASNSP_1960_3_14_2_161_0/`
  (the local copy of the text is `/tmp/b60.txt`).  This is the paper that proves the sphere theorem
  in even dimensions once Klingenberg's estimate is available; it *states* all the ingredients
  (Theorems 3, 4, 5 = Klingenberg, Klingenberg's assembly, Toponogov) and proves the "Berger
  lemmas" 1 and 2 that reduce the global geometry to them.
* **Berger 1961**, *Les variétés riemanniennes homogènes normales simplement connexes à courbure
  strictement positive*, Ann. Scuola Norm. Sup. Pisa (3) **15** (1961) 179–246
  (`http://www.numdam.org/item/ASNSP_1961_3_15_3_179_0/`, local text `/tmp/berger.txt`) — this is
  about homogeneous normal spaces, **not** the general proof; do not follow it for the geometry.
* **Klingenberg 1961**, *Über Riemannsche Mannigfaltigkeiten mit positiver Krümmung*, Comment. Math.
  Helv. **35** (1961) 47–54 (`https://eudml.org/doc/139206`; the full text was **not** retrievable
  — the PDF is behind Springer, `doi:10.1007/BF02567004`).  This is the paper that removes the
  even-dimensional restriction.  **Its argument is not known to us** (see §37.5).
* **Shankar–Sormani** arXiv:0709.2331, local text `/tmp/ss.txt` — a length-space version of the
  *long homotopy lemma* (Thm 6.2), of the cut-locus lemma (Lemma 8.1) and of the injectivity-radius
  theorem (Thm 8.3).  Their Open Problem 12.2 says explicitly that *"the Riemannian long homotopy
  lemma has also often been used **in combination with Morse Theory** to prove the existence of
  smooth closed geodesics"* — i.e. the long homotopy lemma alone is **not** enough.
* **Brendle–Schoen**, *Curvature, sphere theorems and the Ricci flow*, arXiv:1001.2278, local text
  `/tmp/bs.txt`: "the classical proof of the Topological Sphere Theorem relies on comparison geometry
  techniques (see e.g. [44], Chapter 6)" — [44] = Cheeger–Ebin, *Comparison theorems in Riemannian
  geometry*, Chapter 6.
* The lecture notes `arXiv:2404.09792` (local text `/tmp/cg2.txt`) give the Grove–Shiohama proof
  (§5.2.3) and the local Toponogov comparison (§3.3).

### 37.2 BERGER'S NORMALISATION AND THE EXACT STATEMENTS USED

Berger normalises the metric so that the pinching constant is `δ = 1`, i.e. **`1/4 < K ≤ 1`**, and
writes `d(V)` for the diameter and `Geo(p,q)` for the set of minimising geodesics from `p` to `q`.
Then:

* (Myers, classical) `d(V) ≤ π/√(1/4) = 2π`.
* **THÉORÈME 3 (Klingenberg, [4] théorème 1 p. 655)** — *even dimension only*:
  `V` compact, simply connected, **even dimension**, strictly positive curvature.  If `p, q ∈ V` with
  `d(p,q) ≥ π` then `Geo(p,q)` has **exactly one** element; in particular `d(V) ≥ π`.
* **THÉORÈME 4 (Klingenberg, [4] p. 664–666)** — the assembly, *and it does not use the dimension*:
  if (a) for all `r, s` with `d(r,s) ≥ π` the set `Geo(r,s)` has exactly one element, and
  (b) there exist `p, q` such that for every `r ∈ V`, `d(r,p) ≥ π` or `d(r,q) ≥ π`,
  then `V` is homeomorphic to `S^{dim V}`.
* **THÉORÈME 5 (Toponogov, [6] théorème 1 p. 719)** — the global hinge comparison for a
  `δ`-pinched manifold, with the model space of curvature `δ`: for a triangle with two sides
  `a, b ≤ π` and included angle `γ` satisfying `cos a cos b + sin a sin b cos γ ≥ 0`, the third side
  `c` satisfies `c ≤ c̃` where `c̃` is the third side of the model triangle.  (Corollary 1 of
  Théorème 5 is the spherical cosine-law consequence used in Lemma 2.)
* **LEMME 1 (Berger)** — for a *diameter pair* `p, q` (`d(p,q) = d(V)`) and any unit vector `u` at
  `p`, there is a minimising geodesic from `p` to `q` whose initial direction makes an angle `≤ π/2`
  with `u` (in modern language: `p` is a **critical point of `d(·,q)`**).  Proof uses Théorème 5.
* **LEMME 2 (Berger)** — for a compact `δ`-pinched `V` and a diameter pair `p, q`: for every
  `r ∈ V`, `d(r,p) ≥ π` or `d(r,q) ≥ π`.  Proof: Lemma 1 + Corollary 1 of Théorème 5.
* **Proof of THÉORÈME 1** (compact simply connected, `δ > 1/4`, **even dimension** ⟹ `≅ S^{dim V}`):
  pick a diameter pair `p, q`; Théorème 3 gives (a) (and `d(V) ≥ π`), Lemma 2 gives (b), and
  Théorème 4 concludes.  ∎
* THÉORÈME 2 treats the rigidity case `δ = 1/4` (classification as a rank-one symmetric space).

### 37.3 WHAT THIS MEANS FOR THE PROJECT

1. **The normalisation matters.**  In our statement `QuarterPinched cov` means `1 < K ≤ 4`
   (`ChallengeDeps.QuarterPinched`).  Write `κ` for the infimum and `K` for the supremum of the
   sectional curvature over orthonormal pairs (both attained, since the unit tangent sphere bundle is
   compact and the curvature function is continuous).  Then `1 < κ` and `K ≤ 4`, hence
   `4κ > K` **strictly**.  The two estimates we need are
   * Rauch: `conj(M) ≥ π/√K`  (normalised to `K ≤ 4` this is the *proved* `conj ≥ π/2`), and
   * Klingenberg: `inj(M) ≥ π/√K`, hence `diam(M) ≥ π/√K > π/(2√κ)`;
   then **Grove–Shiohama in the scale-invariant form**, `sec ≥ k` and `diam > π/(2√k)` ⟹
   `M ≅ S^n`, applied with `k = κ`, finishes.  Equivalently (and this is what Berger does) one
   rescales the metric by `1/κ` so that `sec ∈ [1, 4/κ)` with `4/κ < 4`, runs Rauch + Klingenberg
   with the *upper* bound `< 4`, and applies Grove–Shiohama with `sec ≥ 1`.
   **Note `K ≤ 4` alone gives only `diam ≥ π/2`, not `> π/2`**: the round sphere of curvature `4`
   has `diam = inj = π/2` and *is* a sphere, so the strictness of `κ > 1` — equivalently
   `K < 4κ` — is exactly what makes the argument work.
2. **Grove–Shiohama replaces Théorème 4.**  The team has already reduced the assembly to
   `Commons.Topology.Sphere.two_disk_cover_strong` / `homeo_sphere_of_punctured_euclidean`; the
   modern route (notes §5.2.3) is: `diam > π/(2√k)` ⟹ (Step 1) `d(·,p)` is concave outside
   `B_{π/2}(p)` and has a *unique* maximum at `q`; (Step 2) it has **no critical point** outside
   `{p,q}` — case 3 of Step 2 is exactly the *hinge comparison* for legs `≤ π/2` and angle `≤ π/2`;
   (Step 3) the flow box `Commons.Topology.Metric.homeo_level_times_interval`; (Step 4) the twisted
   sphere.  So Grove–Shiohama needs **Toponogov too**, but only for hinges with both legs `≤ π/2`.
3. **Klingenberg's estimate is the deep input.**  It is: no closed geodesic of length `< 2π/√K` in
   a compact simply connected manifold with `sec ≤ K`.  Equivalently (via Klingenberg's cut-locus
   lemma, `Shankar–Sormani` Lemma 8.1, which is `VHinge`-free geometry): the nearest cut point of
   `p` is either conjugate to `p` along a minimising geodesic, or the far end of a geodesic loop of
   length `2 inj(p)`.  Since Rauch gives `conj ≥ π/√K` (proved), the conjugate branch is ruled out
   as soon as `inj(M) ≤ π/√K`, so the **only** missing input is: *there is no null-homotopic closed
   geodesic of length `< 2π/√K`*.

### 37.4 CORRECTION OF §37.3(2)'s EARLIER VERSION (§25/§31.5 OF THIS FILE)

Earlier sessions asserted that in **odd** dimensions Klingenberg's estimate "follows from the long
homotopy lemma alone".  **That is not right.**  The long homotopy lemma (Shankar–Sormani Thm 6.2 /
Klingenberg 1962) says: if `c` is a *contractible* closed geodesic with `ℓ(c) < 2 conj(M)`, then
**any** null-homotopy of `c` contains a curve of length `≥ 2 conj(M)`.  This does *not* by itself
forbid `c` (the homotopy may pass through long curves on its way from `c` to a point); one needs an
existence theory (Morse theory of the free loop space, as Shankar–Sormani's Open Problem 12.2
explicitly notes) to produce a closed geodesic that is a *minimax* and can then be shortened.  So:
* even dimensions: the second-variation argument with a **periodic parallel normal field** (needs the
  holonomy at a null-homotopic loop to lie in `SO(n-1)` with `n-1` odd — commons lemma
  `Commons.LinearAlgebra.InnerProductSpace.exists_fixed_vector_of_odd_finrank_det_one` is available)
  gives a shorter closed *curve*; the passage from "shorter closed curve" to "shorter closed
  geodesic" is the same Morse-theoretic step.
* odd dimensions: Klingenberg 1961; the method is **not known to this project** (§37.5).

