`g = e^{-2/x²} dx²`.  **That counterexample is not a Riemannian manifold** (`g` vanishes at the
origin, so the metric is degenerate there, contradicting positive definiteness) — but the *conclusion*
is nevertheless right for a different reason: `chartAt H z` is arbitrary atlas data, so one may
choose an atlas whose charts are arbitrarily sheared at the relevant point, and then
`chartGram z z a a = ⟪a, a⟫_{fibre at z}` (which uses the chart-z identification, `frameVector_self`)
can be made arbitrarily small relative to the model norm.  So **(UCB) is genuinely unavailable**.

The *right* formulation is chart-invariant, and it is provable:

*   **`exists_radius_contMDiffAt_expMap` made uniform, in the fibre norm**:
    `∃ρ>0, ∀ (z : M) (v : TangentSpace I z), ‖v‖ < ρ → ContMDiffAt 𝓘(ℝ,E) I ∞ (fun w => expMap cov z w) v`.
    This statement *is* chart-invariant (the vector `v : TangentSpace I z = E` is read in the chart at
    `z`, and `expMap cov z w` uses the same chart, so changing the atlas conjugates the statement by
    the derivative of the chart change at `z`, which also conjugates the fibre norm).  Proof plan:
    1. fix a chart `φ` of the atlas; on a compact `K` inside its source, the fibre norm of a vector
       given in `φ`-coordinates is uniformly equivalent to the `φ`-coordinate model norm, because the
       chart-metric coefficient function `g^φ` is smooth and positive definite on the compact set
       `φ(K)`;
    2. in `φ`-coordinates, a geodesic is a solution of the (autonomous, phase-space) ODE
       `(u,w)' = (w, chartGeodesicVectorField φ (u,w))`; the set of initial data `{(φ z, w) : z ∈ K,
       ‖w‖_{g^φ} ≤ 2}` is compact, so the uniform ODE solution theorem
       (`Commons.Analysis.ODE.exists_uniform_secondOrderODE_solution_in_open`) gives a radius
       `T > 0` uniform over `K` and smooth dependence on the initial data;
    3. `expMap cov z v` is the time-one point of the geodesic through `z` with velocity `v`, and that
       geodesic satisfies the `φ`-coordinate geodesic equation
       (`GeodesicGlobal.chartODE_of_isGeodesic` with centre `φ`), so its time-one point is a smooth
       function of the `φ`-coordinates of the initial data (ODE smooth dependence + uniqueness
       `Commons.Analysis.ODE.secondOrderODE_eqOn_of_contDiffAt`);
    4. cover `M` by finitely many such chart domains (compactness) and take the minimum of the
       finitely many radii.
    The pieces 1 + 2 are commons-context (pure ODE + calculus); pieces 3–4 are project-specific.
*   With the uniform exp smoothness, redo route (M) (`HingeLocal.edist_expMap_le_model`) with the
    point-dependent ingredients `ρb` (`ModelBound.mfderiv_expMap_model_bound_polar`) and
    `ρsm` (`exists_radius_contMDiffAt_expMap`) replaced by their uniform counterparts.  NOTE that
