## 38. SESSION 44 (cont.): THE ATLAS DEPENDENCE OF `expMap` — A CORRECTION OF THE §37.5 PLAN

This section records why the *uniform* local comparison radius cannot be proved through
`Submission.Geodesic.expMap`, and what replaces it.  It supersedes §37.5(B1)'s concrete plan.

### 38.1 THE DEFINITIONS AT ISSUE

```
def HasChartGeodesic cov x v u w : Prop :=
  u 0 = (extChartAt I x) x ∧
  w 0 = triv_x.continuousLinearMapAt ℝ x v ∧
  (∀ t ∈ Ioo (-2) 2, u t ∈ (extChartAt I x).target) ∧      -- stays in the chart at x
  (∀ t ∈ Ioo (-2) 2, HasDerivAt u (w t) t) ∧
  (∀ t ∈ Ioo (-2) 2, HasDerivAt w (chartGeodesicVectorField cov x (u t, w t)) t)

def expMap cov x v := if h : ∃ u w, HasChartGeodesic cov x v u w
                      then (extChartAt I x).symm ((Classical.choose h) 1) else x
```

Two features matter.
* The chart-geodesic equation has to be solved on the **whole fixed interval `Ioo (-2) 2`** and
  the solution has to **stay in the chart at `x`** — a condition whose *geometric* strength depends
  on how large the chart at `x` is, i.e. on the chosen atlas.
* `continuousLinearMapAt ℝ x` converts the model-space element `v` into the chart-`x` *coordinate*
  velocity.  A vector of small **fibre** norm can therefore have an arbitrarily large
  **coordinate-model** norm if the chart at `x` is badly scaled.

### 38.2 THE OBSTRUCTION (this is why (US) is FALSE)

Let `M = ℝ` with the standard smooth structure and the standard metric, but regard it as a
`ChartedSpace ℝ ℝ` whose atlas consists of the *restrictions of the identity to tiny intervals*
(all mutually compatible, so all the hypotheses `IsManifold 𝓘(ℝ,ℝ) ∞ ℝ` hold) and with
`chartAt H x` chosen to be such a tiny chart around `x` with half-width `ε_x > 0`.  Then
`(extChartAt I x).target` is a model interval of radius `ε_x`.

* For the standard metric, `chartGram x x v v = ‖v‖²` (the chart coefficient is `1`), so "fibre norm
  small" is "`‖v‖ = √(chartGram x x v v)` small" — no compensation takes place.
* The chart-`x` solution of the geodesic equation with coordinate velocity `v` is the straight line
  `t ↦ t v`.  It stays in the target on `Ioo (-2) 2` **iff** `2 ‖v‖ ≤ ε_x`.  Hence for
  `‖v‖ ∈ (ε_x / 2, ∞)` the definiens of `expMap cov x v` takes the **junk branch** and
  `expMap cov x v = x`.

Consequently, if `ε_{x_n} → 0` along a sequence, then for **every** `ρ > 0` there are `n` and `v`
with `√(chartGram x_n x_n v v) = ‖v‖ < ρ` such that `w ↦ expMap cov x_n w` has a **jump
discontinuity** at the fibre-norm radius `ε_{x_n}/2 < ρ` (from the geodesic endpoint down to the
junk value `x_n`).  So neither

* `∃ρ>0, ∀z ∀v, ‖v‖_fibre < ρ → ContMDiffAt 𝘭(ℝ,E) I ∞ (fun w => expMap cov z w) v`
  (§37.5(B1) (US)), nor
* the same statement with the extra clause "`expMap cov z v` is not the junk value",

is a theorem.  The *pointwise* statements (`exists_radius_contMDiffAt_expMap x`,
`exists_pos_hasChartGeodesic x`, `HingeLocal.edist_expMap_le_model x`) remain true — their radii
just depend on `x` and may tend to `0` along a sequence of *atlas* charts.  This is the same
phenomenon as the §35 refutation (the atlas is not tied to the metric), and it is the reason that
"uniformise route (M)" is *not* a viable plan.

### 38.3 WHAT IS CHART-INVARIANT: `GExp`

`Submission.GlobalExp.GExp hmet x v` is defined through `geodCurve` (Hopf–Rinow): it is the point
at arc length `‖v‖_fibre` from `x` in the direction of `v`.  Its defining property
(`GExp_spec`) is that the underlying curve is `C^∞` on a neighbourhood of `[0,T]`, is a geodesic
and is unit speed — all **intrinsic** statements.  Therefore

* the *statement* `∃ρ > 0, ∀ z, GCompHolds hmet z ρ` (the uniform local comparison radius, the only
  input of `HingeKey2.gcompHolds_pi_of_uniform`) is chart-invariant, and
* the correct exponential map for uniform statements is `GExp` (or any construction from a *fixed*
  chart's ODE flow, see §38.4), never `expMap`.

### 38.4 THE REPLACEMENT: A CHART-FLOW EXPONENTIAL MAP

Fix a chart `φ = extChartAt I z₀` and a compact `Z ⊆ φ.source`.  In `φ`-coordinates the pair
`(u, u')` of a geodesic satisfies the *autonomous* phase-space ODE
`z' = Fφ z`, `Fφ (y,w) = (w, chartGeodesicVectorField cov z₀ (y,w))`, which is `C^∞` on the open
set `(extChartAt I z₀).target ×ˢ univ` (`GeodesicGlobal.contDiffOn_chartSpray'`).  The data set
`{(φ z, w) : z ∈ Z, √(chartGram x₀ z w w) ≤ 1}` is **compact** (the fibre-norm-`1` "ellipsoid" at
`z` is contained in the closed model ball of radius `λ^{-1/2}` by the *fixed-chart* bound
`ChartBounds.exists_chartGram_bounds x₀ Z`), so the uniform flow theorem on compact sets
(commons: `Analysis/ODE/uniform_flow_compact_open`, requested this session) gives

* a uniform existence radius `ρ_flow > 0` for `|t| ≤ ρ_flow`,
* smooth dependence of the time-`t` map on the data, and
* uniqueness of the solution.

The time-one point `(α p 1).1` of this flow, read back through `φ⁻¹`, is a *geometric* object: by
`GeodesicGlobal.isGeodesicAt_of_chartODE` it is an intrinsic geodesic, and by uniqueness of
geodesics it agrees with `GExp hmet z v` wherever the latter's parameter is the fibre norm.  Hence:

> **`∃ρ > 0, ∀ z, ∀ (v : TangentSpace I z), `√(chartGram z z v v) < ρ →`
> `ContMDiffAt 𝘭(ℝ,E) I ∞ (fun w => GExp hmet z w) v`.**

This is the correct replacement for (US) and it is provable with the requested flow lemma plus
`ContDiff ℝ ∞`-pointwise-then-uniform reasoning on the fixed chart.  A finite cover of `M` by
compact pieces of atlas charts then globalises it.

### 38.5 CONSEQUENCE FOR THE PLAN

Everything downstream of the *pointwise* local comparison must be re-based on `GExp`:

1. `HingeLocal.edist_expMap_le_model` (pointwise, apex `= x₀`) stays valid and is used *pointwise*
   exactly as it is; the uniform statement must be re-proved with `GExp` (a `GExp`-version of the
   chain `ModelBound.mfderiv_expMap_model_bound_polar` ▸ `ModelPath.pointwise_bound` ▸ length
   integral).  The differential bound for `GExp` comes from the **variational equation of the
   `φ`-chart ODE flow** (which also gives Gauss's lemma in chart coordinates intrinsically) rather
   than from `mfderiv (expMap x₀)`.
2. `HingeKey2.gcompHolds_pi_of_uniform` and the Key Lemma are already stated with `GCompHolds` /
   `GExp`, so they are unaffected.
3. The `expMap`-based files of sessions 26–43 remain correct as *pointwise* statements; nothing has
   to be deleted, but no uniform statement may be phrased with `expMap`.

### 38.6 A MATHLIB GOTCHA (found by the fleet while refuting the first version of the flow lemma)

`ContDiffAt ℝ ∞ f x` in Mathlib means `∀ n : ℕ, ContDiffAt ℝ n f x`
(`contDiffAt_infty`) — the neighbourhood in which `f` is `C^n` may depend on `n`.  It is therefore
**strictly weaker** than "`f` is `C^∞` on some neighbourhood of `x`", and `ContDiffAt.contDiffOn`
cannot be used to produce `ContDiffOn ℝ ∞ _ U` (it needs `∞ = ω`, and `∞ ≠ ω` in `ℕ∞ω`).  Any
lemma about smooth dependence of a flow on its initial datum must therefore take its smoothness
hypothesis in the form `ContDiff ℝ ∞ F` (globally) or `ContDiffOn ℝ ∞ F U` (on a fixed open `U`) —
never `∀ p, ContDiffAt ℝ ∞ F p`.  The requested commons lemmas are stated accordingly.

### 38.7 THE EXACT RECIPE FOR THE UNIFORM LOCAL COMPARISON (SESSION 45 SHOULD DO THIS)

**Available after session 44** (all proved):
* `Commons.Analysis.ODE.uniform_flow_compact_global` and
  `Commons.Analysis.ODE.uniform_flow_compact_open` — for a field `F : E → E` that is `C^∞` on a
  fixed open `U`, a compact `K ⊆ U` admits `ρ > 0`, a flow `α : E → ℝ → E` with `α p 0 = p`,
  `(α p)' = F ∘ α p` on `|t| ≤ ρ`, smooth dependence `ContDiffAt ℝ ∞ (fun q => α q t) p`, and
  uniqueness of solutions on `|t| ≤ ρ`.
* `Submission.HingeGExp.edist_GExp_le_model` — the *pointwise* local hinge comparison for `GExp`.
* `GeodesicGlobal.contDiffOn_chartSpray'` — the chart spray is `C^∞` on the chart target.
* `GlobalBM.chartGram_continuousLinearMapAt` — `chartGram x z (clm z X) (clm z Y) = ⟪X,Y⟫`.
* `GlobalExp.eqOn_Ioo_of_isGeodesicAt_of_eq` — **uniqueness of intrinsic geodesics** with the same
  initial data.
* `GeodesicGlobal.isGeodesicAt_of_chartODE` — a chart-ODE solution is an intrinsic geodesic.

**Step 1 (chart-flow map).**  Fix `z₀`, a compact `Z ⊆ (chartAt H z₀).source`, and put
`Fφ (q : E × E) := (q.2, chartGeodesicVectorField cov z₀ q)` on the open set
`U := (extChartAt I z₀).target ×ˢ univ`.  Apply the flow lemma with
`K := ((extChartAt I z₀) '' Z) ×ˢ Metric.closedBall 0 1` (compact by
`ChartBounds.exists_chartGram_bounds z₀ Z`: the fibre-norm-1 ellipsoid at `z` lies in the model ball
of radius `λ^{-1/2}`).  Get `ρ_flow`, `α`.

**Step 2 (scaling).**  The chart geodesic equation is homogeneous in the velocity:
if `s ↦ (u s, w s)` solves the chart ODE with `w 0 = u₀`, then `s ↦ (u (c s), c • w (c s))` solves
it with initial velocity `c • u₀` (this is the `HasChartGeodesicOn.const_smul` of
`Submission/RauchGen.lean`; for the second-order form it follows from
`Commons.Analysis.ODE.secondOrderODE_solution_unique`).  Hence the chart ODE solution with initial
velocity `w₀` **at time `1`** equals the flow solution with initial velocity `ρ_flow⁻¹ • w₀`
**at time `ρ_flow`**:
`(chartSol w₀ 1) = (α (φ z, ρ_flow⁻¹ • w₀) ρ_flow).1`.

**Step 3 (identify with `GExp`).**  For `z ∈ Z` and `v : TangentSpace I z`, let `w₀` be the
`φ`-coordinates of `v`, i.e. `w₀ = clm_{z₀} z (frameVector z z v)`.  The time-1 point of the chart
ODE solution with initial velocity `w₀`, read through `φ⁻¹`, is an intrinsic geodesic through `z` of
initial velocity `v`; by Step 2 it is the value, at chart time `ρ_flow`, of the curve underlying
`α`.  Both the geodesic equation and its initial data are intrinsic, so by
`eqOn_Ioo_of_isGeodesicAt_of_eq` this curve coincides with the geodesic defining `GExp hmet z v`
(whose parameter is the *fibre* norm `√(chartGram z z v v)`, and the chart metric speed of a chart
geodesic is constant, `GeodesicGlobal2.chartGram_const_of_chartODE`).  Hence
`GExp hmet z v = (extChartAt I z₀).symm ((α (φ z, ρ_flow⁻¹ • w₀) ρ_flow).1)`.

**Step 4 (smoothness).**  Choose `ρ := min ρ_flow (√λ · ρ_flow)` (so that
`‖ρ_flow⁻¹ • w₀‖ ≤ 1` for `√(chartGram z z v v) < ρ`).  In Step 3 the right-hand side is smooth in
`w₀` at each `(φ z, ρ_flow⁻¹ • w₀) ∈ K` (flow lemma) and `v ↦ w₀ = clm_{z₀} z v` is linear, so
`v ↦ (extChartAt I z₀) (GExp hmet z v)` is `C^∞` at `v`.  Convert to
`ContMDiffAt 𝘭(ℝ,E) I ∞ (fun w => GExp hmet z w) v` by the chart-at-the-image transition
(`(extChartAt I p) ∘ (extChartAt I z₀).symm` is a local diffeomorphism at `φ (GExp hmet z v)`).

**Step 5 (globalise).**  Cover `M` by finitely many pieces `Z_i` of atlas charts (each `Z_i`
compact, `Z_i ⊆ (chartAt H z_i).source`) — e.g. from a finite subcover of the open cover
`{(chartAt H z).source : z : M}` of the compact `M`; take the minimum of the finitely many radii.

**Step 6 (uniform local comparison).**  Re-run route (M) (`ModelBound.mfderiv_expMap_model_bound_polar`
▸ `ModelPath.pointwise_bound` ▸ the length integral) with `GExp` in place of `expMap` and with the
uniform radius from Step 5; the differential bound for `GExp` is the variational equation of the
`φ`-chart flow (equivalently: Gauss's lemma plus Rauch I in the `φ`-chart coordinates, which is
what `Submission.ModelBound.lean`/`Submission.ModelPath.lean` already do *with* `expMap`).
Conclusion: `∃ρ > 0, ∀ z, GCompHolds hmet z ρ`, and then `HingeKey2.gcompHolds_pi_of_uniform`
gives the global Toponogov hinge comparison.


