### 39.2 `Submission/JacobiGauss.lean` (NEW, COMPLETE, sorry-free)

**The Gauss lemma in the Jacobi-field language**, *unbundled* (no `JData`, so it applies to the
chart-flow geodesics, whose chart speed is not normalised to `1`):

* `jacobi_gauss` : along a chart geodesic `(u,w)` with a Jacobi system solution `(Y,U)`
  (`Y 0 = 0`, `U 0 = 1`), for every `t ∈ Ioo A B`,
  `chartGram z₀ (φ⁻¹ (u t)) ((Y t) a) (w t) = t · chartGram z₀ q a (w 0)`.
  (Proof: the proof of `RauchGen.JData.jacobi_normal_gen` upgraded from the orthogonality case to
  the general affine form; the pairing `s ↦ ⟨(Y s)a, w s⟩` has constant derivative `⟨a, w 0⟩` and
  vanishes at `0`.)
* `jacobi_velocity` : `(Y t) (w 0) = t • w t` — **the radial Jacobi field is `t γ'(t)`**.
  (Proof: the two pairs `s ↦ ((Y s)(w 0), (U s)(w 0))` and `s ↦ (s • w s, w s)` both solve the
  first-order Jacobi system `jacobiSysVec` with the same initial value `(0, w 0)`; uniqueness of
  linear ODEs on `Icc (min 0 t) (max 0 t)` — `Commons.Analysis.ODE.eqOn_of_hasDerivAt_linearODE`
  with the explicit bound `max 0 (1 + 2 C₁ + C₂)` from continuity of `gammaOp`/`curvOp` — gives the
  identity.  The only new algebraic input is `chartCurv_self`.)
* `chartCurv_self` : `chartCurv cov x₀ p X X = 0` (immediate from the definition, by `abel`).
* **`chartFlow_gauss`** — the assembled form used by the model bound: for the variation family `Φ`
  of the chart flow (`(Φ (0,t)).1 = φ q`, `(Φ (0,t)).2 = u + t • y`), a Jacobi system solution
  `(Y,U)` along the sliced geodesic `(gc Φ, gv Φ)` and `p = φ⁻¹((gc Φ) 1)`,
  `chartGram x₀ p ((Y 1) u) ((Y 1) y) = chartGram x₀ q u y`
  — i.e. **`⟨(dF)_u u, (dF)_u y⟩` in the chart-flow coordinates is the metric at the base point**.

Consequences for the model bound (with `p := chartFlowExp D q u`, `D y := (Y 1) y`, `u` the chart
velocity of the geodesic through `q`):

* **radial**: `(Y 1) u = w 1` (`jacobi_velocity` at `t = 1`), and by constancy of the chart speed
  (`GeodesicGlobal2.chartGram_const_of_chartODE`) `⟪w 1, w 1⟫_p = ⟪u, u⟫_q`;
* **Gauss (the needed form)**: `⟪(Y 1) u, (Y 1) y⟫_p = ⟪u, y⟫_q`, i.e.
  `⟪(Y 1) u, D y⟫_p = ⟪u,y⟫_q`: indeed `(Y 1) u = w 1` and `jacobi_gauss` (with `a := y`) gives
