  `c < F s`, and `F` is *midpoint-closed above the threshold* (`a, b ∈ Icc 0 s`, `a < b`,
  `c < F a`, `c < F b` ⟹ `c < F ((a+b)/2)`), then `c ≤ F t` for all `t ∈ Icc 0 s`.
  *Proof*: the classical dyadic argument.  Induction on `n` shows that all the dyadic points
  `k s / 2^n` (`k ≤ 2^n`) are *strictly* above `c`: for even `k = 2j` this is the level-`n`
  statement at `j`, for odd `k = 2j+1` the point is the midpoint of
  `[j s/2^n, (j+1) s/2^n]` and the midpoint closure applies (the sub-segment has length
  `s/2^n ≤ s ≤ π`-irrelevant here).  Then every `t ∈ Icc 0 s` is the limit of
  `xₙ = ⌊t 2^n/s⌋ · s/2^n` (which lies in `Icc 0 s` and satisfies `|t - xₙ| ≤ s/2^n`), so
  `F (xₙ) → F t` and `ge_of_tendsto'` gives `c ≤ F t`.
* **`tendsto_arccos_model_sub_div`** : for `r ∈ (0, π)` the right derivative at `0` of
  `t ↦ arccos (cos t cos r + sin t sin r cos θ)` is `-cos θ`, i.e.
  `((arccos … - r)/t) → -cos θ` along `𝓝[>] 0`.  **`r < π` (not `r ≤ π`) is essential**: at
  `r = π` the function is `t ↦ π - t` and the limit is `-1 ≠ -cos θ`.
  Proved through `HasDerivAt.tendsto_slope_zero_right` applied to `arccos ∘ u` (`u` the cosine
  argument) and `√(1 - cos²r) = sin r > 0`.

