### 45.4 WHAT `no_crit_far_q` REALLY SAYS (and why the conclusion is about `p`)

The notes' step 2 case 2 reads "apply the same argument to `d(·,q)`".  The formalisation makes the
structure explicit:  criticality of `d(p,·)` is used *only* to produce a shortest geodesic from `x`
to `p` together with the inequality `⟪ξ, u₂⟫ ≥ 0` (`ξ` the unit direction to `q`); the concavity is
then applied to the function `d(q,·)` along that geodesic.  Consequently the theorem still
concludes `¬ IsCrit hmet p x`, which is what the assembly needs.  (By contrast, applying
`no_crit_far` with `p` and `q` interchanged only yields `¬ IsCrit hmet q x`.)

