### 47.1 THE ASCENT PREDICATE AND ITS ALGEBRA (§47.1)

* `IsAscentAt hmet p x v` — every shortest direction from `x` to `p` makes an obtuse angle with `v`
  (equivalently, by the first variation formula, `d(p, ·)` increases in the direction `v` at the
  rate `-min_u ⟪v,u⟫ > 0`).
* `isAscentAt_smul` (positive scaling), **`isAscentAt_add`** (convex combinations with positive
  total weight), **`isAscentAt_sum`** (finite sums `∑ φ i • V i` with `φ i ≥ 0` and `∑ φ i > 0`).
  This is *exactly* the algebra that makes the partition-of-unity gluing work: if `V_i` is a
  gradient-like section on the open set `U_i` and `{φ_i}` is a partition of unity subordinate to
  `{U_i}`, then `Σ φ_i V_i` is gradient-like wherever it is defined — the convexity of the
  condition `∀u ∈ S_x, ⟪v,u⟫ < 0` is immediate, and no continuity/openness of the individual
  conditions is needed.
* `exists_isAscentAt_of_not_isCrit`, `exists_isAscentAt_off_pq`: the bridge from the critical point
  theory (`GSNoCrit.exists_unit_away_of_ne`) — every point of `M \ {p,q}` carries an ascent
  direction (in the restricted range `diam ≤ 2π/3`).

### 47.2 THE CONTINUITY ENGINE

* `chartFlowExp_phase_continuousAt`, **`chartFlowExp_continuousAt_joint`**: the chart-flow
  exponential map `(q, u) ↦ chartFlowExp D q u` is continuous (jointly) wherever the phase
  `((extChartAt I z₀) q, flowScale D • u)` lies in `chartData z₀ Z`.  Proof: the phase map is
  smooth, the flow `D.α` is jointly smooth at `chartData` (`ChartFlowData.hjoint`), and
  `(extChartAt I z₀).symm` is continuous on its target.
* **`GExp_frameVector_continuousAt`**: consequently `(y, w) ↦ GExp y (frameVector z₀ y w)` is
  **continuous in the base point** `y` for small coordinates `w`.  This is the
  `GExp`-continuity-in-the-base-point statement that the library was missing (§38's obstruction was
  about `expMap` with *unbounded* radii; for the chart flow the reachable distances are governed by
  the flow radius `ρ`, which is why only *small* vectors are covered here — and why the argument
  below cuts the minimizing geodesics at a small fixed time `t`).

### 47.3 THE NO-CORNER LEMMA (THE MATHEMATICAL HEART)

**`eq_GExp_of_minimising_broken`**: if the broken path `x₀ → z → p` with `z = GExp x₀ (t • w)` is
minimising (`d(x₀,z) = t`, `d(z,p) = r - t`, `d(x₀,p) = r`, `0 < t < r`, `‖w‖ = 1`), then
`p = GExp x₀ (r • w)`; equivalently, the limit of shortest directions is again a shortest
direction.

*Proof.*  Let `G₀ := geodCurve … x₀ w` be the minimising ray of length `t` and `η` a shortest
direction at `z = G₀ t` towards `p` (Hopf–Rinow).  If the incoming direction `ξ := G₀'(t)` and `η`
were different, then `CornerCut.corner_cut` at `z` would give `d(G₀(t-τ), G₂ τ) < 2τ` for small
`τ`; together with the sub-arc minimising identities (`geodCurve_sub_arc_minimising'`) the triangle
inequality would give `d(x₀,p) < t - τ + 2τ + (r-t) - τ = r`, a contradiction.  Hence `ξ = η`, and
then `GExp_geodCurve_add` applied to the *full* ray `geodCurve … x₀ w` of length `r` (which agrees
with `G₀` near `t` by `geodCurve_agree`) gives `G r = GExp (G₀ t) ((r-t) • ξ) = GExp (G₀ t)
((r-t) • η) = p`, while `G r = GExp x₀ (r • w)`.

*Remark*: this is the "no corner" ingredient in the form needed — it does *not* require any
long-range continuity of `GExp`.

### 47.4 WHAT REMAINS FOR THE FLOW (exact recipe)

The single missing piece is the **sequential compactness assembly** turning §47.2 and §47.3 into

