### 49.1 WHAT IS STILL MISSING FOR GROVE–SHIOHAMA, AND WHY

The complete GS proof needs three things the project does not yet have:

1. **`diam > π/2` (Klingenberg).**  Both the full and the restricted (`diam ≤ 2π/3`) formalised
   versions of GS take `diam > π/2` as a hypothesis; the project can only get `diam ≥ π/2` from
   Myers + Rauch (`conj ≥ π/√K_max ≥ π/2`).  The strict inequality needs Klingenberg's injectivity
   estimate, in particular the *odd-dimensional* Morse-theoretic input.  **This is the deepest open
   gap and it blocks the whole GS route, not just step 3.**
2. **The continuous global gradient-like field of step 3(a).**  The pointwise algebra
   (`isAscentAt_finsum`) and the pointwise gluing (`exists_ascent_field_of_local`) are done; what a
   partition of unity adds is *continuity* of `Σ φᵢ Vᵢ`, needed for the flow.  Ingredients:
   (i) continuity of the section `y ↦ frameVector z₀ y v` (in this Mathlib there is **no**
   `Trivialization.continuous_symm`/`continuousAt_symmL` — the continuity has to be extracted from
   the `VectorBundle`/`FiberBundle` instances or from the chart-flow `hjoint`, see `GExpFlow`);
   (ii) a partition of unity on the open set `M \ {p,q}` (the commons request
   `Commons.Topology.PartitionOfUnity.exists_isSubordinate_of_isOpen` isolates exactly this, since
   Mathlib's API only covers closed sets).
3. **The flow and the level-set homeomorphism (step 3(b))** — `f(φ_t x) = f x + t` (or strict
   monotonicity with a hitting time) plus `Commons.Topology.Metric.homeo_level_times_interval`, and
   then the two-disk assembly (`Commons.Topology.Sphere.two_disk_cover_strong`).

### 49.2 THE KLINGENBERG CHAPTER — THE PLAN (for whoever picks it up)

Everything funnels through `inj ≥ π/√K_max`.  After rescaling by `1/κ` (`κ = inf sec > 1`) the
metric has `sec ∈ [1, 4/κ)` with `4/κ < 4` strictly, so it suffices to prove:

> a closed simply connected Riemannian manifold with `sec ∈ [1, 4)` has `inj > π/2`.

The standard structure (Cheeger–Ebin Ch. 5, Petersen §6.5, Shankar–Sormani arXiv:0709.2331):

* `inj(p) = min(conj(p), ½ · (shortest geodesic loop at p))`;
* a nearest cut point is either conjugate to `p` along a minimising geodesic, or the meeting point
  of exactly two minimising geodesics, giving a smooth geodesic loop of length `d(p,q)` at `p`;
* the conjugate radius half: **already available** (`ConjRadius.pi_half_le_of_isConjugateTime` for
  the `K_max ≤ 4` bound, `RauchGlobal.eqOn_zero_of_jacobi` for the local form);
* "a minimising geodesic has no conjugate point in its interior" — this is the missing step and it
  needs the **index form of a broken field** (`IndexLemma.indexIntegral_nonpos_of_minimizing` is
  the smooth chart-local version); the classical proof replaces a Jacobi field with conjugate ends
  by a field with a corner, whose index form is strictly negative;
* "no null-homotopic closed geodesic of length `< π`" — even dimensions: a periodic parallel unit
  normal field exists (the holonomy of a null-homotopic loop on the `(n-1)`-dimensional normal
  space lies in `SO(n-1)`, `n-1` odd, so it has a fixed vector:
  `Commons.LinearAlgebra.InnerProductSpace.exists_fixed_vector_of_odd_finrank_det_one`), and the
  second variation along it is `< 0`; odd dimensions: Klingenberg 1961 (free loop space Morse
  theory / the long homotopy lemma, `Shankar–Sormani` §6, §9–10) — **not formalisable at present**.

**Practical conclusion**: the target `Submission.sphere_theorem` cannot be completed by the
comparison-geometry route without the odd-dimensional Klingenberg input.  The measurable output of
this project is therefore the library: curvature/pinching, geodesics, the chart metric, Gauss,
Hopf–Rinow, Myers, the Jacobi tensor and shape operator, Rauch I and II, the index lemma, the
second variation, the jet-gap lemma, **Toponogov's hinge comparison (hinge size `< π`)**, the
`π/2`-ball lemma, Berger's concavity, the Grove–Shiohama critical point theory (steps 1 and 2), the
metric-ball-is-a-disk theorem, and the ascent-field algebra with its local persistence theorem.

