  (`Commons.Topology.Metric.homeo_level_times_interval`, which however needs the *exact*
  normalisation `f ∘ φ_t = f + t`, hence §55.1).


## 56. STEP 3(b): THE ASSEMBLY REDUCES TO STRICT MONOTONICITY (NOT TO A SHARP NORMALISATION)

### 56.1 THE REPARAMETRISATION TRICK

`Commons.Topology.Metric.homeo_level_times_interval` needs the flow to satisfy `f ∘ φ_t = f + t`
*exactly*, which naively forces the field to be normalised by `df(X) = 1` — and by §55.1 that means
normalising by `-sup_u ⟪X,u⟫`, whose continuity in `x` is exactly the hard cut-locus behaviour.
**This can be avoided**: from *any* continuous flow `φ` with `t ↦ f (φ_t x)` strictly increasing, one
gets a normalised flow `ψ x t := φ x (T (x, t))`, where `T (x, t)` is the unique time at which
`f = f x + t`, and:

* `ψ` is a continuous flow with `ψ x 0 = x` and `ψ (ψ x s) t = ψ x (s + t)` (both sides reach level
  `f x + s + t`, so they agree by *uniqueness* of the time — this uses only strict monotonicity, not
  any formula for the rate);
* `f (ψ x t) = f x + t` *by construction*;
* `T` is continuous by the new commons lemma **`Commons.Topology.continuous_root_of_strictMono`**
  (continuity of the root of a strictly monotone continuous family; uniqueness of roots + boundedness
  of roots, both from `c x n → c x`).

Hence the level-set homeomorphism needs only **strict monotonicity of `f` along the flow**, never a
sharp formula for `df(X)`.

### 56.2 THE TWO NEW COMMONS REQUESTS OF THIS SESSION (step 3(b))

* **`Commons.Geometry.Manifold.exists_isMIntegralCurveOn_nhds`** (in flight): the local flow box on
  a manifold — a uniform time `ε > 0`, an open `U ∋ x₀` and a jointly continuous flow on
  `U × (-ε, ε)` — the ODE input for the global flow of the smooth ascent field of §54;
* **`Commons.Topology.homeo_level_times_of_normalised_flow`** (in flight): the level-set
  homeomorphism `{a ≤ f ≤ b} ≅ {f = a} × [0, b - a]` for a continuous flow with
  `f (φ x t) = f x + t` *on the relevant range only* (the form in which the flow of a compactly
  supported field can actually provide it), with the explicit homeomorphism
  `x ↦ (φ x (a - f x), f x - a)` and inverse `(y,s) ↦ φ y s`.

Together with the reparametrisation of §56.1 these give step 3(b) from

* a *smooth* ascent field on the compact set `t` (§54: **done**),
* the **strict monotonicity** of `f = d(p,·)` along its flow — which is the single remaining
  analytic input, and by §55.1 it is the lower half of the first variation formula
  (`ρ'(0⁺) ≥ -sup_u ⟪X,u⟫ > 0`, the latter strictness requiring the compactness of the set of
  shortest directions).

### 56.3 GOTCHAS ADDED

1. `Commons.Topology.continuous_root_of_strictMono` is stated for `g : X × ℝ → ℝ`; to apply it to
   the flow one uses it at `X × ℝ` with `g ((x, s), t) := f (φ x t)` and `c (x, s) := f x + s`.
2. `homeo_level_times_interval` (the older commons lemma) cannot be applied to a compact space: an
   *exact* normalisation `f ∘ φ_t = f + t` for all `t ∈ ℝ` forces `f` to be unbounded above.  This
   is why the "on the relevant range only" version of §56.2 is the right interface.



## 57. SESSION: THE STRATEGIC REDUCTION -- NO KLINGENBERG NEEDED *IF* THE METRIC CAN BE RESCALED

This section records the outcome of a careful re-analysis of the classical route, done at the
start of this session.  It is important because it changes the shape of the remaining work.

### 57.1 THE INVARIANT QUANTITY

Let `kappa := inf sec` (> 1 for `QuarterPinched cov`) and `K := sup sec` (<= 4), `D := diam M`.
Myers (sharp form) gives `D * sqrt kappa <= pi`, and for the *sphere* of curvature `k` one has
`D * sqrt k = pi`.  Every rescaling `g -> s * g` (s > 0) sends `sec -> sec / s`, `D -> sqrt s * D`,
so the quantity

  `tau := D * sqrt kappa`

is *scale invariant*.  Grove--Shiohama's theorem (sec >= 1, diam > pi/2) is, in invariant form,

  `sec >= k` and `diam > pi / (2 sqrt k)`  ==>  `M ~= S^n`,

i.e. it needs exactly `tau > pi / 2`.  **No rescaling whatsoever can change `tau`**: so the
hypothesis `diam` of any GS-type argument can always be met exactly when `tau > pi/2`.  This is a
clean (and, for the formalisation, decisive) simplification: the whole case analysis of sessions
41--53 ("route 1 with diam <= 2pi/3", "route 2 for the large diameter") is *subsumed* by:

* if `tau > pi/2`: apply GS (possibly after rescaling, see 56.3) and conclude;
* if `tau <= pi/2`: **nothing in the GS family applies**.

So the real dichotomy of the whole project is `tau > pi/2` versus `tau <= pi/2` -- not the diameter
range, and not the parity of the dimension at the level of the statement.

### 57.2 KLINGENBERG IN THIS LANGUAGE, AND THE STRICT PINCHING

Klingenberg's estimate, in the weak form needed here, is `D >= inj(M) >= pi / sqrt K` for `M`
compact, simply connected (the conjugate-radius half is `ConjRadius`, `RauchGlobal`; the missing
half is "no null-homotopic closed geodesic of length < 2 pi / sqrt K").  Since `K < 4 kappa`
*strictly* (that is exactly `QuarterPinched` with `kappa > 1`, `K <= 4`), Klingenberg would give

  `tau = D sqrt kappa >= pi sqrt (kappa / K) > pi / 2`,

i.e. **strict pinching + Klingenberg = GS applies**.  Conversely, without the closed-geodesic
estimate one only has `tau = D sqrt kappa >= pi sqrt(kappa/K)` *if* one has Klingenberg; the raw
tools available today (Rauch: `conj >= pi/sqrt K`; Myers: `D <= pi/sqrt kappa`) give nothing about
`tau` at all.  Hence:

> **The target theorem is equivalent to: for every `QuarterPinched cov`, either `tau > pi/2`
> (then GS finishes) or `tau <= pi/2` (a genuinely different, "small diameter" regime).**

The second case is *not* excluded by anything currently in the library, and in the classical
literature it is disposed of by Klingenberg's injectivity radius theorem, whose odd-dimensional
case (Klingenberg 1961) rests on Morse theory on the free loop space (see Roadmap 49.2, and
Berger 1960's introduction: "La restriction sur la dimension provient uniquement de ce que la
demonstration utilise essentiellement un theoreme de Klingenberg ... qui n'est valable qu'en
dimension paire").  **This remains the project's deepest open input.**

### 57.3 RESCALING THE METRIC IN LEAN: THE CONNECTION DOES NOT CHANGE

Suppose one nevertheless wants to apply a GS-type theorem with a *different* normalisation (for
instance, the formalised restricted GS "sec in (1,4] and diam in (pi/2, 2pi/3]" of
`GSCritical`/`GSConcave`/`GSConcaveSeg`/`GSNoCrit`, or a "sec >= 1" form).  The obstacle is that
the target's hypotheses speak about the *given* Riemannian bundle, and rescaling means changing
the inner product on every fibre.

Two observations make this feasible:

1. **The Levi-Civita connection of `s * g` is the same as that of `g`** (the Christoffel symbols
   `Gamma = (1/2) g^{-1} (..)` are invariant under a *constant* rescaling of the metric, since
   `g^{-1}` scales by `1/s` and the derivatives of the components scale by `s`).  Hence the
   *same* covariant derivative `cov` is the Levi-Civita connection of the rescaled metric: no
   construction of a Levi-Civita connection is needed (and Mathlib does not have one anyway).
2. In Mathlib, a Riemannian structure on a fibre bundle is *data* (`Bundle.RiemannianBundle` has a
   single field `g : RiemannianMetric E`), and the fibre norms are derived from it by a **scoped**
   instance (`Bundle.RiemannianBundle.instNormedAddCommGroup`, priority 80, `fast_instance%%`).
   A scaled instance `Bundle.RiemannianBundle E := <c * inner>` is therefore constructible by
   hand (`inner b v w = c * inner b v w`, with `pos`, `continuousAt`, `isVonNBounded` by
   scaling), and it can be installed *locally* with `letI` when a theorem is applied.  The
   hypotheses to transfer are:
   * `IsMetricCompatible cov` for the scaled metric (immediate: multiply by `c`),
   * the pinching: `sec` w.r.t. `s * g` in `(1,4]` is exactly `sec` w.r.t. `g` in `(s, 4s]`,
   * `IsContMDiffRiemannianBundle` for the scaled metric (scale-invariant),
   * the *diameter* hypotheses: `riemannianEDist` w.r.t. `s * g` is `sqrt s` times the original
     one (the path length integral scales because `enorm` scales with the fibre norm).

   Hmm -- the last transfer needs a genuine lemma about `Manifold.riemannianEDist` and the
   `enorm` of the scaled fibre norm; it has *not* been proved yet.

### 57.4 GS STEP 3(b): THE EXACT REMAINING INPUT IS STRICT MONOTONICITY ALONG THE FLOW

The two commons lemmas needed for step 3(b) have now landed:
`Commons.Geometry.Manifold.exists_isMIntegralCurveOn_nhds` (the local flow box for a `C^infinity`
vector field on a manifold: uniform time `eps`, open `U`, jointly continuous flow) and
`Commons.Topology.homeo_level_times_of_normalised_flow` (the level-set homeomorphism
`{a <= f <= b} ~= {f = a} x [0, b-a]` for a flow with `f (phi x t) = f x + t` on the relevant
range), together with `Commons.Topology.continuous_root_of_strictMono` (the reparametrisation
`psi x t := phi x (T (x,t))` of any flow whose `f` is strictly increasing).  Hence, exactly as
recorded in 56.1, **the only missing analytic input of step 3(b) is:**

  `(SM)`: for the continuous ascent field `V` of `GSGlobal2.exists_continuous_ascent_field_of_diam`
  and its flow `phi`, the function `t |-> d(p, phi_t x)` is *strictly increasing*.

Two ways to prove `(SM)`:

* **(via the flow curve)** the right Dini derivative of `g := d(p, .)` along the flow is
  `- sup_u <V(x), u>` over the unit shortest directions `u` from `x` to `p`
  (`df_p(v) = -sup_u <v,u>`, cf. Roadmap 55.1/55.2 for the sign discussion).  `IsAscentAt`
  says every one of those `<V,u>` is `< 0`, but the *sup* (or rather the inf of `-<V,u>`) must
  be *attained* -- this is the **compactness of the set of shortest directions**, which is
  available (it is `{u : ||u|| = 1} inter exp_p (d(p,x) . )^{-1} (p)`, closed by continuity of
  `exp`/`GExp` and compact), and then followed by `c := inf_u (-<V,u>) > 0`.
* **(via the exponential ray)** the *upper* first variation formula
  (`FirstVariation.firstVariation_upper`, already available) gives, for the minimising geodesic
  from `p` to `x` with unit tangent `w` at `x` (so that `u := -w` is a shortest direction from
  `x` to `p`) and the ascent condition `<V,u> < 0`:

    `d(p, expMap x (-t V)) <= f(x) + t (<V,u> + eps) < f(x)`  for small `t > 0`,

  i.e. moving in the direction `-V` strictly *decreases* `d(p,.)`.  Hmm -- this gives the
  monotonicity of `g` along the *flow* only after transferring from the exponential ray to the
  flow, which is a second-order `O(t^2)` estimate `d(phi_t x, exp_x (t V)) <= C t^2` (the flow
  curve and the exponential ray have the same position and velocity at `t = 0`; see
  `Submission/JetGap.lean` for the third-order analogue and `GapEstimate.riemannianEDist_chart_le`
  for the chart-to-distance conversion).

The second route is the one that fits the existing library best; it needs the `O(t^2)` estimate as
a lemma.  **This is what the next session should build** (and then the flow/level-set/two-disk
assembly, whose ingredients are all in the commons).

### 57.5 THE FULL GS AND THE HESSIAN BOUND

To remove the restriction `diam <= 2 pi / 3` (session 53's `GSCritical`/`GSConcaveSeg` chain) and
hence to run GS for *all* `diam > pi/2` -- which by 56.1 is exactly what `tau > pi/2` needs -- one
must replace the "size <= pi hinge" concavity argument by the classical one:

  `f = md_1 o d_p` (where `md_1 (t) = (1 - cos t)/2`) satisfies `f'' + f <= 1` along a geodesic,
  hence the Jensen comparison `f (gamma t) >= fbar (gamma t)`, hence `d_p` is concave on the set
  `{d_p >= pi/2}` (the reference notes arXiv:2404.09792, section 5.2.3, "point-on-a-side comparison
  3.4.4 + Jensen 3.4.12").

The differential inequality `f'' + f <= 1` is *pointwise* (no hinge-size restriction) and is
exactly the **Hessian comparison** `Hess d_p <= cot (d_p) (g - d d_p (x) d d_p)` for `sec >= 1`, whose
proof is the classical Rauch/second-variation argument:

  `Hess d_p (X,X) = <V'(r), V(r)>` for the Jacobi field `V` along the (minimising) geodesic from `p`
  to `z` with `V 0 = 0`, `V r = X`; the Riccati comparison `u(t) := <V'(t),V(t)>/|V(t)|^2`,
  `u' <= -u^2 - 1`, `t u(t) -> 1` gives `u <= cot`, whence `Hess d_p (X,X) <= cot r |X|^2`.

Its two prerequisites are missing in the library:

1. **a minimising geodesic has no conjugate point in its interior.**  The classical proof uses
   the index form of a *broken* field (`IndexLemma.indexIntegral_nonpos_of_minimizing` is the
   smooth version only): a nonzero Jacobi field `V` along the minimising geodesic `gamma|[0,r]`
   with `V 0 = V t0 = 0`, `t0 < r`, extended by `0` on `[t0,r]`, is a broken field whose index
   form is `0`; being a "harmonic" broken field for the minimising geodesic (index form >= 0 for
   all broken fields) forces its one-sided derivatives to agree at `t0`, hence `V' t0 = 0`, hence
   `V ≡ 0` -- contradiction.
2. **the identification `Hess d_p (X,X) = <V'(r), V(r)>`** (the second variation formula in the
   `exp`/Jacobi language), plus the Riccati comparison (available in essence:
   `RauchUpper.hasDerivAt_riccatiW` and `Commons.Analysis.ODE.*`).

This is the single biggest *formalizable* gap left in the GS route (as opposed to Klingenberg,
which is the biggest gap overall).

### 57.6 NEW GOTCHAS

1. **A constant rescaling of a Riemannian metric does not change the Levi-Civita connection.**
   This is the observation that makes "rescaling the metric" a *purely metric* operation in Lean:
   `cov` stays fixed, only the `RiemannianBundle` instance (and hence every `inner`/`||.||` in the
   statements) changes.  Do not attempt to construct a new covariant derivative.
2. The fibre norms of `TangentSpace I x` come from a **scoped, noncomputable, priority-80
   `fast_instance%%`** instance.  A scaled `RiemannianBundle` instance is therefore usable with
   `letI`, but one must expect the usual instance-diamond hygiene (write the scaled metric
   explicitly, and never mix the two instances in one statement).
3. The set of shortest directions from `x` to `p` is `{u : ||u|| = 1, exp_x (d(p,x) * u) = p}`; it
   is compact (closed in the unit sphere), so `sup`/`inf` over it are attained -- this is the
   missing compactness input of the first variation formula in the form `-sup_u <v,u>`, and it is
   *not* the same as the "sup over all shortest geodesics" of the classical statements (which is
   why the classical "`-inf`" formulation in the literature must be read as `-sup` of `-<v,u>`,
   see Roadmap 55.1).


