### 62.3 THE ASSEMBLY, AND THE ONE REMAINING GEOMETRIC GAP (CORRECTED ANALYSIS)

Recall the classical assembly (arXiv:2404.09792 §5.2.3): with `f = d(p,·)`, `g = d(q,·)` and `ε > 0`
small, the field's trajectories cross `{f = ε}` and `{g = ε}` exactly once, giving
`W := M \ (B_ε(p) ∪ B_ε(q)) = {f ≥ ε} ∩ {g ≥ ε} ≅ {f = ε} × [0,1]`; then
`M = B_ε(p) ∪ W ∪ B_ε(q)` is a "twisted sphere", i.e. `two_disk_cover_strong` applies.

Two facts are needed for the collar `W ≅ {f = ε} × [0,1]`, i.e. for the abstract two-function
machinery (two barriers/escapes/crossing times, then `τ`-continuity via
`Filter.tendsto_of_subseq_tendsto`, or a closed-graph argument with `isClosedMap_fst_of_compactSpace`):

* (H1) the chosen field `V` is an *ascent* field for `f` on `W` — available
  (`GSField.exists_isAscentAt_off_pq`, partition of unity as in `GSGlobal2`);
* (H2) the same `V` is a *descent* field for `g` on `W`, i.e.
  `⟪V,u'⟫ > 0` for every shortest direction `u'` from `x` to `q`.

(H1)+(H2) together say: `∃V, (∀u ∈ Dir(x→p), ⟪V,u⟫ < 0) ∧ (∀u' ∈ Dir(x→q), ⟪V,u'⟫ > 0)`, equivalently
`0 ∉ conv(Dir(x→p) ∪ (-Dir(x→q)))`.  Two remarks:

* a **sufficient** condition is the classical "all pairs obtuse", `⟪u,u'⟫ < 0` for all
  `u ∈ Dir(x→p)`, `u' ∈ Dir(x→q)`: if `0 = Σλa + Σμb` with `a ∈ Dir(x→p)`, `b ∈ -Dir(x→q)`, then
  `‖Σλa‖² = ΣΣλμ⟪a,-b⟫` is a sum of *strictly negative* multiples, forcing either
  `0 ∈ conv Dir(x→p)` or `0 ∈ conv Dir(x→q)` — both excluded by `¬IsCrit p x = GSNoCrit.not_isCrit_of_ne`
  and its mirror image `¬IsCrit q x`.
* the concavity argument of `GSNoCrit.no_crit_far` gives, for `x` with `f x > π/2`, only
  `min_{u} ⟪u',u⟫ < 0` (a *minimum*), i.e. *some* `u` obtuse to `u'` — **not** the "all pairs"
  statement.  The three cases of the classical Step 2 are: (i) `d(p,x) > π/2`: `far_of_far_endpoints`
  + `concaveOn_dist_geodCurve` along a shortest geodesic from `x` to `q` (all tools available), (ii)
  `d(q,x) > π/2`: the mirror image, (iii) both distances `≤ π/2`: then the *hinge comparison*
  (Toponogov, formalised) says `D ≤` the model distance of a hinge with sides `f x, g x ≤ π/2` and
  angle `≤ π/2`, which is `≤ π/2` — contradicting `D > π/2`.  So the *joint* condition
  `0 ∉ conv(Dir(x→p) ∪ -Dir(x→q))` (weaker than "all pairs obtuse") looks provable by exactly this
  case analysis; that would unlock the collar without any new comparison theory.

