### 62.6 A SECOND ROUTE TO THE CAP (worth trying before the two-function collar)

Instead of the collar `W ≅ {f = ε} × [0,1]`, one can split `M = {f ≤ D-ε} ∪ {f ≥ D-ε}` and use
`two_disk_cover_strong` with `A := {f ≤ D-ε}` (the **half-disk theorem**: `A ≅ D^n`, proved by
gluing the small-ball chart `{f ≤ t}` with the collar `{t ≤ f ≤ D-ε}` — see §62.4(1)) and
`B := {f ≥ D-ε}` (the **cap**).  Here `A ∩ B = {f = D-ε} = ∂A`, and one checks `∂B = {f = D-ε}`
as well, so the two-disk cover applies once `Cap ≅ D^n` is known.

For the cap there is an alternative to the two-function collar, using only available machinery:

* `Cap := {f ≥ D-ε}` is contained in a small ball `B(q,α)` and contains `B(q,δ)`
  (uniqueness of the maximum: `GSCritical.step1_diameter_unique`, plus compactness — this is a
  pure topology lemma still to be formalised: for a continuous `f` on a compact space with a unique
  maximiser `q`, every neighbourhood of `q` contains `{f ≥ sup f - δ}` for some `δ > 0`);
* for `x ∈ Cap`, `x ≠ q`: every *shortest* geodesic `γ` from `x` to `q` has both endpoints at
  distance `> π/2` from `p`, hence is entirely outside `B_{π/2}(p)`
  (`GSConcaveSeg.far_of_far_endpoints`), so `t ↦ f(γ(t))` is concave there
  (`GSConcaveSeg.concaveOn_dist_geodCurve`) and satisfies the chord bound
  `f(γ(τ)) ≥ f(x) + (τ/L)(D - f(x))` — **strictly increasing**, so the whole segment `[x,q]` stays
  in `Cap` and `f` strictly decreases along the ray from `q` through `x`;
* consequently, in the chart `Φ := GExp q : closedBall (0,α) ≅ {g ≤ α}`
  (`GSDisk.exists_pos_homeo_closedBall`), the set `Φ⁻¹(Cap)` is *star-shaped* with respect to `0`
  with a radial function `ρ(u) = sup{t : Φ(tu) ∈ Cap}`, which is continuous (closed set + strict
  monotonicity + compactness, the same argument as the `τ`-continuity of §62.3).  Then
  `Cap ≅ {tu : ‖tu‖ ≤ ρ(u)} ≅ closedBall (0,1)` via the radial normalisation `tu ↦ (t/ρ(u))·u`;
* the same argument gives `{f ≤ b} ≅ D^n` for *every* `b < D` (taking `A := {f ≤ b}` in the two-disk
  cover), i.e. the "half-disk" statement of §62.4(1) can also be obtained this way with `q ↦ p`.

The strict monotonicity above is exactly the kind of statement proved inside `GSNoCrit.no_crit_far`
(the case `d(p,x) > π/2`), so this route reuses that file's structure; it avoids the *joint
ascent/descent* condition of §62.3 entirely.

