By Myers (with `kappa := min sec > 1`, attained since `sec` is continuous on the compact `M×SM`),
the diameter satisfies `D ≤ pi / sqrt kappa ≤ pi`.

So the target splits into exactly two cases:

* **`D = diam > π/2`**: GS applies (in its invariant form `D · sqrt kappa > π/2` this is automatic
  since `kappa ≥ 1`).  The formalised chain needs `D ≤ 2π/3`, which is *done* (§63); the remaining
  sub-case `2π/3 < D ≤ π` needs the **Hessian-bound concavity** of §57.5.
* **`D ≤ π/2`**: needs **Klingenberg's diameter estimate** `D ≥ inj(M) ≥ π/√K` with `K := max sec`;
  since `K < 4 kappa` (strict pinching), this would give `D sqrt kappa ≥ π sqrt(kappa/K) > π/2`,
  contradicting `D ≤ π/2`.  This is the free-loop-space input (Klingenberg 1961) and is the
  project's deepest gap.

### 64.2 THE HESSIAN CONCAVITY IS REALLY NECESSARY (a refuted shortcut)

The bottleneck of the whole restricted chain is the hypothesis

  `hTD : T / 2 + D ≤ π`

of `GSConcaveSeg.concaveOn_dist_geodCurve` (with `T` the length of the minimising geodesic and `D`
an upper bound for `f = d(p,·)` along it).  In every application `T ≤ diam = D`, so it reads
`3D/2 ≤ π`, i.e. `D ≤ 2π/3`.  **The following shortcuts do NOT work** (verified on paper):

1. *Use the triangle inequality `T ≤ D − f(x)`.*  The correct triangle inequality is
   `D ≤ f(x) + T`, i.e. only the **lower** bound `T ≥ D − f(x)`.
2. *Use the perimeter bound* (`sec ≥ 1` ⟹ every triangle has perimeter `≤ 2π`).  Applied to the
   triangle `(p, q, x)` (with `d(p,q) = D`, `d(q,x) = T`, `d(p,x) = f(x) ≥ D − ε`) it gives
   `T ≤ 2(π − D) + ε`, whereas the concavity needs `T ≤ 2(π − D)`: **off by `ε`**, and in the round
   sphere the perimeter bound is *attained* for exactly the configurations where the concavity is
   nevertheless true.  So the perimeter bound cannot replace the Hessian bound.
3. *Pointwise version of the midpoint argument.*  Keeping track of the actual midpoint `u` gives the
   pointwise condition `min(u, T−u) + F(u) ≤ π` for `u ∈ [0,T]`; at `u = T/2` this is
   `T/2 + F(T/2) ≤ π`, and the perimeter bound only yields `T/2 + F(T/2) ≤ 2π − D − T/2`, again
   insufficient when `D < π`.

**Conclusion**: the concavity of `f = d(p,·)` along a *far* minimising geodesic for the whole range
`π/2 < f < π` must come from the differential inequality (Riccati/Hessian), as in the source
(arXiv:2404.09792 CH11: `Hess_{d(·,p̃)} = diag(0, cot(f̃) I)` in the model, then transfer by the
point-on-a-side comparison).  The three missing ingredients are exactly those of §57.5:
(i) a minimising geodesic has no conjugate point in its interior (index form of a **broken** field;
`IndexLemma.indexIntegral_nonpos_of_minimizing` is the *smooth* chart case only),
(ii) `Hess d_p(X,X) = ⟨V'(r), V(r)⟩` for the Jacobi field `V` with `V(0) = 0`, `V(r) = X^⊥`
(second variation of the distance function),
(iii) the Riccati comparison (`RauchUpper.hasDerivAt_riccatiW`, `Commons.Analysis.ODE.*`).
Once (i)–(iii) are available, `concaveOn_dist_geodCurve` can be reproved **without** `hTD`
(and `GSCritical.step1_diameter_unique` can be reproved without `D ≤ 2π/3`: the classical argument
uses the concavity of `f` outside `B_{π/2}(p)`, plus the perimeter bound only in the case
`D = π`).

### 64.3 RESCALING THE METRIC: WHAT IT WOULD AND WOULD NOT GIVE

Should someone still want to exploit rescaling (§57.3), here is the precise arithmetic.  Applying
the restricted GS theorem to `g' = c · g` requires `QuarterPinched cov'` (i.e. `1 < sec/c ≤ 4`,
i.e. `c < inf sec` and `c ≥ sup sec / 4`) together with diameter `D' = sqrt c · D ∈ (π/2, 2π/3]`.
With `kappa = inf sec`, `K = sup sec`, `tau = D sqrt kappa`, the interval
`(max(K/4, π²/(4D²)), min(kappa, 4π²/(9D²)))` for `c` is non-empty exactly when

  `tau > π/2`  **and**  `D sqrt K < 4π/3`  (and `K < 4 kappa`, which is the strict pinching).

For the *target's* normalisation (`kappa ≥ 1`, `K ≤ 4`) this extends the handled range from
`D ≤ 2π/3` to `D < 4π/(3 sqrt K) ≥ 2π/3`, i.e. it is a genuine extension whenever `K < 4` strictly
(e.g. `K = 1.5` gives `D < 3.99`, more than `π`), but it can never reach the cases
`D √K ≥ 4π/3` or `tau ≤ π/2`.  Implementing it requires building the rescaled
`RiemannianBundle` (`g' = c • g`, i.e. all five fields of `Mathlib`'s `RiemannianMetric`:
`inner`, `symm`, `pos`, `continuousAt`, `isVonNBounded`), transferring `IsMetricCompatible`
(the Christoffel symbols are unchanged), `QuarterPinched` (`sec' = sec/c`) and the distance
(`riemannianEDist' = sqrt c · riemannianEDist`, via `pathELength' = sqrt c · pathELength` and the
infimum).  Estimated size: a few hundred lines of instance work.  **Priority: lower than 64.2**,
since it does not touch the two hard cases.

### 64.4 ASSEMBLY ROBUSTNESS

`restricted_sphere_theorem` currently uses the *cap* route (§62.6), i.e. the split
`M = {f ≤ D-ε} ∪ {f ≥ D-ε}` with `f = d(p,·)`.  The classical route instead splits
`M = B_ε(p) ∪ W ∪ B_ε(q)` with the two-function collar `W ≅ {f = ε} × [0,1]` (§62.3).  Either way,
*only* the comparison-theory input (64.2) is missing; the topological assembly is complete and can be
reused verbatim.


