### 65.2 THE MATHEMATICAL IDEA (this is the important part)

**THE `T/2 + D ≤ π` RESTRICTION IS NOT NEEDED.**  The previous chain obtained the concavity of
`t ↦ d(p, γ t)` from the *hinge comparison at the midpoint* `m`: if `x = γ(m-a)`, `y = γ(m+a)` then
`d(x,p) + d(y,p) ≤ 2 d(m,p)`, which needs `a + d(m,p) ≤ π`.  The classical remedy is the
Hessian/Riccati bound (`Hess d_p + ...`), which is a large piece of work (§57.5, §64.2).

**The cheap remedy**: the hinge comparison is needed only for *small* half-widths `a`, where
`a + d(m,p) ≤ π` is automatic as soon as `d(m,p) < π` — i.e. as soon as `D < π`.  Concavity of a
continuous function on an interval is a **local** property (a function which is concave on a
neighbourhood of every point is concave), so the local midpoint inequality upgrades to global
concavity.  Concretely, the doubling argument:

  if the midpoint inequality holds for all pairs of width `u`, then it holds for all pairs of
  width `2u` (apply it at the three midpoints `m - u/2`, `m`, `m + u/2` of the configuration
  `s, m-u/2, m, m+u/2, t` and add),

iterated, plus the Archimedean property, gives the midpoint inequality for *all* widths from the
one for small widths.  No Hessian bound, no Riccati comparison, no index theory!

Two further ingredients of the unrestricted chain:

* `GSConcaveSeg.far_of_far_endpoints` (the "farness propagation" `f ≥ π/2` along a minimising
  segment with far endpoints) was **already** free of the `2π/3` restriction -- it only needs the
  length `≤ π` and `D ≤ π`.
* Step 1 (uniqueness of the maximum) is redone *without* the two-sided hinge at the midpoint of
  `[q₁,q₂]` (which needed `ℓ/2 + u ≤ π`).  Instead: the unrestricted concavity forces `f ≡ D` on
  the minimising geodesic `[q₁,q₂]`; then the **two hinge comparisons at an interior point** `z`
  of that geodesic, with **small opposite rays** (`a ≤ (π-D)/2`), give `cos θ < 0` and
  `cos(π-θ) < 0`, i.e. `cos θ < 0` and `cos θ > 0` -- a contradiction.  Here `θ` is the hinge
  angle between the ray and a shortest direction from `z` to `p`.  This is
  `Submission.GSStep1.not_both_eq_of_hinge_pair` + `step1_diameter_unique_of_lt_pi`.

