  `x`) and `InjAllAtLeast hmet r`, one has `2 r ≤ T` — i.e. `inj(x) ≤ ½·(length of the loop)`.

**The trick that makes the loop lemma work in the library's "indexed-`TangentSpace`" world.**  The
two halves of a loop are two geodesics from `x` to the midpoint, and one wants to feed their two
initial velocities to `InjAtLeast hmet x r`; but the library's tangent vectors are *indexed* by
their base point, so `curveVelocity G 0 : T_{G 0}M` and `-curveVelocity G T : T_{G T}M` are not
syntactically in the same space (`G 0`, `G T` are only *propositionally* equal to `x`), and the
`Norm`/`SMul` instances at the two points do not unify.  **The fix: take the two tangent vectors at
the MIDPOINT `G (T/2)` instead** — there `(T/2) • curveVelocity G (T/2)` and
`(-(T/2)) • curveVelocity G (T/2)` live in the *same* type with the *same* instances (so `norm_neg`,
`sub_smul`, `smul_eq_zero` all apply), and the two `GExp` values are `G T` and `G 0`, which are
*syntactically* `G T` and `G 0` — so they can be *rewritten* to `x` in the goal (`rw [hGT, hG0]`)
before applying injectivity.  No `set`-induced unfolding mismatch appears (see §68.3 (3)).

