  minimising vectors of the same norm.**  (A non-minimising member of maximal norm would, by the
  cut-down lemma, produce a witness with strictly smaller max.)

### 69.3 WHAT IS MISSING FOR THE WITNESS EXISTENCE (the one subtle point)

The set of witness pairs `{(u,v) : u ≠ v, GExp p u = GExp p v, max ‖·‖ ≤ M₀}` is **not closed**
(`u ≠ v` is open), so the minimum of `max ‖·‖` need not be attained.  A minimizing sequence either
has a non-degenerate limit (then `norm_eq_norm_of_isMinWitness` applies, using
"no witness has smaller max" — the definition of the infimum), or it **degenerates**:
`uₙ ≠ vₙ → u` with `GExp p uₙ = GExp p vₙ`.  In the degenerate case one needs:

> **`exp_p` is a local diffeomorphism at every non-conjugate vector** — equivalently: if
> `d(exp_p)_u` has a non-zero kernel then there is a Jacobi field `J` along `s ↦ exp_p(s u)` with
> `J(0) = J(1) = 0`, `J ≢ 0` (i.e. `IsConjugateTime cov γ ‖u‖`).

the library has the key instance: `Submission.FlowJacobi.exists_expMap_mfderiv_eq_jacobiTensor`
gives, for `‖v‖ < ρ`, chart data `(u,w)` of the geodesic with initial velocity `v` and a Jacobi
tensor `(Y,U)` with `Y 0 = 0`, `U 0 = 1` and
`(mfderiv 𝓘(ℝ,E) I (fun z => expMap cov x₀ z) v) x = frameVector (expMap v) ((Y 1) x)`.
So the missing step is only: *`mfderiv` non-injective ⇒ not locally injective* (the inverse
function theorem, contrapositive), plus the routine verification that `s ↦ frameVector(u s)((Y s) x)`
satisfies `IsJacobiAt` (from the `(Y,U)` ODE, exactly as in `RauchUpper.JData`), and the
re-parametrisation `s ↦ exp_p(s • (u/‖u‖))` of the conjugate time `1 ↦ ‖u‖`.
**With the dichotomy (A) two minimising vectors of equal norm / (B) a conjugate time `≤ M₀ < π/√K`,
Rauch disposes of (B) and (A) gives the broken geodesic loop.**  This is the next concrete
deliverable (item 1 below).

