### 71.2 THE CUT-POINT STEP: WHAT IS NOW PROVED, AND THE EXACT REMAINING GAP

Write `InjAtLeast hmet p m` for injectivity of `GExp p` on the open ball of radius `m` (fibre norm)
and suppose `m` is the injectivity radius: `InjAtLeast hmet p m` and `¬ InjAtLeast hmet p r` for
every `r > m`.

**Two mathematical facts established this session** (both elementary, and both now in
`Submission/CutPoint.lean`):

1. **`ν = m`.**  Let `ν` be the infimum of `max ‖u‖ ‖v‖` over *witness pairs* (`u ≠ v` with
   `GExp p u = GExp p v`).  Then `ν ≥ m` (no witness below the injectivity radius) and `ν ≤ r` for
   every `r > m` (a witness with both norms `< r` exists for every `r > m`), so `ν = m`.
2. **`CutPoint.norm_eq_and_minimising_of_witness_pair`.**  If `InjAtLeast hmet p m` holds and
   `u ≠ v` are witnesses of norm `≤ m`, then `‖u‖ = ‖v‖ = m` and **both rays are minimising**:
   `d(p, GExp p u) = ofReal m = d(p, GExp p v)`.  (`InjAtLeast hmet p m` excludes `u = v`; a
   non-minimising ray would have failed earlier, at a time `s < m`, contradicting minimisation below
   the injectivity radius via `CutWitness.exists_lt_of_not_minimising_ray`; and both being
   minimising forces the two lengths to be equal to the common distance.)
3. **`CutPoint.norm_eq_and_minimising_of_witness_sequence`** — the same conclusion from a
   *convergent* witness sequence `U n ≠ V n`, `GExp p (U n) = GExp p (V n)`, `‖U n‖ < m + 1/(n+1)`,
   `U n → u`, `V n → v`, `u ≠ v`, with `GExp p` continuous at `u` and `v`.

**So the whole cut-point step is reduced to the following single missing statement.**

> **(CP)** From `¬ InjAtLeast hmet p r` for all `r > m` (with `m` the injectivity radius) produce a
> convergent witness sequence `U n ≠ V n` with `‖U n‖ < m + 1/(n+1)`, `‖V n‖ < m + 1/(n+1)`,
> `GExp p (U n) = GExp p (V n)`, whose limits `u ≠ v` are distinct.

The *compactness* half of (CP) is **done**: `CutPoint.exists_tendsto_witness_sequence` takes the
existence of witness pairs with both norms `< r` for every `r > m` and produces a witness sequence
`U n ≠ V n` with `‖U n‖, ‖V n‖ < m + 1/(n+1)` together with limits `u, v` of norm `≤ m` (the
subsequence is extracted from the compact product of the **fibre** closed balls of radius `m + 1`,
using `isCompact_closedBall` and `ProperSpace (TangentSpace I p) := FiniteDimensional.proper`).
What is missing is the identification of the limits:

* **(a) Continuity of `GExp p` in the initial vector.**  The identifications
  `GExp p (U n) = GExp p (V n)` and the conclusion `GExp p u = GExp p v` at the limit require
  continuity of `GExp p` at `u` and `v`.  The library *has* continuity of the smooth chart
  exponential `expMap` (and `exists_pos_GExp_eq_expMap` identifies the two), but only on the set of
  vectors `v` for which a chart geodesic exists on `Ioo (-2) 2`.
* **(b) The degenerate case `u = v`.**  Then the witness sequence collapses and one needs the
  micro-local analysis at the limit: a non-zero kernel vector of `mfderiv expMap` there (bridge (B1),
  §70.1) gives a singular Jacobi tensor, which (B2), *now available* as
  `forall_not_locally_injective_of_norm_lt_pi_half`, forbids as soon as `‖u‖_fibre < π/2` and `u` is
  in the `expMap`-good region.  So (b) is *done modulo (a)*.

**Why (a) is a genuine obstacle (and not just laziness).**  `HasChartGeodesic` on `Ioo (-2) 2`
demands that the parametrised geodesic stays inside the chart target for **parameter** `t ∈ (-2,2)`,
i.e. for geodesic length up to `2‖v‖_fibre`: the *extension* of the geodesic by its own length
(going twice as far as the point we are interested in, `m = ‖u‖_fibre`).  The chart target contains
only some metric ball around `p`; both the "good radius" and the chart bounds `λ ≤ chartGram ≤ Λ` are
arbitrary positive chart-dependent constants, and the condition that matters,
`m/√λ ≤ ρ_good`, is **scale invariant** (rescaling the metric `g → s·g` multiplies `m` by `√s` and
`λ` by `s`; rescaling the chart by a linear map multiplies both the model norm and `λ` compatibly).
So neither trick can be used to enlarge the good region.  In the standard stereographic chart of the
round sphere the good radius is exactly `‖v‖_fibre < π/2` (the geodesic reaches the south pole at
parameter `π/‖v‖`), which is the *right* order of magnitude, but in a small chart it can be
arbitrarily small in metric terms.  Hence:

> **the cut point analysis of the injectivity radius needs a *global* exponential map**, i.e. an
> identification of `GExp p v` with the smooth model along the *whole* ray and continuity of
> `v ↦ GExp p v` — the "H1(b)" item recorded in the earlier Roadmap (global geodesic flow, glued
> from the `ChartFlowData`/`chartFlowExp` boxes).  Building that is the next self-contained piece of
> work; everything else in the cut-point step is in place.

