### 73.4 WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT GIVE FOR THE CUT-POINT STEP (the key analysis)

**What it gives.**  With `m = inj(p)`, `GExp p` is now a *continuous* map on `B̄_m`, and the
characterisation (6) says that inside `B̄_m` the exponential map is determined by the two continuous
functions `v ↦ d(p, GExp p v)` and `v ↦ d(GExp p (v/2), GExp p v)`.

**The fold (the remaining obstruction).**  The cut-point step of
`CutStep.exists_two_minimising_of_not_injAtLeast` needs `GExp p` to be continuous at the limits `u`,
`v` of a witness sequence whose norms converge to `m` *from above*: the terms `U n` have norms
`< m + 1/(n+1) > m`, i.e. they lie **outside** the ball on which continuity is available.  Continuity
"from outside" is exactly continuity *beyond the first cut point* — the **fold** — and no
distance-based method can give it:

* the characterisation (6) fails for `‖v‖ > inj(p)` because its *existence* half needs the ray to be
  minimising, i.e. `d(p, GExp p v) = ‖v‖`, which fails past the fold;
* the same failure is what rules out the "positions-only propagation" described below;
* the only known way to control `v ↦ GExp p v` beyond the fold is the **global geodesic flow**
  (smooth dependence on initial data, iterated along the ray) — the "H1(b)" item of §71.

**The proved variant (`hK`-side consequence).**  `InjRadius.two_mul_le_of_injAllAtLeast_of_geodCurve_loop`
already gives one half of Cheeger–Ebin's Lemma 1.8.  With `exists_pos_injAtLeast` one now knows that
