while `nS w → v` and `‖nS w‖ = m`, so `F (nS w) → F v` by the *inside-the-ball* continuity
(`GExpRay2.continuousOn_GExp_closedBall_of_injAtLeast`).  Adding the two gives `F w → F v`.
For `‖v‖ < m` the point `v` is an interior point of the ball and the inside-the-ball continuity
already applies; for `v = 0` one uses the ray bound `d(p, F w) ≤ ‖w‖`.

**Consequence.**  `GExp p` is continuous *at* every point of the closed ball of radius `inj(p)`,
not merely *within* it.  The "fold" is invisible as long as the limiting vector has norm `≤ inj(p)`,
which is exactly the situation of a witness sequence, because witness vectors of norm `< m` are
excluded by injectivity.

### 74.2 THE NEW CUT-POINT STEP (`Submission/CutStep2.lean`)

* **`LocallyInjectiveAtGExp hmet p w`**: `GExp p` is injective on some neighbourhood of `w`.
* **`exists_two_minimising_of_not_injAtLeast`**: if `InjAtLeast hmet p m` and it fails for every
  larger radius, and `LocallyInjectiveAtGExp hmet p w` holds for every `w` with `‖w‖ ≤ m`, then
  there are `u ≠ v` with `‖u‖ = ‖v‖ = m`, `GExp p u = GExp p v` and `d(p, GExp p u) = m`.

The proof is now short: `CutPoint.exists_tendsto_witness_sequence` produces the witness sequence
with limits `u`, `v` of norm `≤ m`; §74.1 gives `F u = F v`; injectivity on the *open* ball of radius
`m` forces `‖u‖ = ‖v‖ = m` (if one limit had norm `< m`, the corresponding terms are in the open
ball together with the other limit's terms — both norms `< m` — and injectivity would make them
equal, contradicting the witness property; the distance formula
`edist_GExp_eq_norm_of_le_of_injAtLeast` then pins the other norm to `m` as well); and the degenerate
case `u = v` is *by definition* the failure of `LocallyInjectiveAtGExp`.  The conclusion is
`CutPoint.norm_eq_and_minimising_of_witness_pair`.

This **supersedes** `Submission.CutStep.exists_two_minimising_of_not_injAtLeast`, whose hypotheses
(`hexp`, `hcont`, `hnotloc`, `hball`) require the chart at `p` to be so large that the *fibre* ball
of radius `m + 1` sits inside the *model* agreement ball of `expMap cov p`, i.e.

  `m + 1 < ρ₁ · √(lam/Lam)`   (`ρ₁` = the agreement radius of `exists_pos_GExp_eq_expMap`,

`lam`, `Lam` the chart-metric bounds of `ChartBounds.exists_chartGram_bounds` at `p`).  That is a
genuine constraint: `expMap` is only the *chart* exponential map and is junk once the chart geodesic
leaves the chart, so for a small chart and a large injectivity radius those hypotheses simply fail.
The new version needs no such thing.

### 74.3 WHAT IS *STILL* MISSING — AND IT IS NOW ONE CLEAN STATEMENT

The only unproved input of §74.2 is

> **`hloc`**: `LocallyInjectiveAtGExp hmet p w` for every `w` with `‖w‖ ≤ m = inj(p)`.

This is the classical statement *"`exp_p` is a local diffeomorphism at every non-conjugate vector"*,
phrased without conjugacy.  §73.4's "fold" analysis was really about this statement: a degenerate
witness sequence (`U n ≠ V n`, `F (U n) = F (V n)`, `U n, V n → u`) shows precisely that `F` fails
to be injective on every neighbourhood of `u`.

Two further remarks that sharpen what has to be proved.

* **The tangential-merging form is the only obstruction.**  The cut-down lemma
  (`CutWitness.exists_witness_lt_max`) lets one choose the minimising sequence of witness pairs so
  that **both** members are minimising with **equal** norm (`CutWitness.norm_eq_norm_of_isMinWitness`).
  The degenerate case then reads: two *distinct* minimising vectors `U n ≠ V n` of the *same* norm
  `t n → m` with the same image, whose directions `ξ n`, `η n` both converge to the common limit
  direction.  So the missing statement is exactly *"a minimising geodesic does not have a nearby
  distinct minimising geodesic with the same endpoint and the same length"* — the classical
  non-conjugacy statement in its sharpest form (the Jacobi field realising the tangency is the
  witness of the conjugate point).

* **`hloc` is *not* available from `InjAtLeast` alone at points of norm `< m` either.**  If
  `‖w‖ < m` and `F v = F w` with `‖v‖ > m` then `F` is not locally injective at `w`; the minimising
  vector of `F v` is (uniquely) `w`, because its norm is `‖w‖ < m`, so there is no contradiction
  with injectivity.  Hence `hloc` has to come from the differential of `exp`, not from injectivity.

