### 74.5 THE PRECISE SHAPE OF THE REMAINING GAP IN `hloc` (analysis of this session)

The following analysis was carried out in full and should save the next session a lot of time.

* **The cut-point step does not need *continuity* beyond `inj`** — that is §74.1.  What it needs is
  `hloc` (§74.3), i.e. the absence of *nearby distinct* preimages of the same point.  Note that a
  collision pair `u ≠ v` with `‖u‖ = ‖v‖ = m` (exactly what the cut-point step *produces*) does
  **not** contradict `hloc at u`: `hloc` only forbids collisions with `w, w'` both in a single
  neighbourhood of `u`.  So `hloc` remains consistent with the conclusion.

* **A "one chart, no transitions" iteration of the flow does work** — the step size issue is not a
  real obstacle.  Writing a *unit* tangent vector at `q` in the chart `z₀` as `frameVector z₀ q u`
  gives `‖u‖_model ≤ 1/√λ` (`λ` = the lower bound for `chartGram z₀` on the box `Z`), so a step of
  **arclength `h := √λ/c`** (`c = flowScale D`) has phase `(φ q, c·(h u))` of coordinate norm
  `≤ 1`, i.e. inside `chartData`; and the fibre norm is preserved by the flow, so the *same* `h`
  works at every step.  (The constraint `1/√λ ≤ 1/c` which appears when one insists on using the
  *unit* velocity with the full flow time `c⁻¹` is an artefact of that normalisation.)

* **What the iteration still lacks is the *conclusion*, not the steps.**  Each step map (in the
  state space `(x,ψ) ∈ E × E`, `ψ` the chart coordinate of a unit tangent vector at `φ⁻¹ x`)
  is smooth and **injective** (flow map ∘ linear scaling), and its composition along a ray is
  therefore injective.  But `GExp p` is `w ↦` the **position of the endpoint** of the ray, and the
  map "state ↦ position of the flow line" is *not* injective (it is a submersion: the tangent
  bundle has twice the dimension of the manifold).  Hence injectivity of the state map does not
  give injectivity of `GExp p`; what one needs is that the differential of the *state map* is
  invertible and that the final "small step" determines the state — i.e. precisely the classical
  differential/inverse-function-theorem argument, for which the derivative of the flow's time-`t`
  map must be shown invertible (see `Commons.Analysis.ODE.injective_fderiv_flow_timeMap`).
  Concretely: with the chain rule,
  `d(GExp p)_u = (d Ψ)_{state(u)} ∘ d(state map)_u` where `Ψ(q,z) = GExp q z`, and one shows
  `d(GExp p)_u` is surjective (hence injective, both spaces being `n`-dimensional) by exhibiting the
  *inverse* of `d Ψ` along the image of `d(state map)`.

* **A limitation of `ChartFlowData`.**  Its smoothness hypothesis `hjoint` is stated only at phases
  **in `chartData z₀ Z`**.  Note however that `hjoint` is a `ContDiffAt` statement, i.e. a *germ*
  condition: it says that `(q,s) ↦ α q.1 q.2` is `C^∞` at each `(p,t)` with `p ∈ chartData z₀ Z`
  and `|t| < ρ`, hence on an *open neighbourhood* of that set.  So smoothness of α is available
  slightly **beyond** `chartData × (-ρ,ρ)`.  For the iteration this should be exploited by choosing
  the compact piece `Z` (hence `chartData`) generously, so that every phase at which the flow is
  evaluated lies in the interior of the smoothness region.

* **The derivative of the flow's time-`t` map.**  This is the ingredient that converts "the steps
  are injective" into "`GExp p` is locally injective".  It is available from the flow-inverse
  identity (round trip) plus the chain rule: with `Φ_t(q) := α q t` one has
  `Φ_{-t} ∘ Φ_t = id` near `p` (both `p` and `α p t` in the *open* set `O`), so
  `fderiv Φ_{-t} (α p t) ∘ fderiv Φ_t p = id` and `fderiv Φ_t p` is **injective**.  A request for
  this statement as a generic ODE lemma (`Commons.Analysis.ODE.injective_fderiv_flow_timeMap`) is in
  flight; note that `IsOpen O` is *essential* (without it the statement is false — a colleague
  produced a formal counterexample with `O = {0}` and `α ≡ 0`).

### 74.6 GOTCHAS (additions)

* **The `SMul`/`Norm` instance depends on the base point.**  The same phenomenon as for `Norm`
  (below) affects `•`: the tangent space `TangentSpace I a` carries its *own* `SMul ℝ`/`Norm`
  instances coming from the `RiemannianBundle`, and transferring a term across a change of base
  point (by `rw`, by `← h` with `h : a = b`, or by building a term whose expected type is
  `TangentSpace I b`) can fail with "Application type mismatch: … has type `TangentSpace I a` but
  is expected to have type `TangentSpace I b`", even though both reduce to `E`.  This is why the
  lattice of `rw`s that identifies `GExp p ((s+t) • v)` with `GExp (GExp p (s • v)) (t • γ̇ s)` is
  delicate: the *coefficient order* (`s * ‖v‖` vs `‖v‖ * s`) and the *form* of the reparametrised
  vector (`(s * ‖v‖) • ξ` vs `s • (‖v‖ • ξ)`) both matter, because `rw` matches the *stored* form
  while `curveVelocity_comp_mul` produces the `c * s` order.  (Consequence: the only non-unit ray
  continuation lemma currently available is `GExpGlob.GExp_add_smul`, stated for a unit vector; the
  non-unit form must be assembled by hand at each use, which is best done by first normalising the
  velocity with `unitOf` and never rewriting the function.)

* **The *norm instance* depends on the base point.**  For `w : TangentSpace I a`, the fibre norm is the
  instance `instNormedAddCommGroupOfRiemannianBundleOfIsTopologicalAddGroupOfContinuousConstSMulReal a`
  coming from the `RiemannianBundle`, and *different base points give different instance terms*.  As a
  consequence `Eq` statements between tangent vectors at different base points typecheck (the *types*
  `TangentSpace I a` and `TangentSpace I b` are both defeq to `E`), but **rewriting inside a `‖·‖`**
  across a change of base point does not: the norm's instance argument is already elaborated and is
  not updated by the rewrite ("Application type mismatch: `-1 • curveVelocity …` has type
  `TangentSpace I (GExp x ((-1 * t) • -v))` but is expected to have type
  `TangentSpace I (GExp x (t • v))`").  Workarounds: keep the base point syntactically fixed (e.g.
  use `curveVelocity_comp_mul` to reparametrise, rather than rewriting the curve), state the
  transported form with an explicit `▸`, or transport the *function* first and re-elaborate.

* **The fibre norm is not the model norm.**  For `w : TangentSpace I x`, `‖w‖` is the *Riemannian*
  norm, and `CutStep.sqrt_chartGram_eq_norm` identifies it with `√(chartGram x x w w)`;
  `chartGram_self_apply` shows that this is the model-space inner product of `w` *with the fibre
  inner product at `x`*, which is only *comparable* to the model norm
  (`ChartBounds.exists_chartGram_bounds`).  So for `w : E` one must not silently read `‖w‖` as the
  fibre norm of `w`, and conversely.
* **`ChartedSpace E (TangentSpace I x)` does not synthesise.**  Statements of the form
  `HasMFDerivAt 𝓘(ℝ,ℝ) 𝓘(ℝ,E) (fun s : ℝ ↦ s • v) 0 _` with `v : TangentSpace I x` *fail*
  instance search (the tangent space carries its own `NormedAddCommGroup`/`NormedSpace` from the
  Riemannian bundle, and they are not definitionally equal to the ambient ones).  With `v : E` the
  same statement elaborates — that is why the library phrases such lemmas with `E`-vectors.
* `rw [smul_smul]`: in this Mathlib the lemma reads `a • b • x = (a * b) • x` (so the useful
  direction is forward, and `← smul_smul` searches for `(a*b) • x`).
* `rw [← h]` rewrites *inside* subterms: to replace only the "outer" occurrence of a variable (e.g.
  `w` in `(m / ‖w‖) • w`) use `congrArg` on the intended subterm, or `nth_rewrite`.
* `Metric.mem_closedBall`/`mem_ball` state membership through `dist`; after a `rw` of the goal it is
  easy to end up with a `dist`-shaped goal where a norm-shaped term was expected.  Write
  `rw [Metric.mem_closedBall, dist_zero_right]` explicitly.
* `Filter.Tendsto.div` needs the *limit* of the denominator to be non-zero; `tendsto_normSpherical`
  therefore carries the hypothesis `v ≠ 0` (the case `v = 0` is handled separately by the ray
  bound).
* `zero_le` is a *theorem* (`0 ≤ a`), not a function: for a pointwise `g ≤ f` in the squeeze
  theorem use `fun _ => bot_le` (in `ℝ≥0∞`, `⊥ = 0`).
* **`λ` is a reserved token** (the lambda binder), so it cannot be used as a variable name; the
  homogeneity lemmas therefore use `c`.
* `HasDerivAt.scomp` has the *point* `x` as its **first** explicit argument in this Mathlib
  (`HasDerivAt.scomp x hg hh` with `hg` the outer function); `hw.scomp hh` with the method notation
  is resolved differently and reports an unhelpful "expected `HasDerivAt ?m ?m (?m ?m)`" error.  The
  `_of_eq` variant takes `(x)`, then `(hh)`, then `(hy)`.
* `HasDerivAt.const_smul` produces the function `c • f` (a `Pi.smul`), not `fun x ↦ c • f x`; the two
  are definitionally equal but `simpa only [Pi.smul_apply]` does **not** see it (there is no
  application to rewrite); use
  `rw [show c • f = (fun x ↦ c • f x) from rfl] at h`.
* `ContDiffAt`/`ContMDiffAt` of a *pair* of variables: `contDiffAt_id.fst.prodMk (… .smul … .snd)`
  builds the map `zw ↦ (zw.1, c • zw.2)`; the composite `(fun q ↦ α q.1 q.2) ∘ (…)` is closed by
  `simpa only [Function.comp_def]`.
* `Field notation` on `ContMDiffOn`: `contMDiffOn_id.const_mul` does **not** work (the lemma is
  `Function.const_mul`); use `(contMDiff_const.mul contMDiff_id).contMDiffOn` or prove the linear
  estimate separately with `smul`.  `fun_prop` does **not** handle `ContMDiffOn` here.

