### 75.2 THE SYNC LEMMA (`Submission/LensSync.lean`) — THE SET OF WITNESS LENGTHS IS
### UPWARD CLOSED

**`LensSync.edist_eq_sub_of_minimising_ray`**: for a unit `ξ` and `0 ≤ ℓ ≤ t` with the ray of `ξ`
minimising at **both** `ℓ` and `t`, the two points of the ray satisfy
`d(GExp p (ℓ • ξ), GExp p (t • ξ)) = t - ℓ`.  (Upper bound: the ray bound
`GExpRay2.edist_GExp_smul_le_mul_norm`.  Lower bound: the triangle inequality.)  This looks
trivial, but it is exactly the missing hypothesis of the no-corner lemma in the next statement.

**`LensSync.eq_GExp_of_minimising_pair` (the SYNC LEMMA)**: let `ξ ≠ η` be unit vectors at `p` and
`ℓ > 0` with

* both rays **minimising at `ℓ`**, and
* `GExp p (ℓ • ξ) = GExp p (ℓ • η)` (the *lens closes* at length `ℓ`).

Then for every `t ≥ ℓ` at which the ray of `ξ` is still minimising,

`GExp p (t • ξ) = GExp p (t • η)`.

The two rays stay **in sync**.  The proof is a single application of the *no-corner lemma*
`GSField.eq_GExp_of_minimising_broken` to the broken path `p → x → y` with
`x = GExp p (ℓ • η) = GExp p (ℓ • ξ)` and `y = GExp p (t • ξ)`: the two side conditions are exactly
`d(p,x) = ℓ` and `d(x,y) = t - ℓ` (the second from `edist_eq_sub_of_minimising_ray`), and the
conclusion identifies `y` with the point of the **η**-ray at time `t`.

**`LensSync.eq_GExp_smul_of_witness_pair`** is the same statement in the form of a *minimising
witness pair* (`u ≠ v`, `‖u‖ = ‖v‖ = ℓ`, `GExp p u = GExp p v`, both rays minimising at `ℓ`):
`GExp p ((t/ℓ) • u) = GExp p ((t/ℓ) • v)` for every `t ≥ ℓ` at which the ray of `u` is minimising.

**Consequence (this is the structural content for the cut-point step).**  Write
`L := {ℓ : ∃ a *minimising witness pair* of length ℓ}`.  The sync lemma says
**`[ℓ, ∞) ∩ [0, τ] ⊆ L` for every `ℓ ∈ L`** (with `τ = min(τ(ξ), τ(η)) ≥ ℓ`).  So `L` is
*upward closed from each of its elements*; in particular `L ⊇ (inf L, M)` for some `M > inf L`.
Since `inf L = inj(p)` on the nose (see §75.4), the only question left in the cut-point step is
whether the infimum is **attained**.

### 75.3 **THE NAIVE LENS LEMMA IS FALSE** (a negative result that saves the next session real time)

In §74 it was conjectured that the cut-point step could be finished by a **lens lemma**

> (L)  if `sec ≤ K`, `ℓ < π/√K`, and two *minimising* rays from `p` of the same length `ℓ` meet at
> the same point, then they coincide,

which would immediately exclude the *degenerate* case of the witness sequence (`u = v`) and hence
finish `CutStep2`.  **This statement is FALSE.**  Counterexample: the real projective plane `ℝP²`
with a metric of constant curvature `K > 0`.  There `π/√K` is the **conjugate** radius, but the
injectivity radius is `π/(2√K)`; a point `x` of the cut locus at distance `π/(2√K)` from `p` is
reached by **two** minimising geodesics of length `π/(2√K) < π/√K` (the two halves of the great
circle through `p` and its antipode, which are identified in `ℝP²` — concretely, with `sec = 4` and
`p = [N]`, the equator points are at distance `π/4` and are reached by two meridians), and those two
geodesics form the lens whose flushness yields the closed geodesic of length `2·inj = π/2`.  So the
lens exists well below the conjugate radius.

The correct classical dichotomy (Cheeger–Ebin Lemma 5.6, and Xu arXiv:1401.5549 Lemma 2.2) is

> at a *minimum* point of `d(p,·)` on the cut locus, either `p` is conjugate to the point along
> **every** minimal geodesic joining them, **or** there are two distinct minimal geodesics (a
> geodesic loop).

The **lens case is harmless** — it is exactly what the cut-point step *wants* (it produces the
broken closed geodesic loop of length `2 inj(p)`).  The only case that has to be *excluded* is the
**degenerate** one, where the two minimising vectors merge, i.e. where `GExp p` fails to be locally
injective at a vector of norm exactly `inj(p)`; and there the classical input is *"`exp_p` is a local
diffeomorphism at non-conjugate vectors"* (equivalently, by Rauch, `inj(p) ≥ π/√K` otherwise).

Two further consequences worth recording:

* the `sec ≥ 1` **hinge comparison** (`HingeGlobal.HingeCompAt`, `HingeClosed.gcompHolds_pi_le`,
  available in the project!) is **exactly tight** on a geodesic lens and therefore *cannot* see it:
  for a lens with angle `α` at `p`, sides `ℓ, ℓ - ε` and third side `ε`, the comparison reads
  `ε ≤ arccos(A(ε))` with `A(ε) = cos ℓ cos(ℓ-ε) + sin ℓ sin(ℓ-ε) cos α ≤ cos ε`, i.e.
  `arccos(A(ε)) ≥ ε`, with equality **iff** `α = 0`.  So a lower curvature bound permits lenses;
  only an *upper* curvature bound (Rauch's direction, `sec ≤ K`) could forbid them — and it does
  not forbid them either (see the `ℝP²` counterexample).
* **the degenerate case is intrinsically about the differential of `exp`**, so no amount of
  distance-function (hinge/Rauch) comparison can finish the cut-point step; the inverse function
  theorem at a non-conjugate vector is needed.  This confirms §74.3 and §74.6 (a).

