The analysis of §75.3, §75.8 and §75.9 sharpens the whole Klingenberg chapter to the following
classical shape (Cheeger–Ebin, Lemma 5.6), which the *already proved* library pieces fit into:

> **`inj(p) = min { conj(p), ½ ℓ(p) }`**, where `conj(p)` is the conjugate radius at `p` and `ℓ(p)`
> the length of the shortest closed geodesic loop at `p`.

The two halves:

* **`conj(p) ≥ π/√K`** for `sec ≤ K`.  *Available in the project* in the form
  `RauchGlobal.eqOn_zero_of_jacobi` (`sec ≤ 4` ⟹ no conjugate point before `π/2`), i.e. exactly the
  **conjugate branch** of the cut point analysis.  Hmm: careful, the *available* statement is the
  Jacobi-field one; converting it into a statement about `conj(p)` needs the identification
  "`(d exp_p)_w` singular ⟺ a non-zero Jacobi field along the ray vanishes at `0` and `1`", which is
  `FlowJacobi.mfderiv_expMap_eq_jacobiTensor` + `JacobiTensor.jacobiTensor_injective` (also
  available).  *This is the cheapest route to the conjugate branch and should be done first.*
* **`inj(p) ≤ ½ ℓ(p)`**: a geodesic loop of length `T` forces `inj ≤ T/2`.  *Available*:
