  injectivity produces a (broken) minimising loop of length `2 inj(p)` — is the **cut-point step**,
  and that is where all the work is.

So the cut-point step has to produce, from `InjAtLeast p m` + failure of injectivity beyond `m`,
one of:

1. **two distinct minimising vectors of norm `m` with the same image** — the *lens*; then
   `σ * τ⁻¹` is a broken geodesic loop at `p` of length `2m`; by **flushness** (corner cutting +
   the existence of a shortest closed curve in a free homotopy class) it yields a closed geodesic of
   length `≤ 2m`; **this case is proved by `CutStep3.exists_two_minimising_or_not_locallyInjective`**
   modulo the second alternative not occurring;
2. **a merging**, i.e. `GExp p` not locally injective at some `w` with `‖w‖ = m`.  Here the
   classical statement (Xu arXiv:1401.5549, Theorem B) is that `p` is *conjugate* to `GExp p w`
   along **every** minimal geodesic; hence `conj(p) ≤ m` and `m ≥ π/√K` by the conjugate branch.
   This is the case that the naive lens lemma of §75.3 was meant to exclude and that §75.8 excludes
   by the *thin lens* argument (Rauch II), or alternatively by the differential theory of §75.5.

