**(a) The *minimal-witness* reduction (this works, and it is almost the cut-point step).**
`CutPoint.exists_minWitness_or_not_locally_injective` (chart level) says that either there is a
witness pair `a ≠ b` with `GExp a = GExp b` whose `max ‖·‖` is minimal among all witness pairs with
`max ‖·‖ < R`, or `expMap` is not locally injective at some `w` with `‖w‖ < R`.  If the minimal
witness exists *and* `m < R`, then its `max`-norm is `≤ m` (there are witness pairs with
`max ∈ [m, m + ε)` for every `ε > 0`, and all of them lie in the range), and — since a minimal
witness has **equal** norms (`CutWitness.norm_eq_norm_of_isMinWitness`) — both norms are `≤ m`, so
`CutPoint.norm_eq_and_minimising_of_witness_pair` turns the pair into the desired **two distinct
minimising vectors of norm exactly `m` with the same image**.  This is precisely the known
"cut-point step below the local-diffeomorphism radius" (`LocalDiffeo.exists_two_minimising_of_not_injAtLeast_of_lt`)
and it shows *why* the hypothesis `hloc` is needed only for `‖w‖` beyond the chart/flow box at `p`:
the degenerate alternative can be excluded by Rauch (`CutPoint.forall_not_locally_injective_of_norm_lt_pi_half`,
which needs the *chart* norm `< π/2` and the *model* norm `< ρ`) — and those two bounds are not
available at radius `m` once `m` exceeds the box.

**(b) The merging case forces the second geodesic to be non-minimising.**  In the degenerate case
one gets `Uₙ, Vₙ → u` with *both* norms tending to `m` (from `max → m` and the injectivity on the
open ball: if both norms were `< m` then `Uₙ = Vₙ`), and
`d(p, yₙ) = ‖Uₙ‖ = min(‖Uₙ‖, ‖Vₙ‖)`: indeed if `d(p,yₙ) < ‖Uₙ‖ =: ℓ ≤ m` then a minimising vector
`z` with `‖z‖ = d(p,yₙ) < ℓ ≤ m` and `z ≠ Uₙ` would satisfy the hypotheses of
`norm_eq_and_minimising_of_witness_pair`, forcing `‖z‖ = m > d(p,yₙ)`, a contradiction.  Hence
`d(p, yₙ) = ‖Uₙ‖ → m` and the *maximal* one of `‖Uₙ‖, ‖Vₙ‖` is `≥ m`: the longer of the two
geodesics from `p` to `yₙ` is *not* minimising (it is longer than `d(p,yₙ)`).

**(c) The remaining gap, sharply.**  With (b), the cut time of the direction of the *longer*
geodesic satisfies `m ≤ τ < ‖Vₙ‖ ≈ m`, so `τ → m`; and at `τ` either there are two minimising
vectors (whence, by (a), a lens pair of norm `m`: if a witness pair of norm `τ ≤ m` exists then
`norm_eq_and_minimising_of_witness_pair` forces `τ = m`), **or** `p` is conjugate along the ray at
time `τ`.  Either of the two conclusions is enough: the second gives, by
`ConjRadius.pi_half_le_of_isConjugateTime` (Rauch), `τ ≥ π/2` and hence `m ≥ π/2`.

So the merging case is *harmless* — a lens pair or `m ≥ π/2` — **provided** one has the
*fixed-direction* form of Klingenberg's lemma:

> at a cut time `τ(ξ)` of the ray of a unit `ξ`, either there is a witness
> `v ≠ τ ξ` with `‖v‖ ≤ τ` and `GExp p v = GExp p (τ ξ)`, or `IsConjugateTime cov (ray ξ) τ`.

This is weaker than Xu's Theorem B (no "smooth loop", no "exactly two", no Hessian of the distance
function) and it is the precise missing input of the whole cut-point step.  It should be attacked
either

1. by *iteration of the local chart analysis* along the ray (the global geodesic flow: the chart
   analysis applies at a point of the ray after translating the problem to a chart at that point —
   the obstacle is that the arriving velocities are unit vectors, so the "small velocity" hypothesis
   of `CutPoint.exists_jacobiTensor_singular_of_not_locally_injective` is not met), or
2. by the tangential-segment-domain / locally-minimising-geodesic argument of Xu (arXiv:1401.5549,
   §2–3), or
3. by the Sturm/Rauch-II route of §75.8.

Note that the arguments in (a)–(b) are elementary and only use the library's existing dichotomy and
the `norm_eq_and_minimising_of_witness_pair` lemma; what fails is the passage from "the ray stops
being minimising at `τ`" to "there is a conjugate point at `τ`" — i.e. exactly the classical
content of Klingenberg's lemma.
