### 77.1 WHAT WAS PROVED

**(A) `Submission/CutKlingenberg.lean`** (namespace `Submission.CutKlingenberg`).

* **`exists_two_minimising_or_not_locallyInjective_of_cutTime`** — *the fixed-direction Klingenberg
  lemma*, which is exactly the missing input identified in §76.7.  Let `InjAtLeast hmet p m` with
  `m > 0`, let `ξ` be unit with `cutTime hmet p ξ = m` (i.e. `ξ` realises the injectivity radius as a
  cut time).  Then **either** there is a second *minimising* vector `v ≠ m • ξ` of norm `m` with the
  same image as `m • ξ` (so the two rays form a broken minimising geodesic loop at `p` of length
  `2m`), **or** `¬ LocallyInjectiveAtGExp hmet p (m • ξ)`.
* **`exists_two_minimising_of_cutTime_of_locallyInjective`** — the two-minimising-vector conclusion
  under the *single-point* hypothesis `LocallyInjectiveAtGExp hmet p (m • ξ)` (with the full metric
  conclusion `d(p, GExp p v) = d(p, GExp p (m • ξ)) = m`).

  This sharpens §74/§75's `CutStep3.exists_two_minimising_of_hloc_sphere`, whose hypothesis is local
  injectivity at **every** point of the sphere of radius `m`: here it is needed only at the endpoint
  `m • ξ` of the cut ray, which is what the classical Klingenberg lemma produces.

  **Proof (the part that §75.9 believed to be blocked by "the fold").**  For `tₙ = m + 1/(n+1) > m`
  the ray is not minimising (`CutTime.minimisingSet_eq_Icc`), so a minimising vector `vₙ` from `p` to
  `GExp p(tₙ • ξ)` (Hopf–Rinow) has `‖vₙ‖ < tₙ` and `d(p, GExp p(tₙ • ξ)) = ofReal ‖vₙ‖`; the ray
  bound gives `d(p, GExp p(tₙ • ξ)) → d(p, GExp p(m • ξ)) = m`, hence `‖vₙ‖ → m`.  A subsequence
  `vₙ → v` has `‖v‖ = m`, and the identification `GExp p v = GExp p(m • ξ)` needs continuity of
  `GExp p` at the limits — which is available **even though the sequence `vₙ` approaches `v` from
  outside the ball**, because `GExpBoundary.tendsto_GExp_of_tendsto_of_norm_le` only requires the
  *limit* norm to be `≤ m` (it uses the radial projection onto the sphere of radius `m` plus the ray
  bound).  This is the point at which the "fold" of §75.9 is *not* an obstacle: the fold only bites
  at points of norm **strictly greater** than the injectivity radius, and at the cut time the limit
  has norm exactly `m`.  The dichotomy then follows as in §73: if `v ≠ m • ξ` we are done, and if
  `v = m • ξ` then the two sequences `vₙ` and `tₙ • ξ` — distinct, with the same image, both
  converging to `m • ξ` — witness `¬ LocallyInjectiveAtGExp hmet p (m • ξ)`.

**(B) `Submission/InjRadiusCut.lean`** (namespace `Submission.InjRadiusCut`).

* `cutTime_lt_of_not_minimising`.
* **`injAtLeast_of_forall_cutTime_le`**: if every ray is minimising up to `m`, then `GExp p` is
  injective on the open ball of radius `m`.
* **`injAtLeast_iff_forall_cutTime_le`**: `InjAtLeast hmet p m ↔ ∀ ξ, ‖ξ‖ = 1 → m ≤ cutTime p ξ`.
  Together with §77.3 below this is the classical statement `inj(p) = min_ξ τ(ξ)` (Xu Thm 1.2 /
  Petersen), i.e. **the injectivity radius is the minimum of the cut times**.
* **`exists_cutTime_lt_of_not_injAtLeast`**: `¬ InjAtLeast hmet p m → ∃ ξ unit, cutTime p ξ < m`.

  **Proof of the converse direction.**  Given a witness pair `u ≠ v` with `‖u‖, ‖v‖ < m` and
  `GExp p u = GExp p v`, the cut-time hypothesis forces `‖u‖ = ‖v‖ =: ρ` and both rays to be
  *minimising* at `ρ`: a strictly smaller norm for one of the two would make the other ray
  non-minimising strictly before `m`, contradicting `m ≤ τ`.  The **sync lemma**
  (`Submission.LensSync.eq_GExp_of_minimising_pair`) then shows that the two rays agree on all of
  `[ρ, m)`, so (two geodesics agreeing on a non-degenerate interval are equal —
  `GlobalExp.eqOn_Ioo_of_isGeodesicAt_of_eq`) the two rays are the same geodesic on `(0, m)`.  But
  `GExp p` is injective on a *positive* ball (`GExpRay2.exists_pos_injAtLeast`), and on a small ball
  the two rays are `GExp p (s • ξ)` and `GExp p (s • η)`; their agreement there forces `ξ = η`, hence
  `u = v` — contradiction.

  This equivalence is what makes the two classical descriptions of the injectivity radius
  interchangeable, and it is the form in which the cut-point step should be attacked (see §77.4).

### 77.2 CONSEQUENCE FOR THE CUT-POINT STEP

The cut-point step can now be obtained **without** the hypothesis `hloc_sphere` of §73/§74/§75 in
the following situation: let `InjAtLeast hmet p m` and `∀ r > m, ¬ InjAtLeast hmet p r`, and let
`ξ` be a direction with `cutTime p ξ = m`.  Then, unless `m` is a conjugate time, the fixed-direction
Klingenberg lemma gives two minimising vectors of norm `m` (because for a *non-conjugate* direction
the classical equivalence "non-conjugate ⟺ `d exp` invertible ⟹ `GExp` a local diffeomorphism"
excludes the second alternative).  So the sharp remaining gap of the cut-point step is

> `∃ ξ, cutTime p ξ = m`  **or**  a way to handle the merging alternative at the sphere,

and §77.3 shows that the first of these is the natural target.

### 77.3 THE ATTAINMENT OF THE INJECTIVITY RADIUS AS A CUT TIME (open)

`exists_cutTime_lt_of_not_injAtLeast` gives, for every `r > m` (with `m = inj(p)`), a direction `ξ_r`
with `m ≤ τ(ξ_r) < r`, so the cut times approach `m` from above and admit a subsequence
`ξ_k → ξ`.  For `t < m` the rays of `ξ_k` are minimising at `t` and `tendsto_GExp_of_tendsto_of_norm_le`
passes the minimisation to the limit, giving `τ(ξ) ≥ m`; the *equality* `τ(ξ) = m` would follow if
one could pass the *non*-minimisation at `t > m` to the limit, which is exactly the "fold" (joint
continuity at points of norm `> inj(p)`), i.e. the global geodesic flow of §71 ("H1(b)").  Note that
if the cut-point step *succeeds* (two minimising vectors of norm `m`), then those two rays meet at
their common cut time `m` by the sharpness argument below, so the attainment is a byproduct.

### 77.4 THE SHARPNESS OF THE MEETING TIME (the classical "cut locus" statement)

With the machinery now in place the following classical statement is provable, and it is the sharp
form of the sync lemma: **if `ξ ≠ η` are unit, both rays minimising at `ℓ > 0`, and
`GExp p (ℓ • ξ) = GExp p (ℓ • η)`, then `cutTime p ξ = ℓ = cutTime p η`.**  Indeed, if `τ(ξ) > ℓ`
then the sync lemma gives agreement of the two rays on `[ℓ, τ(ξ)]`, whence (equality of
parametrised geodesics on a non-degenerate interval, then injectivity of `GExp p` on a small ball as
in §77.1(B)) `ξ = η`.  Consequences: a "lens" of two distinct minimising geodesics from `p` to `x`
has length exactly the cut time of its directions, and hence `2 ℓ ≥ ℓ(p)` is sharp precisely at the
cut time.  **This was formalised at once** in `Submission/InjRadiusCut.lean` as
`cutTime_eq_of_minimising_pair` and `cutTime_eq_cutTime_of_minimising_pair`, together with the
witness-pair form `cutTime_eq_of_witness_pair` (two distinct minimising vectors of the same norm and
the same image have cut time exactly that norm, in both directions).  In particular, *if the
cut-point step succeeds* — two minimising vectors of norm `m = inj(p)` — then the injectivity radius
is attained as the cut time of both directions, so the attainment question of §77.3 comes for free in
that branch, and `inj(p) = ½ ℓ(p)` holds there (the two halves of the broken loop are exactly
minimising minimal geodesics).

