### 78.2 The exact shape of the even-dimensional (Berger–Klingenberg 1960) proof

Petersen, *Riemannian Geometry*, Theorem 6.5.1 + Lemma 6.4.7 + Lemma 5.7.12 (see also
Abresch–Meyer, MSRI 30, §1).  For `M` compact, **orientable**, **even-dimensional**, `0 < sec ≤ K`
(with `K < 4` for the strict form): `inj(M) ≥ π/√K`.  Proof:

1. **(Lemma 6.4.7 = Klingenberg's lemma.)** `inj(p) = min{conj(p), ½ℓ(p)}`, where `ℓ(p)` is the
   length of the shortest **geodesic loop** at `p`; and for compact `M`,
   `inj(M) = min{conj(M), ½ℓ(M)}` with `ℓ(M) = inf_p ℓ(p)`.
2. If `inj(M) < π/√K` then `conj(M) ≥ π/√K > inj(M)`, so the infimum is realised by a geodesic
   loop; the **symmetric midpoint argument** shows that the shortest loop (at a point realising
   `inj(M)`) is a genuine **closed geodesic**, of length `2 inj(M) < 2π/√K`.
3. **(Synge.)** For `M` even-dimensional and orientable, the normal holonomy around a closed
   geodesic lies in `SO(n-1)` with `n-1` **odd**, hence fixes a vector (Synge's lemma), i.e. there
   is a **closed parallel unit normal field** `v₀` along `c`.
4. **(Second variation.)** The family `c_ε(s) = exp_{c(s)}(ε v₀(s))` consists of **closed** curves
   with `L(c_ε) < L(c) = 2 inj(M)` for small `ε ≠ 0` (the second variation is `-∫ sec < 0`).
5. Since `c_ε ⊆ B(c_ε(0), ½L(c_ε))` and `½L(c_ε) < inj(M) ≤ inj(c_ε(0))`, every point of `c_ε` is
   joined to `c_ε(0)` by a **unique** minimising geodesic.  Letting `c_ε(t_ε)` be the point of
   *maximal distance* from `c_ε(0)`, the segment `s_ε` to it is **perpendicular** to `c_ε` at
   `c_ε(t_ε)` (first variation at a farthest point).  As `ε → 0`, `t_ε → inj(M)` and `s_ε`
   subconverges to a segment from `c(0)` to `c(inj(M))` perpendicular to `c` there.
6. **(Lemma 5.7.12.)** Because `π/√K > inj(M)` and `c` realises the injectivity radius at `c(0)`,
   there are **exactly two** segments from `c(0)` to `c(inj(M))` — the two halves of `c` — and both
   arrive **tangentially** at `c(inj(M))`.  A third segment arriving perpendicularly is a
   contradiction.  ∎

### 78.3 What this session's parametric chain does *not* do (correcting a hope)

I first hoped that the parametric jacobiTensor theorem (`∀‖v‖ < π/√K, expMap locally injective at
`v``) would discharge the cut-point step hypothesis `hloc_sphere`
(`LocallyInjectiveAtGExp hmet p w` for `‖w‖ = inj(p)`), because `InjAtLeast p m` with `m < π/√K`
would give it.  **This is not so**: `LocallyInjectiveAtGExp` is a statement about the **global**
`GExp p`, while the jacobiTensor theorems live in the **chart model** `expMap`; by
`GlobalExp.exists_pos_GExp_eq_expMap` the two agree only on the chart-validity ball around the
origin, and nothing in this session reaches beyond it.  The chart/global gap is precisely the
flow-iteration gap of §73, and it is still the wall.  What the parametric chain *does* give: the
chart-level cut-point machinery (`CutStep`, `CutStep2`, `CutStep3`, which all carry an explicit
agreement-radius hypothesis `hexp`) can now be run with `m < π/√K` **provided** the agreement radius
`R ≥ m`, instead of only with `m < π/2`.

### 78.4 The "fit lemma" (Lemma 5.7.12, first part) and exactly what it needs

> two **distinct** minimising vectors `u ≠ v` of the **same** norm `m` at `p` with the same image,
> with `InjAtLeast hmet p m` and `m < conj(p)` (which `§78.1`'s `pi_div_sqrt_le_of_isConjugateTime`
> supplies as soon as `m < π/√K`), have **opposite terminal velocities** at `q = GExp p u` — i.e.
> the concatenation of the two rays is a genuine geodesic at `q`.

This is the *flushness* statement of §76.7 and is used by both the even-dimensional route
(step 2 above) and by the cut-point step.  The classical proof (Petersen 5.7.12) is:

* if the terminal velocities are *not* opposite, pick `w ∈ T_qM` with `⟨w, σ'(m)⟩ < 0` and
  `⟨w, τ'(m)⟩ < 0` (possible unless `σ'(m) = -τ'(m)`);
* let `q(s) = exp_q(s w)`; by the **inverse function theorem** at `u` and `v` (a *local
  surjectivity* of `GExp p`, available from the **non-conjugacy** of `u`, `v`, which is
  `§78.1`'s `pi_div_sqrt_le_of_isConjugateTime` + the Jacobi-tensor correspondence) lift `q(s)`
  to curves `u(s)`, `v(s)` with `GExp p u(s) = GExp p v(s) = q(s)`;
* the **first variation of the *length* of a family of geodesics with a fixed start and a moving
  endpoint** gives `d/ds ‖u(s)‖|_0 = ⟨w, σ'(m)⟩ < 0`, hence `‖u(s)‖ < m` (and same for `v`);
* `u(s) ≠ v(s)` for small `s`, so `(u(s), v(s))` is a witness pair inside the open ball of radius
  `m`, contradicting `InjAtLeast hmet p m`.  ∎

So the two missing pieces are (a) the *local surjectivity* of `GExp p` at a non-conjugate vector
(chart version of the IFT; the *injectivity* direction is in the commons as
`locally_injective_of_injective_mfderiv`, the surjectivity/inverse-branch direction is not), and
(b) the **length/energy first variation with a moving endpoint**.  The project has the *distance*
version (`FirstVariation.firstVariation_upper`), which gives `d(p, q(s)) < m` but **not**
`‖u(s)‖ < m`; the length version is the missing analytic tool (it is a one-line boundary term for a
family of geodesics, but it is not in the library).

### 78.5 Other gaps in the even-dimensional route (unchanged by this session)

* **Existence of a shortest geodesic loop** (step 1/2): needs Arzelà–Ascoli on arclength-parametrised
  geodesic loops (the commons has `arzela_ascoli_curves`), lower semicontinuity of the length
  (`Commons.Topology.Metric.polygon_sum_le_of_tendstoUniformly`), and the passage
  "uniform limit of unit-speed geodesics is a geodesic" (the chart velocities are bounded and
  equicontinuous, so a subsequence converges uniformly and the geodesic equation is a closed
  condition — a *project-level* lemma).
* **The global injectivity radius** `inj(M)`: `A := {r | InjAllAtLeast hmet r}` is closed and
  downward closed (from `InjAtLeast p r'` for all `r' < r` follows `InjAtLeast p r`), so
  `InjAllAtLeast (sSup A)` holds; but the step "the infimum over `p` is attained at some `p`"
  (Petersen: continuity of `p ↦ inj(p)`) is not available.  A workaround for the *diameter*
  conclusion: with `InjAllAtLeast hmet m` one gets `diam ≥ m` from any point
  (`Klingenberg.ofReal_le_edist_of_injOn_of_forall_le` needs only one point), so the even-dimensional
  theorem in the form `InjAllAtLeast hmet (π/√K)` would suffice.
* **Synge's lemma** (step 3): a closed parallel unit normal field along a closed geodesic in even
  dimension.  Needs (i) parallel transport along a curve as a linear isometry of tangent spaces,
  (ii) its **homotopy invariance** on a simply connected manifold (the determinant of the holonomy
  around a loop is locally constant on the loop space, hence `+1` when `M` is simply connected —
  `ΛM` is connected), or (iii) the disk/product-integral version (a closed curve bounds a disk;
  radial parallel transport trivialises the pullback bundle; the monodromy is a product of
  transports along short arcs, each orientation-preserving).  Either route is a substantial
  infrastructure project.
* **Periodic second variation** (step 4): the project's second-variation machinery is for chart
  families with fixed endpoints; the closed/periodic case (test field periodic and parallel, no
  boundary terms) is a new development, modelled on `GlobalBM`'s glued-parallel-field technique.
* **Lemma 5.7.12, second part** (step 6): "exactly two segments" — the no-third-segment argument
  (`Proposition 1.1` of the USTC lecture notes / Petersen 5.7.12 first part) is *proved* in essence
  by the same fit argument: two segments with a *third* on the way give the shortening, i.e. it is
  a corollary of §78.4 once (a) and (b) are in place.

