`Submission/Commons`, `Submission/Work`) is still the target reduction `hK := by sorry` in
`Submission.lean`.  **NEW this session: `Submission/UniformInj.lean`** (namespace
`Submission.UniformInj`, sorry-free, imported by `Submission.lean`), containing

> **`Submission.UniformInj.exists_pos_injAllAtLeast`** :
> `∃ ρ : ℝ, 0 < ρ ∧ InjAllAtLeast hmet ρ`

i.e. **there is a radius `ρ > 0` such that `GExp x` is injective on the ball of radius `ρ` in
`T_xM`, uniformly in `x ∈ M`.**  `#print axioms` = `propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound`.

### 79.1 The proof, and why it needs *no* curvature hypothesis

The statement is a purely *local* fact about the exponential map (it is true for every Riemannian
metric on a compact manifold, with no curvature assumption).  The route is the standard one —
the exponential map is a local diffeomorphism at the origin of every tangent space, uniformly by
compactness — but it is carried out in the **chart-flow model** of §73/§78, which is what makes the
constants uniform.

For a chart-flow datum `D : ChartFlowData z₀ Z` (§78, `Submission/GExpFlow.lean`) put

  `Hfun D (x, u) = (D.α (x, flowScale D • u) (flowScale D)⁻¹).1 : E`

so that `GExp q (frameVector z₀ q u) = (extChartAt I z₀).symm (Hfun D ((extChartAt I z₀) q, u))`.

* **`contDiffAt_Hfun`** — `Hfun D` is jointly `C^∞` at every phase of `chartData z₀ Z`
  (`D.hjoint` composed with the smooth map `(x,u) ↦ ((x, c·u), c⁻¹)`).
* **`Hfun_zero`** (`Hfun D (x,0) = x`) — the flow of the spray at zero velocity is constant
  (the spray vanishes at zero velocity, `chartSpray_zero`).
* **`hasDerivAt_flowPos`** — the derivative of the flow position in the initial velocity at flow
  time `c⁻¹` is `c⁻¹ • w`.
* **`Hfun_smul_ray`** — the ray identity in the flow model:
  `Hfun D (x, r • u) = (D.α (x, c • u) (r * c⁻¹)).1` for `r` near `0`.  This is
  **homogeneity of the chart geodesic flow** (`ChartFlowHom.chartFlowData_homogeneous`, i.e.
  quadraticity of the spray) — the *only* nontrivial input.
* **`hasDerivAt_Hfun_along`** — hence `d/dr |₀ Hfun D (x, r • u) = u` (chain rule + the spray
  equation at time `0`: the derivative is `c⁻¹ • (c • u) = u`).
* **`fderiv_Hfun_apply_snd`** — therefore
  `(fderiv ℝ (Hfun D) (x, 0)) (0, u) = u`, i.e. **the differential in the velocity variable at zero
  velocity is the identity** (the chart-level `d exp₀ = id`).  The proof composes the
  `HasFDerivAt` of `Hfun D` at `(x,0)` with the curve `r ↦ (x, r • (s·u))` (an arbitrary scale
  `s > 0` chosen so that all the phases stay in the flow domain) and compares with
  `hasDerivAt_Hfun_along` by uniqueness of derivatives; the general `u` follows by linearity of
  `fderiv`.
* **`exists_uniform_injective_Hfun`** — the *uniformity in `x`* comes from the commons lemma
  **`Commons.Analysis.Calculus.exists_uniform_injective_of_hasStrictFDerivAt`** (proved by the
  fleet this session): if `H : E × E → E` is strictly differentiable at every `(x, 0)` with
  `x ∈ K` (`K` compact), with derivative `f' x` there, and `f' x (0,z) = z`, then `H (x, ·)` is
  injective on a ball of radius `ε > 0` **independent of `x ∈ K`**.  The proof is a compactness
  argument: a bad sequence `xₙ → x`, `uₙ ≠ vₙ → 0` with `H (xₙ,uₙ) = H (xₙ,vₙ)` contradicts the
  *joint* strict differentiability at `(x,0)` applied to the **pair** `((xₙ,uₙ), (xₙ,vₙ))`
  (`‖uₙ-vₙ‖ ≤ ½‖uₙ-vₙ‖`).  Note that this uses strict differentiability of the *joint* map — the
  pointwise (in `x`) strict differentiability is **not** enough, which is exactly why the
  compactness is needed.
* **`GExp_eq_symm_Hfun`** — the identification of `GExp` with the chart model, including the zero
  velocity.
* **`injAtLeast_of_chartModel`** — transfer of the chart-model injectivity to `InjAtLeast hmet q r`
  on a *fibre*-norm ball, using `√λ‖w‖ ≤ ‖frameVector z₀ q w‖` (`λ` a chart-metric lower bound) and
  the (extended) chart flow identity.
* **`exists_pos_injAllAtLeast`** — assembly over a finite atlas: the commons lemma
  **`Commons.Geometry.Manifold.exists_finite_cover_isCompact_subset_chartAt`** gives
  `Z : Fin n → Set M` compact with `Z i ⊆ (chartAt H (z i)).source` and `⋃ i, Z i = univ`; for each
  `i` we take the flow datum (`exists_chartFlowData`), the model injectivity radius (`ε i`), the
  chart-metric lower bound (`λ i`, from `exists_chartGram_bounds`) and the phase radius
  (`η i`, from `exists_pos_phase_mem`); the uniform radius is
  `min_i (min (ε i) (η i) * √(λ i))`.

### 79.2 What this buys

* It is the **base case of the global geodesic flow** (§73's "wall"): the iteration of the chart
  flow along a ray can now be restarted at any base point with a *uniform* step.
* It makes the *pointwise* injectivity radius positive *uniformly*, hence `injSup hmet > 0`
  (`Submission/InjSup.lean`), and it is the input for the classical fact that `p ↦ inj(p)` is
  continuous (indeed 1-Lipschitz) — still open.
* It does **not** by itself discharge the cut-point step's hypothesis
  `hloc_sphere : ∀ w, ‖w‖ = inj(p) → LocallyInjectiveAtGExp hmet p w`: `LocallyInjectiveAtGExp`
  is *local injectivity at the point* `w`, whereas what is uniform is injectivity on the ball of
  radius `ρ` around `0`.  The cut-point step still needs the global geodesic flow, exactly as
  §73.4/§78.3 say.

