### 81.6 The precise shape of the remaining gap (analysis, with references)

Reading Xu, *On conjugate points and geodesic loops in a complete Riemannian manifold*,
arXiv:1401.5549 (fetched and read this session), the classical statement that the project still lacks
is ([8,9] = Klingenberg; Xu Theorem B; the "fixed-direction Klingenberg lemma" of §76.7):

> **Klingenberg–Xu.**  Let `x₀` be a point of the cut locus `C_p` at distance `m = d(p, C_p)`
> (the closest cut point, so `m = inj(p)`).  Then either `p` and `x₀` are conjugate to each other
> along *every* minimal geodesic from `p` to `x₀`, or the *two* minimal geodesics from `p` to `x₀`
> form a **closed geodesic loop at `p`** (a whole geodesic, smooth at both ends hence through `x₀`),
> of length `2m`.

Xu's proof (which is what would have to be formalised) is an argument with the **tangential segment
domain** of `exp_p`: the minimal geodesic `γ` from `q` to `x₀` lifts uniquely to `T_pM` in the segment
domain (using Arzelà–Ascoli on the lifts and the nonsingularity of `d exp_p` at the *non-conjugate*
limit vector `w`), unless `γ` glues with a minimal geodesic from `p` to form a whole geodesic
through `x₀`; a local-minimum argument reduces the local to the global case (`F_{p,q}` on `C_p`).

**The clean logical structure of Klingenberg's lemma** `inj(p) ≥ min{conj(p), ½ℓ(p)}` (the "≥" half
of the Cheeger–Ebin structure theorem; this project has the other half, `inj(p) ≤ ½ℓ(p)`, via
`two_mul_le_of_isGeodesicAt_loop` of §81.5) is therefore:

1. let `m := inj(p)` (`InjAtLeast hmet p m` + maximality, `InjRadius.exists_injAtLeast_max`);
2. `Submission.CutStep3.exists_two_minimising_or_not_locallyInjective` (fully proved) gives: either
   *two distinct minimal vectors* `u ≠ v` of norm `m` with the same image `x`, or a *failure of local
   injectivity* of `GExp p` at some `w` with `‖w‖ = m`;
3. in the first case: `d(p,x) = m`, so `x` is a *closest* cut point and **Klingenberg–Xu** gives
   either conjugacy (⟹ `m ≥ π/√K` by `RauchK.pi_div_sqrt_le_of_isConjugateTime`, which is enough for
   `(K2)`), or a closed geodesic loop at `p` of length `2m` (⟹ by definition of `ℓ(p)`, `½ℓ(p) ≤ m`);
4. in the second case one needs `hloc_sphere` (§73.4, §80.4): `GExp p` is locally injective at every
   vector of norm `m` — the "fold", i.e. C¹-smoothness of `v ↦ GExp p v` beyond the injectivity
   radius, which is exactly the part of the global geodesic flow that is still missing
   (see §80.4's annulus/one-step gluing plan, whose base case, joint `C^∞` of the chart flow, and the
   closedness of the geodesic equation under uniform limits are all in the library).

So the remaining gap of the project is exactly the conjunction of (i) `hloc_sphere` and (ii)
Klingenberg–Xu — two statements of comparable depth, both about the *global structure of the
exponential map* rather than about curvature comparisons.

