### 81.8 The "geodesic arc with coinciding endpoints" vs a genuine closed geodesic

`Submission.KlingenbergLemma.IsClosedGeodesicLoopAt hmet p T γ` is deliberately stated with the
**velocity matching** `γ̇(T) = γ̇(0)` (in addition to `0 < T`, `γ 0 = p = γ T`, `C^∞`, geodesy at every
parameter and unit speed).  Without it the predicate would be satisfied by a *geodesic arc whose two
endpoints happen to coincide* — in particular the loop produced by the extremal lens of §81.1,
`t ↦ β((1 - t/ν) • u)`: it is smooth as a curve on `ℝ` and closed, but it descends to a genuine closed
geodesic on the circle `ℝ/Tℤ` only when the ray is **periodic**, i.e. `β̇(1) = β̇(-1)` (equivalently
`β(s + 2) = β(s)` in the `u`-parametrisation).  The lens condition `GExp p u = GExp p (-u)` alone does
not imply that: it says `β(1) = β(-1)` and no more.  This matters because

* the *easy* half of the structure theorem, `inj(p) ≤ ½ℓ(p)`, must be applied to **genuine** closed
  geodesics (that is what `ℓ(p)` counts), and
* the Klingenberg–Xu input of §81.6 produces a loop "smoothly through `x₀`", i.e. with the matching
  at *both* ends, hence a genuine closed geodesic.

Two consequences are formalised in `Submission/KlingenbergLemma.lean`:

* `two_mul_le_of_isClosedGeodesicLoopAt`: `IsClosedGeodesicLoopAt hmet p T γ → InjAtLeast hmet p r →
  2r ≤ T` — the easy half, now stated in terms of the predicate.  Two ingredients: the reflection
  formula `γ̇(T - ·)'(0) = -γ̇(T)` (`curveVelocity_comp_sub_neg_at`) turns the non-reversal hypothesis
  `(T/2)•γ̇(0) ≠ (T/2)•γ̇(T-·)'(0)` of `two_mul_le_of_isGeodesicAt_loop` into `γ̇(0) ≠ 0`, which unit
  speed gives; the rest is the loop lemma with `a := -1`, `b := T + 1`.
* `le_two_mul_of_isClosedGeodesicLoop_lower`: the transference step of §81.7.

For the record, the "anti-matching" case is *impossible*: a unit-speed geodesic on `ℝ` with
`γ 0 = γ T` and `γ̇(T) = -γ̇(0)` would be symmetric about `T/2` (uniqueness of geodesics applied to
`s ↦ γ(T - s)`), forcing `γ̇(T/2) = 0` — this is the same argument as in
`LensLoop.curveVelocity_GExp_ne_neg_of_lens`.

### 81.9 The concrete plan for the remaining two inputs (for the next session)

Both missing inputs (i) `hloc_sphere` and (ii) Klingenberg–Xu are statements about the **geodesic flow
in the initial data**, and both should be attacked through the same object: the *state flow*

  `Φ_t (q, v) := (the point at arclength t along the unit-speed geodesic in the direction of v,
                  and its velocity)`, for `v ≠ 0`,   `Φ_{t+s} = Φ_t ∘ Φ_s`.

**Why this is the right formulation (new observation of this session).**  The "annulus/one-step
gluing" of §80.4 was planned as an induction on the *radius* `r` of the ball in `T_pM`, which forces
the moving-tangent-space bookkeeping at every step.  But the state flow is a *flow in the initial
data* and satisfies the semigroup property `Φ_{t+s} = Φ_t ∘ Φ_s`; hence

> smoothness of `Φ_δ` for a *fixed small* `δ` (uniformly over a compact piece of the state space)
> implies smoothness of `Φ_T` for **every** `T`, by writing `T = 2^k δ` and using
> `Φ_T = Φ_δ^{2^k}` — *for each specific trajectory one only needs `Φ_δ` near the finitely many
> intermediate states along it*.

So the only place where charts, trivialisations and the moving tangent space are needed is the **base
case `Φ_δ`**, which is exactly the chart-flow box the library already has:
`Submission.ChartFlowData` (`hjoint`: joint `C^∞` of the flow in the phase and the time),
`Submission.GExpFlowVelocity.curveVelocity_GExp_chartFlow` (identification of the *state* with the
flow), `Submission.ChartFlowHom.chartFlowData_homogeneous` (homogeneity, needed to convert the flow
time into arclength), `Submission.ChartFlowRoundTrip.chartFlowData_semigroup` (the semigroup property
in the chart), `Submission.ChartFlowJoint.chartFlowExp_joint_contMDiffAt` (joint smoothness), plus the
finite-cover/Lebesgue-number argument of `Submission.UniformInj.exists_pos_injAllAtLeast` for the
**uniformity** over the compact state space.

**Payoff 1 (`hloc_sphere`).**  With `Φ` smooth in the initial state one gets: `v ↦ GExp p v` is `C¹`
at every `v`, its derivative is the solution of the Jacobi equation (the project has the Jacobi
machinery), and hence — by the inverse function theorem —
`GExp p` is a local diffeomorphism at every vector that is not conjugate to `p`.  Together with the
equivalence "`d(GExp p)_w` singular ⇔ the ray of `w` has a conjugate point at arclength `‖w‖`"
(`Submission.ConjPoint`, `Submission.ConjRadius`, `Submission.JacobiTensor`) this replaces
`hloc_sphere` by the *non-conjugacy* of the closest cut point — which is precisely the (Rauch) input
that the Klingenberg estimate consumes: `‖w‖ = inj(p) < π/√K ⟹ p and `GExp p w` are *not* conjugate
(`Submission.RauchK.pi_div_sqrt_le_of_isConjugateTime`).

**Payoff 2 (Klingenberg–Xu).**  The same `C¹`-smoothness of `v ↦ GExp p v` on the ball of radius
`inj(p)` is what Xu's argument needs (`d exp_p` nonsingular at the limit vector, the unique lift of a
minimal geodesic in the tangential segment domain, Arzelà–Ascoli on the lifts) — see
`Submission/Roadmap.lean` §81.6 and Xu, arXiv:1401.5549, §2.

