   `sec ∈ [K/4, K]`, the shortest closed geodesic has length `≥ 2π/√K` (Klingenberg 1961, sharp at
   `ℂPⁿ`/`ℍPⁿ`).  The "thin lens" half reduces to **Rauch II** (the second variation of the distance
   function, i.e. `f'' + Kf ≥ 0` for `f = d(σ,τ)`) plus the Sturm comparison that is already in the
   commons (`Commons.Analysis.ODE.sturm_eqOn_zero_of_second_deriv_add_nonneg`); the "fat"/rotating
   lens half needs Synge (even dimensions; the determinant-sign input is in the commons as
   `Commons.LinearAlgebra.det_pos_forall_or_det_neg_forall`) and — for **odd** dimensions — the
   Morse theory of the free loop space (Palais–Smale condition C, the minimax, and the index estimate
   `ind ≥ n - 1 ≥ 2`), which is not in Mathlib and is the honest long-term wall, as recorded since
   §80.6.
4. The existence (and packaging) of the shortest closed geodesic, i.e. the hypothesis `hℓ` of
   `Submission.KlingenbergLemma.le_two_mul_of_isClosedGeodesicLoop_lower`: a lower bound for the
   lengths of all genuine closed geodesic loops through `p`.

Items 1 and 2 are analytic statements about the *global geodesic flow* (no curvature comparisons);
item 3 mixes curvature comparisons (Rauch II) with global minimaxtheory; item 4 is compactness plus
the loop-space minimax.  This session removed the last *combinatorial* gaps (the loop from an
extremal lens, the Klingenberg inequality, the dichotomy-to-loop transference) and left the remaining
work as pure analysis.

## 82. The doubling step for the smoothness of the exponential map (session 8)

### 82.1 What the wall is, again, and what was decided

Since §73.4/§80.4/§81.9 the bottleneck for `hloc_sphere` (local injectivity of `GExp p` at the
sphere `‖w‖ = inj(p)`) is **C^∞-smoothness of `v ↦ GExp p v` beyond the injectivity radius** — the
"fold".  All other ingredients of that reduction are in the library: `Commons.Geometry.Manifold.
locally_injective_of_injective_mfderiv` (invertible `mfderiv` ⟹ locally injective), the parametric
Rauch theorem (`RauchK.pi_div_sqrt_le_of_isConjugateTime`, `π/√K ≤ conj`), and the Jacobi machinery
relating singular `d exp` to conjugate points (`CutPoint.exists_jacobiTensor_singular_of_...`).

The chosen route (designed in §81.9 and refined here) is the **doubling induction**: prove, by
induction on a radius `R`, that the family
`Ψ_z (q,u) := GExp q (frameVector z q u)` (a map `M × E → M`) is `C^∞` at `(q,u)` whenever
`‖frameVector z q u‖ < R`, *uniformly over the charts `z` of a fixed finite atlas*, and then

  `Ψ_z(q,u) = GExp (midPt) V`,  with  `midPt = GExp q ((‖w‖/2)•(‖w‖⁻¹•w))`,  `w = frameVector z q u`,

so that both the midpoint position and the remaining vector have fibre norm `‖w‖/2 < R`, i.e. both
are in the *known* region; the inductions then gives `R ↦ 2R`.  (Note: the "halving" is essential —
a splitting into two non-equal pieces `θ‖w‖` and `(1-θ)‖w‖` cannot double the radius.)

### 82.2 What is now proved (`Submission/GExpSmooth.lean`, imported by `Submission.lean`)

All declarations are sorry-free (`#print axioms` = `propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound`).

* `GExp_eq_chartFlow_of_phase` — the chart-flow description of `GExp` **without** a hypothesis
  `q ∈ Z` (only `q ∈ source` and the phase in the flow domain `O`); the existing
  `GExp_eq_symm_Hfun` needed `q ∈ Z`, which is *not* enough for the eventual statement of a step.
* `gexpChart hmet z`, `SmoothOnBall hmet z Z R` — the induction statement (per atlas index, in
  *fibre-norm* terms).
* **`exists_pos_smoothOnBall`** — the **base case**: for a finite atlas `(z i, Z i)` with compact
  pieces covering `M`, there is `δ > 0` such that `Ψ_{z i}` is `C^∞` at every `(q,u)` with
  `q ∈ Z i` and `‖frameVector (z i) q u‖ < δ`.  Proof: the chart-flow model
  (`GExpJoint.contMDiffAt_chartFlowExp_joint`) gives smoothness in *model* coordinates on
  `Z i × ball(0, η i)` with `η i = 1/(2 flowScale (D i))`, and the chart-Gram bounds
  (`ChartBounds.exists_chartGram_bounds`) convert `‖frameVector‖ < η i·√(λ i)` into `‖u‖ < η i`;
  the minimum over the finite atlas is `δ`.  (This is the *uniform* version of
  `GExpJoint.contMDiffOn_GExp_frameVector`, whose radius depends on the chart.)
* `GExp_ray_eq_unit_ray`, **`GExp_split`** — the midpoint split, straight from
  `GExpGlob.GExp_add_smul` (with `ξ = ‖w‖⁻¹ • w`, chosen so that everything is *syntactically*
