The assembly of the core step (`contMDiffAt_gexpChart_of_half`), with all the pain points identified
in §82.4, is:

1. `hF`: the midpoint map `p ↦ midPt (z i) p.1 p.2` is `C^∞` at `(q₀,u₀)` — by
   `ContMDiffAt.congr_of_eventuallyEq` against `h1 ∘ (p ↦ (p.1, (1/2)•p.2))`, the pointwise identity
   being `half_smul_inv_smul` (`(‖w‖/2)•(‖w‖⁻¹•w) = (1/2)•w`) + `frameVector_smul'`.
2. `hΛ : ContDiffAt ℝ ∞ (halfFun (z j) (z i)) ((φ_i q₀, u₀), 1/2)` — from `h1` (composed with
   `(x,u,s) ↦ ((φ_i)⁻¹ x, s•u)`) and the chart `φ_j` at `midPt` (via `hjsrc`); here the *domain* of
   `halfFun` is the *normed space* `(E × E) × ℝ` with its **self model** `𝓘(ℝ,(E×E)×ℝ)`, which is
   what makes `fderiv`/`clm_apply` applicable; the components `(x,u,s) ↦ x`, `↦ u`, `↦ s` are the
   `ContinuousLinearMap`s `fst/snd`.
3. `hev`: `halfFun` is differentiable at the relevant points **eventually** — extract
   `ContDiffOn ℝ 2 (halfFun) s` from `hΛ` (`ContDiffAt.contDiffOn`, with the vacuous side condition
   and `m = 2`), shrink to the *interior* (so that `s` is a neighbourhood of each of its points), and
   use `ContDiffOn.contDiffAt` + `ContDiffAt.differentiableAt`.
4. `hW`: the second-half map `p ↦ halfVel (z j) (z i) p.1 p.2` is `C^∞` at `(q₀,u₀)`: the function
   `t ↦ (fderiv ℝ halfFun t) ((0,0),1)` is `C^∞` (from `hΛ.fderiv_right` + `ContDiffAt.clm_apply`
   with the constant `((0,0),1)`), composed with the *self-model* target map
   `p ↦ (((φ_i) p.1, p.2), 1/2)` (built with `ContMDiffAt.prodMk_space` twice) and multiplied by the
   constant `1/2`.
5. `hid`: the eventual identity `Ψ_{z i}(p) = Ψ_{z j}(midPt p, halfVel p)` — `GExp_split` +
   `halfVel_eq_clm` + `frameVector_clm` in the non-degenerate case, both sides equal to `p.1` in the
   case `w = 0` (`halfVel = 0` there because the ray is constant).
6. Compose: `h2.comp (q₀,u₀) (hF.prodMk hW)` and `ContMDiffAt.congr_of_eventuallyEq hid`.

Every step is *syntactically* consistent because `midPt`, `halfVel` and the split lemma are stated
with the same expressions (`(‖w‖/2) • (‖w‖⁻¹ • w)`), so **no transport of tangent vectors along a
point equality is needed anywhere** — this was the main design decision of the session (§82.2).

The two "model-bridging" lemmas (self model vs product model for products of normed spaces) turned out
to be unnecessary: `ContMDiffAt.prodMk_space` produces the *self* model of the product directly, and
using it twice gives the self model of `(E×E)×ℝ`; the remaining mismatches were resolved by choosing
the domain of `halfFun` to be `(E×E)×ℝ` (matching the associativity of the tuple).

### 82.3bis What this unblocks, and the *next* precise gap

With `exists_contMDiffAt_GExp` in hand, `LocallyInjectiveAtGExp hmet p w` can be attacked by the
inverse function theorem (`Commons.Geometry.Manifold.locally_injective_of_injective_mfderiv`: an
invertible `mfderiv` plus `C¹` gives local injectivity).  What is still missing for `hloc_sphere` in
the case `m < π/√K` is therefore the **global** form of the classical equivalence

> `d(GExp p)_w` singular  ⟺  the ray `t ↦ GExp p (t • w)` has a conjugate point at time `‖w‖`,

whose `⇐` direction the library already has in the *chart* form (`CutPoint.exists_jacobiTensor_singular_of_mfderiv_eq_zero`,
`RauchK.eqOn_zero_of_jacobi_of_curvLe`).  The `⇒` direction is the standard variation argument:

> if `ξ ≠ 0` is in the kernel of `d(GExp p)_w`, the 2-parameter family `(t,s) ↦ GExp p (t • (w + s•ξ))`
> has an `s`-variation field `J` which is a non-trivial Jacobi field along the ray vanishing at
> `t = 0` and `t = 1` (in the `w`-parametrisation), because `J(1) = d(GExp p)_w ξ = 0` and
> `J'(0) = ξ ≠ 0` (the latter being the global form of `d exp_0 = id`, available as
> `mfderiv_expMap_zero` for the chart exponential and transferable by `exists_pos_GExp_eq_expMap`).

So the next concrete target is `IsConjugateTime_of_mfderiv_singular`: a singular `d(GExp p)_w` produces
`IsConjugateTime cov (ray) 1` (equivalently at time `‖w‖` in the unit parametrisation), whence
`RauchK.pi_div_sqrt_le_of_isConjugateTime` gives `π/√K ≤ ‖w‖` — which is exactly the exclusion of the
merging branch of the dichotomy below the conjugate radius, i.e. **`hloc_sphere` for `m < π/√K`**.

### 82.4 Gotchas found this session (they cost real time)

* **`ContMDiffAt.congr_of_eventuallyEq` has the conclusion function on the *left* of the eventual
  equality**: `h.congr_of_eventuallyEq (H : f₁ =ᶠ f) : ContMDiffAt f₁ x` when `h : ContMDiffAt f x`.
  Using `.symm` in the wrong place gives a confusing type mismatch.
* `ContMDiffAt.prodMk` gives the *product* model `I'.prod J'` for the target, while
  `ContMDiffAt.prodMk_space` gives the *self* model `𝓘(𝕜, E' × F')` — for targets that are products
  of normed spaces one must use `prodMk_space`.
* `ContMDiffAt.fderiv` is *not* the lemma one expects for `t ↦ (fderiv f t)(v)`: it is about
  `Function.uncurry`.  The chain
  `ContDiffAt.fderiv` → `ContDiffAt.clm_apply` (with the constant `v`) is the working one.
* `ContDiffAt.contDiffOn` requires `m ≤ n` with `m ≠ ∞` *and* the side condition
  `m = ∞ → n = ω`; for `m = 2, n = ∞` the side condition is vacuous and the `m ≤ n` proof in
  `ℕ∞ω` is `WithTop.coe_le_coe.mpr le_top` (the `∞` of `ℕ∞ω` is *not* the top `ω`, so `le_top`
  does not apply directly, and `norm_num` cannot see it).
* The smoothness order notation: `(⊤ : ℕ∞ω) = ω` (analyticity), whereas `C^∞` is the element `∞`,
  which is the coercion of `⊤ : ℕ∞`.  `(2 : ℕ∞ω) ≠ ∞` requires `WithTop.coe_inj` +
  `ENat.natCast_ne_top`.
* Setting a triple as `E × E × ℝ` (right-nested) and then writing `(x, u, s)` gives the *wrong*
  nesting for a target `(E × E) × ℝ`; keep the domain type and the tuple shape in sync.
* `contMDiffOn_extChartAt (x) hx` is stated at the *centre* `x`, not at an arbitrary source point;
  the useful form is `(contMDiffOn_extChartAt (I := I) (x := z) (n := ∞)).contMDiffAt
  ((chartAt H z).open_source.mem_nhds hq)`.
* As always: finish with `lake build Submission.<File>` (autoImplicit is off in the lakefile but
  `lake env lean` does not apply the lakefile options).

### 82.5 The fleet requests that turned out to be unnecessary

The two "model-bridging" lemmas of the earlier draft of this section (self model vs product model
for products of normed spaces) were **not** needed in the end: `ContMDiffAt.prodMk_space` already
produces the *self* model of a product of normed spaces, and applying it twice gives the self model
of `(E × E) × ℝ`; the remaining mismatches were removed by choosing the domain type of `halfFun` to
be `(E × E) × ℝ` (so that the tuple nesting agrees with the type nesting).  No fleet requests are
outstanding from this session.

## 83. From the injectivity of the differential to `hloc_sphere` (the IFT half)

### 83.1 What is now proved (`Submission/GExpLocalInj.lean`, imported)

With `exists_contMDiffAt_GExp` (the `C^∞` theorem of §82) in hand, the two directions linking the
*local injectivity* of `GExp p` with the *injectivity of its differential* are now in the library,
both sorry-free (`#print axioms` = `propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound`):

* `contMDiffAt_GExp_one` — the exponential map is `C¹` at every tangent vector (the `1`-smoothness
  used by the inverse function theorem);
* **`locallyInjectiveAtGExp_of_injective_mfderiv`** — an *injective* differential at `v` makes
  `GExp p` locally injective at `v` (`LocallyInjectiveAtGExp hmet p v`, the hypothesis of the
  cut-point step), via `Commons.Geometry.Manifold.locally_injective_of_injective_mfderiv`.  The
  extra work here is the *norm* bookkeeping: the commons lemma gives its `ε`-neighbourhood in the
  **model** norm of `E`, while `LocallyInjectiveAtGExp` is stated in the **fibre** (Riemannian)
  norm; the comparison `exists_model_norm_le_fibre_div` (`Submission/GExpRay2`) supplies the
  conversion, giving the fibre ball of radius `ε·√λ`;
* **`not_injective_mfderiv_of_not_locallyInjective`** — the contrapositive, i.e. **the merging
  branch of the cut-point dichotomy forces a non-injective differential**;
* `not_isInvertible_of_not_injective` — a non-injective differential is not invertible;
* **`locallyInjectiveAtGExp_of_lt_pi_div_sqrt`** — the *reduction*: given the classical statement

  > **(C)** `∀ q, ∀ w ≠ 0, ¬ (d GExp_q)_w` invertible ⟹ `π/√K ≤ ‖w‖`  (a singular differential is
  > a conjugate vector)

  then `LocallyInjectiveAtGExp hmet p v` holds for every `v ≠ 0` with `‖v‖ < π/√K`.  So (C) is the
  *single* remaining input for `hloc_sphere` in the case `m < π/√K`, and with it the merging branch
  of `CutStep3.exists_two_minimising_or_not_locallyInjective` is excluded and the Klingenberg
  transference step `KlingenbergLemma.le_two_mul_of_isClosedGeodesicLoop_lower` fires in all cases.

The proof of the reduction is three lines: `¬LocallyInjectiveAtGExp` ⟹ (contrapositive of the first
item) `¬Injective (mfderiv …)` ⟹ `¬IsInvertible` ⟹ (C) `π/√K ≤ ‖v‖`, contradicting `‖v‖ < π/√K`
(the case `v = 0` is taken care of by the hypothesis `v ≠ 0`; in the intended application `v = w`
has `‖w‖ = m = inj(p) > 0`).

### 83.2 The remaining wall, in its final form: **(C)** = `IsConjugateTime_of_mfderiv_singular`

Everything is now reduced to: *a singular (equivalently, non-injective) differential of
`GExp q` at `w ≠ 0` produces a conjugate vector, i.e. `π/√K ≤ ‖w‖`* — via the classical variation
argument.  The precise plan (all ingredients located):

1. **The variation field.**  With `ξ₀ := ‖w‖⁻¹ • w` (unit) and `ξ ∈ ker (d GExp_p)_w`
   (`ξ ≠ 0`), put
   `γ(s) := GExp p (s • ξ₀)` and `J(s) := mfderiv (fun r : ℝ => GExp p (s • (ξ₀ + r • ξ))) 0 1`.
2. **The endpoint conditions.**  `J 0 = 0` (since `GExp p 0 = p` for every `r`), and
   `J ‖w‖ = (d GExp_p)_w (‖w‖ • ξ) = 0` by the singularity and the chain rule (`‖w‖ • (ξ₀ + rξ) =
   w + r • (‖w‖ξ)`).
3. **Non-triviality — DONE.**  `mfderiv_GExp_zero` (landed this session, `Submission/GExpLocalInj.lean`):
   `mfderiv 𝓘(ℝ,E) I (fun v : E ↦ GExp q (v : T_qM)) 0 = ContinuousLinearMap.id ℝ E`, obtained from
   the eventual agreement `exists_pos_GExp_eq_expMap` + `Filter.EventuallyEq.mfderiv_eq` +
   `Geodesic.mfderiv_expMap_zero`.  The only friction was the fibre-vs-model ball: the agreement
   needs `‖v‖_fibre < ρ` while one naturally writes a **model** ball, and the available comparison
   `exists_model_norm_le_fibre_div` bounds the model norm *by* the fibre norm (the wrong way); the
   missing companion is supplied here from the *other* half of `exists_chartGram_bounds`
   (`chartGram x₀ x₀ a a ≤ Λ‖a‖²`), giving `‖v‖_fibre ≤ √(max Λ 1)·‖v‖_model` and hence the model
   ball of radius `ρ/√(max Λ 1)`.  Consequently `J'(0) = ξ ≠ 0`, so the variation field is not
   identically zero.

4. **Jacobi-ness — the wall.**  `s ↦ J s` must be shown to satisfy the Jacobi equation in the
   germ-level sense `IsJacobiAt cov γ J s`.  The library has the *local* statement
   (`Submission/FlowJacobi.lean`: `variationField_eq_jacobiTensor`, the `s`-variation field of the
   chart geodesic flow is the Jacobi tensor column, valid near the base point and for velocities in
   a small ball) and the *consumer*
   (`RauchK.eqOn_zero_of_jacobi_of_curvLe`, the interval form of Rauch's theorem: a Jacobi field on
   `(a,b) ⊇ [0,t₁]` vanishing at `0` and `t₁ < π/√K` vanishes on `[0,t₁]`).  What is missing is the
   **globalisation of the variation argument along the ray**: for each `t₀` one needs a chart around
   `γ(t₀)` in which the *family* `(s,r) ↦ GExp p (s(ξ₀ + rξ))` is a family of chart geodesics, and
   the `r`-variation field satisfies the chart Jacobi system — the same "V1/V2" computation as
   `FlowJacobi`, but for a family that does **not** emanate from the chart centre (the chart must be
   taken at a point *along* the ray).  Two viable formalisations:
   * *generalise* `variationField_eq_jacobiTensor` to arbitrary smooth families of chart geodesics
     (the proof structure is unchanged: the Jacobi system is linear, and it is enough that the
     family solves the geodesic equation in `s` and that the second-derivative symmetry Clairaut step
     applies); then glue along a finite chart cover of the compact ray `γ([0,T])`;
   * or bootstrap: the Jacobi property holds near `0` by `FlowJacobi` (where `GExp = expMap`), the
     set where it holds is open (germ property), and one shows it is also *closed* by re-solving the
     linear Jacobi ODE from the data at the last point.
### 83.3 The V1/V2 computation is *already* initial-condition-free (finding of this session)

Reading `Submission/FlowJacobi.lean` closely: the three lemmas that perform the V1/V2 computation

* `hasDerivAt_variation` — `Yp Φ` has derivative `Zp Φ` (Clairaut's theorem, `hasDerivAt_deriv_partial_swap`),
* `hasDerivAt_variation_vel` — `Zp Φ` has derivative `deriv (fun t ↦ chartGeodesicVectorField x₀ (Φ (s,t))) 0`,
* `deriv_chartGeodesicVectorField` — the value of that derivative, by the linearisation identity,

take as hypotheses only `hδ`, `hTGT`, `hODE`, `hSM` — i.e. **only** that `Φ` is, near the relevant
times, a jointly smooth family of *chart geodesics* whose positions stay in the chart target.  The
initial conditions `hΦ0 : (Φ (0,t)).1 = (extChartAt I x₀) x₀` and `hΦv : (Φ (0,t)).2 = v + t • x`
are used *only afterwards*, in the final uniqueness step that identifies the solution of the linear
system with the column `D.Y · x` of the Jacobi tensor (`Commons.Analysis.ODE.exists_linearODE_solution_Icc`).

**Consequence for the wall**: the block `hpair2` inside `variationField_eq_jacobiTensor` (which
shows `HasDerivAt (fun r ↦ (Yp Φ r, Ufun Φ r)) (jacobiSysVec … ) t`) can be extracted verbatim as a
standalone lemma

> `variationField_satisfies_jacobiSys` : for an arbitrary smooth family `Φ` of chart geodesics
> (hypotheses `hδ, hTGT, hODE, hSM` only), the pair `(Yp Φ, Ufun Φ)` solves the chart Jacobi system
> on `Icc (-1) 1`.

with no hypothesis tying the family to the chart centre.  That is exactly what the globalisation
needs: for a chart at a point `γ(t₀)` *along* the ray, the family
`(s,r) ↦ (coords of GExp p (s(ξ₀ + rξ)))` restricted to a neighbourhood of `t₀` satisfies
`hδ, hTGT, hODE, hSM`, so the extracted lemma gives the chart Jacobi equation for its `r`-variation
field — and hence `IsJacobiAt` for `J` at `t₀`, by `RauchGen`'s packaging (the same construction
used at `Submission/RauchGen.lean:1215`, with `D` replaced by the pair produced by this lemma, whose
initial data are whatever the family dictates at the left edge of the chart interval).

So the remaining work is genuinely *packaging and gluing*, not new mathematics: (i) extract the
lemma (a verbatim move of an existing proof block), (ii) instantiate it on the chart intervals of a
finite cover of `γ([0,‖w‖])`, (iii) glue the resulting germ-level Jacobi assertions into
`∀ s ∈ Ioo a b, IsJacobiAt cov γ J s` for some `(a,b) ⊇ [0,‖w‖]` (either by the open-and-closed
argument or by a finite induction over the cover), and (iv) apply the interval form of Rauch
(`RauchK.eqOn_zero_of_jacobi_of_curvLe`) to conclude `π/√K ≤ ‖w‖`, i.e. (C).

5. **Conclusion.**  With (1)–(4), the contrapositive of `RauchK.eqOn_zero_of_jacobi_of_curvLe`
   gives `π/√K ≤ ‖w‖`, i.e. (C); combined with `locallyInjectiveAtGExp_of_lt_pi_div_sqrt` this
   closes `hloc_sphere` for `m < π/√K` and hence the merging branch.

### 83.4 Step (i) is done: `variationField_satisfies_jacobiSys` (`Submission/VariationJacobi.lean`, imported)

