available; keeping it out of the matching lemma keeps that lemma about linearity and the ODE only —
which is what makes it reusable in charts at points along the ray, where no chart geodesic-emanating
from the centre exists.

With `variationField_satisfies_jacobiSys` (step (i), §83.4) and this matching lemma, step (ii) is now
purely: instantiate on the chart intervals of a finite cover of `γ([0,‖w‖])`, derive `M` on each,
match the pair produced by (i) against the fundamental solutions, and read off `IsJacobiAt`.



## 84. SESSION: **STEP (ii) IS DONE — THE VARIATION FIELD OF THE RAY IS A JACOBI FIELD** (`Submission/RayJacobi.lean`)

### 84.1 The result, and why it is the missing half of (C)

The wall of §82.3bis/§83 was the *globalisation of the variation argument*: for a singular
`(d GExp_p)_w` one must produce a nontrivial Jacobi field along the whole ray with two zeros, and the
Jacobi property is only available chart by chart.  This session closes that gap *locally at every
point at once*, hence globally by the pointwise nature of `IsJacobiAt`:

> **`Submission.RayJacobi.isJacobiAt_GExp_variation`** (sorry-free, imported by `Submission.lean`,
> `#print axioms` = `propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound`):
> for a **unit** vector `u` (`hu : u ≠ 0`, `hu1 : ‖u‖ = 1`), any `η` and any real `t₀`,
> ```
> IsJacobiAt cov (fun s => GExp p (s • u))
>   (fun s => curveVelocity (I := I) (fun r => GExp p (s • (u + r • η))) 0) t₀
> ```
> i.e. **the `r`-variation field of the family of rays `r ↦ GExp p (σ • (u + r•η))` is a Jacobi
> field along the ray `σ ↦ GExp p (σ • u)`, at *every* time `t₀` — with no hypothesis on the size
> of `t₀` and no hypothesis on the chart.**

Since `IsJacobiAt` is a **pointwise (germ) property**, there is **no gluing problem at all**: applying
the lemma at each `t` separately gives `∀ t ∈ Ioo a b, IsJacobiAt cov γ J t` for any `a < b`.  (This
is the observation that makes §83.2's items (ii)/(iii) collapse into a single local statement.)

### 84.2 The one new technical device: a *recentred* extraction lemma

`Submission/FlowJacobiGen.lean` (new, imported by `RayJacobi`, sorry-free) is a copy of
`FlowJacobi`'s `Variation` section and of `VariationJacobi.variationField_satisfies_jacobiSys` in
which the **fixed** windows are replaced by windows **centred at an arbitrary real `c`**:

* `tgt_eventually_gen`, `hasDerivAt_variation_gen`, `hasDerivAt_variation_vel_gen`,
  `deriv_chartGeodesicVectorField_gen` — the four lemmas of `FlowJacobi`'s V1/V2 computation with
  hypotheses `∀ s ∈ Ioo (c - R) (c + R), …`;
* **`variationField_satisfies_jacobiSys_gen`** — the extraction, with conclusion at every
  `t ∈ Icc (c - R/2) (c + R/2)`.

**Why recentring is essential** (and this was the whole difficulty): `variationField_satisfies_
jacobiSys` demands the *chart ODE* for the family on the fixed window `Ioo (-2) 2`.  A chart taken at
`γ t₀` hosts the ray only on some unknown `Ioo (t₀ - ε) (t₀ + ε)`, and `ε` can be tiny, so the family
must be read **in the chart's own time coordinate**: this is precisely a *recentred* window.  The
alternative — rescaling time by `λ = ε/4` — forces the velocity component of the family to be scaled
as well, and then the extracted Jacobi system is the `λ`-system, whose conversion back to the
unscaled system is pure algebra but ~100 lines of `gammaOp`/`curvOp` scaling lemmas
(`gammaOp` is linear in the velocity, `chartCurv` is *quadratic* in it: `chartCurv_smul_snd`).  The
recentring costs four one-line hypothesis changes and avoids all of that.

### 84.3 The proof of `isJacobiAt_GExp_variation` (map of the file)

1. `exists_window_GExp_family` — **the product box**: by joint smoothness of
   `(σ,r) ↦ GExp p (σ • (u + r•η))` (`contMDiffAt_GExp_family`, from
   `exists_contMDiffAt_GExp` composed with `(σ,r) ↦ σ • (u + r•η)`) there are `R, δ > 0` with
   `GExp p (s • (u + r•η)) ∈ (chartAt H x₀).source` for `s ∈ Ioo (t₀-R) (t₀+R)`, `r ∈ Ioo (-δ) δ`
   (extracted from `Metric.mem_nhds_iff` via `Prod.dist_eq`, because `filter_upwards` does not see a
   product filter here).
2. `δ₀ := min δ (‖u‖ / (2(‖η‖+1)))` — **the shrinking that makes the velocities non-zero**: on
   `Ioo (-δ₀) δ₀` one has `u + t•η ≠ 0` (reverse triangle inequality), which is what
   `hasDerivAt_deriv_chartCurve_of_isGeodesicAt` and `curveVelocity_GExp_ne_zero` need.
3. The chart data `Ψ, ugeo, wgeo, y` and the **unshifted** family
   `Φ (s,r) = (Ψ (s,r), deriv (fun x => Ψ (x,r)) s)`; the three hypotheses `hTGT`, `hODE`, `hSM`:
   * `hODE` at `(t,s)` is `HasDerivAt (fun s' => Ψ (s',t)) … s` **plus**
     `hasDerivAt_deriv_chartCurve_of_isGeodesicAt` applied to the `t`-th ray (the ray
     `σ ↦ GExp p (σ • (u + t•η))` is a geodesic at *every* `σ` by `GExpRayZero.isGeodesicAt_GExp_
     smul_all`, and stays in the chart by the box);
   * `hSM` is `contDiffAt_chartFamily` (joint smoothness in the fixed chart, from
