### 87.5 **Klingenberg's lemma, read from the literature** (arXiv:1401.5549, S. Xu)

The precise classical statement (Xu's Lemma 1.1, attributed to Klingenberg 1959/1995):

> If `x₀ ∈ C_p` satisfies `d(p,x₀) = d(p, C_p)` (the **minimum** of the distance to the cut locus),
> then either there is a minimal geodesic from `p` to `x₀` along which they are conjugate, or there
> exist **exactly two** minimal geodesics from `p` to `x₀` that form a geodesic loop at `p`
> **smoothly passing through `x₀`**.

Xu's Theorem B (the sharpened form) adds: if `d(p,·)` fails to be minimal at `x₀` only locally in
`C_p`, one still gets two minimal geodesics forming a whole geodesic through `x₀`, provided *some*
minimal geodesic `p → x₀` is conjugate-free; and **"if moreover `d(p,x₀)` is also a local minimum of
`d(x₀,·)` in `C_{x₀}`, then the two minimal geodesics form a closed geodesic"**.

**The proof method** (this is the wall, and it is worth recording exactly): the *tangential segment
domain* `D̃_p = {t v : 0 ≤ t < σ(v)}` (where `exp_p` is injective) is the domain of a *diffeomorphism*
onto `M \ C_p`; a minimal geodesic `γ` from `q` to `x₀` whose interior avoids `C_p` **lifts** uniquely
to `D̃_p`; the endpoint of the lift is a point `w_v = d(p,x₀) · v` for each unit direction `v` of a
minimal geodesic `p → x₀`, and the lift family is an equi-Lipschitz family (its distance to the
*conjugate locus* `J̃_p` is bounded below, since the differential is invertible at `w_v`), so
Arzelà–Ascoli applies; two different lifts with different endpoints contradict the uniqueness of the
lift, forcing the two minimal geodesics to be the two halves of one whole geodesic.  **Everything in
this proof is about the *global* structure of `exp_p` beyond the injectivity radius** — i.e. exactly
the "fold" of §73.4/§80.4.  Formalising it requires: (i) `D̃_p` is open and `exp_p` is a diffeo from
`D̃_p` onto `M \ C_p`; (ii) unique path lifting for that diffeo; (iii) Arzelà–Ascoli in `T_pM` with
the metric; (iv) the conjugate-locus distance bound (invertibility of `d exp_p` at the lifted
endpoint).  (i)+(ii) are the hard ones: they need the *cut locus* as a set and the theorem that
`exp_p` restricted to `D̃_p` is injective with open image.

### 87.6 **CAVEAT: `IsClosedGeodesicLoopAt` and velocity matching at the base point**

`KlingenbergLemma.IsClosedGeodesicLoopAt hmet p T γ` requires `curveVelocity γ T = curveVelocity γ 0`
(velocity matching at `p`), while Xu's/Lemma 1.1 conclusion is a **geodesic loop at `p` smoothly
passing through `x₀`**: with `γ₁, γ₂` the two minimal geodesics `p → x₀` of length `m = d(p,x₀)`,
"smooth through `x₀`" means `γ₁'(m) = -γ₂'(m)`, which as one computes (both are geodesics with the
same endpoints and length) is *equivalent* to `γ₂(t) = γ₁(2m - t)`, hence to
`γ₁(2m) = p` **and** `γ₁'(2m) = -γ₂'(0)`; the concatenation `σ := γ₁ * γ₂⁻¹ : [0,2m] → M` is then a
single geodesic with `σ(0) = σ(2m) = p`, i.e. a *geodesic loop* at `p` in the modern ("value-closed")
sense.  Its velocity matching at `0 ≡ 2m` is `γ₁'(0) = -γ₂'(0)`, i.e. `v = -u`, which is
**not automatic**; Xu's Theorem B needs the extra hypothesis that `d(p, x₀)` is also a local minimum
of `d(x₀,·)` in `C_{x₀}` to get a genuine (velocity-matched) closed geodesic.  Consequently the
`hXu` input of `KlingenbergLemma.le_two_mul_of_isClosedGeodesicLoop_lower`, as currently stated,
is **not** what Klingenberg's lemma delivers verbatim, and the final assembly of `(K2)` has an
architectural open question: either
* (a) the transfer lemma should be restated for **geodesic loops** (`γ 0 = γ T`, `γ` a geodesic on
  `[0,T]`, no velocity condition), with the deep theorem needed in the corresponding loop form; or
* (b) one proves the extra "both-sides minimality" hypothesis for the closest cut point of a
  pinched simply connected manifold (this is a *strict-pinching* statement: the Berger/ℂPⁿ
  counterexamples are at the boundary `sec = 4`); or
* (c) one proves that a *shortest* geodesic loop at `p` is automatically velocity-matched (this is
  what the classical statement "`inj(p) = min{conj(p), ½ · (shortest closed geodesic through p)}`"
  presumes, and it is exactly a corner-cutting/shortening statement, for which §87.1 and §87.2 are
  the two quantitative inputs — but the "shortening gives a *closed geodesic*" step needs the
  free-loop minimax when the loop is null-homotopic).

This is the sharpest statement of what is missing that we have; whoever continues should decide
between (a), (b), (c) **before** investing in the lift theory of §87.5.

### 87.4b `Submission/Shorten.lean` — **cutting the corner of a lens loop**

The classical shortening lemma, in the exact form the Klingenberg ladder consumes.  Namespace
`Submission.Shorten`:

* `curveLength_GExp_ray_all` — the (already known) length of a `GExp`-ray, now also for the
  degenerate direction `v = 0` (so that no case split is needed downstream).
* **`curveLength_corner_loop`** — the length of a closed *broken* geodesic loop `β` which coincides
  with the ray of `u₁` on `[0,ε]`, with the ray of `u₂` run backwards on `[T-ε,T]`, and has unit
  speed in between, is exactly `T` (the two ray-pieces contribute `ε` each).  Proved by reducing the
  integral of the speed to the integral of the constant `1` via
  `intervalIntegral.integral_congr_ae` (the pointwise equality holds on `(0,T)`; the exceptional set
  is contained in `{0,T}`, which is null) — this is the *a.e.* idiom to use when a curve's speed is
  only known off the two joining points.
* **`exists_shorter_loop_of_lt`** — **the corner can be cut**.  For unit `ξ, η` at `p`, `0 < ε < m`,
  and the *strict* inequality
  `d (GExp p ((m-ε) • ξ)) (GExp p ((m-ε) • η)) < 2ε` (which is what
  `CornerStrict.edist_GExp_lt_of_unit_ne_neg` delivers: the corner at `q` between the two rays is not
  already straight), there is a **closed piecewise smooth path at `p` of length `< 2m`**:
  ```
  PwPath with three pieces:  [0, m-ε] on the ξ-ray  →  [0,1] on the minimising geodesic A' → B'  →  [0, m-ε] on the η-ray run backwards
  ```
  (`Submission.Distance.PwPath`, so that `P.length` is a sum of three interval integrals that are all
  computable from `curveLength_GExp_ray_all`).  The hypothesis is stated as the strict inequality
  rather than as an inequality between the two *velocities* at `q`, because those velocities live in
  `TangentSpace I (GExp p (m • ξ))` and `TangentSpace I (GExp p (m • η))`, which are only
  propositionally equal — this is the same fibre/bookkeeping phenomenon as in §87.2, and it is the
  reason the "genuine corner" condition is not simply `η ≠ -ξ` (see §87.6 (c)).

### 87.5b `Submission/InjBall.lean` — **geodesic polar coordinates on the injectivity ball**

`InjAtLeast hmet p m` says `GExp p` is injective on the *closed* ball of radius `m`; that is only a
lower bound on the injectivity radius.  The companion *onto* statement was missing.  Namespace
`Submission.InjBall`:

* **`exists_tangent_of_edist_lt`** — every `y` with `d(p,y) < m` is `GExp p v` for some `v` with
  `‖v‖ < m` (from Hopf–Rinow's minimising geodesic `HopfRinow.exists_geodesic_riemannianEDist_eq`
  and `ENNReal.ofReal_lt_ofReal_iff_of_nonneg`);
* **`exists_unique_tangent_of_edist_lt`** — the same with uniqueness: **every point of the open
  `m`-ball around `p` is `GExp p v` for a *unique* `v` of norm `< m`**;
* **`ofReal_norm_eq_edist_of_GExp_eq`** — on the injectivity ball `GExp p` **preserves the distance
  from the base point**: `‖v‖ < m` ⟹ `d(p, GExp p v) = ‖v‖`.  (This is the "the vector is realised
  by the minimising geodesic" statement; the proof is `exists_geodesic_riemannianEDist_eq` for
  `y = GExp p v` + `CutWitness.edist_GExp_le_norm` to put the Hopf–Rinow vector `w` in the ball +
  injectivity to conclude `w = v`.)  Note this *subsumes* `GExpRay2.edist_GExp_eq_norm_of_le_of_injAtLeast`
  in the strict-inequality form.
* **`bijOn_GExp_ball`** — `GExp p` is a **bijection** from `{v | ‖v‖ < m}` onto
  `{y | d(p,y) < ofReal m}` (`Set.BijOn`), i.e. geodesic polar coordinates are well defined on the
  injectivity ball.

### 87.6b `Submission/KlingenbergCore.lean` — **`(K2)` reduces to exactly TWO classical inputs**

`KlingenbergCore.diameter_gt_half_pi_of_klingenberg`: for a compact `M` with `sec ≤ K`, `0 < K < 4`,
**if**

* `hXu` — the **Klingenberg–Xu step**: at a point `p` whose *maximal* injectivity radius `m`
  satisfies `m < π/√K`, two distinct minimising vectors `u ≠ v` of norm `m` with the same image
  produce a **closed geodesic loop through `p` of length `≤ 2m`**; and
* `hdeep` — **Klingenberg's theorem** in the form "every closed geodesic loop at every point has
  length `≥ 2π/√K`" (the free-loop-space Morse-theoretic input),

**then some pair of points is at distance `> π/2`.**

The proof is a two-case argument on the maximal injectivity radius `m` of an arbitrary point `p`:
if `π/√K ≤ m` we are done by `KlingenbergStep.exists_edist_gt_half_pi_of_injAtLeast`; if `m < π/√K`
then `RayConj.exists_two_minimising_of_injAtLeast_of_lt_pi_div_sqrt` produces the two minimising
vectors (this step **needs no `hloc`**: below `π/√K` local injectivity is `RayConj`'s theorem),
`hXu` turns them into a closed geodesic loop of length `T ≤ 2m`, and `hdeep` gives
`2π/√K ≤ T ≤ 2m`, contradicting `m < π/√K`.  The maximal `m` is produced by
`InjRadius.exists_injAtLeast_max` from the uniform radius `UniformInj.exists_pos_injAllAtLeast` and
the failure of injectivity beyond the diameter (`InjSup.not_injAtLeast_of_lt_diam` +
`CutTime.exists_edist_le`).

**Two consequences for the architecture.**

1. **The remaining gap of `Klingenberg.KlingenbergBound` is precisely `hXu` + `hdeep`** — nothing
   else.  Everything else in the ladder (Rauch's conjugate-radius bound, the cut-point dichotomy
   below the conjugate radius, the uniform injectivity radius, the strict corner estimate and the
   shortening lemma, geodesic polar coordinates, `diam ≥ inj`) is *proved*.
2. **`hloc` is not needed** for `(K2)`.  This corrects the impression given by
   `KlingenbergLemma.le_two_mul_of_isClosedGeodesicLoop_lower`, whose hypothesis
   `hloc : ∀ w, ‖w‖ = m → LocallyInjectiveAtGExp hmet p w` is *false* in general at a maximal radius:
   on `Sⁿ` with `m = π` the exponential map at `p` is *not* locally injective at the vectors of norm
   `π` (they all map to the antipode).  The case `m ≥ π/√K` must be handled by the diameter bound
   (as `KlingenbergCore` does), not by the transfer lemma; alternatively the transfer lemma should be
   restated with `hloc` replaced by "`m < π/√K`".

