### 88.4 The two walls, honestly stated

1. **`hdeep`** (`ℓ ≥ 2π/√K` on a simply connected manifold with `sec ∈ [K/4,K]`, Klingenberg 1961):
   the classical proof is the Morse theory of the **free loop space** — Palais–Smale condition (C),
   the minimax, Bott's index estimates and the index inequality `ind ≥ n-1 ≥ 2` in odd dimensions.
   *Nothing of this is in Mathlib*, and it is the genuinely deep input of the theorem.  (For
   *even* dimensions the classical argument replaces part of it by Synge's holonomy argument — for
   which the linear-algebraic input
   `Commons.LinearAlgebra.det_pos_forall_or_det_neg_forall` is already in the commons — but the
   free-loop minimax is still needed.)
2. **`hXu`** (Klingenberg's lemma).  The classical proof (Xu, arXiv:1401.5549, Lemma 1.1 and
   Theorem B) lifts the minimal geodesics into the **tangential segment domain** `D̃_p` (the maximal
   domain on which `exp_p` is injective), where `exp_p` is a diffeomorphism onto `M ∖ C_p`, and runs
   an Arzelà–Ascoli argument on the lifts.  Formalising it needs the cut locus as a set, the
   diffeomorphism property of `exp_p` on `D̃_p`, and unique path lifting — i.e. the *global*
   structure of the exponential map beyond the injectivity radius (the "fold" of §73.4/§80.4).
   The **shortening half** of `hXu` is now proved (`Shorten.exists_shorter_loop_of_lt`); the
   *lift* half is the wall.  §87.6 records a second, independent subtlety: Klingenberg's lemma
   produces a *geodesic loop* (value-closed) and a genuine *closed geodesic* only under an extra
   hypothesis (Xu, Theorem B).

