## 89. SESSION: **THE TANGENTIAL SEGMENT DOMAIN** (`Submission/SegmentDomain.lean`)

The first piece of Xu's proof of Klingenberg's lemma (arXiv:1401.5549), §1–§2: the *tangential
segment domain* `D̃_p = {t v : 0 ≤ t < σ(v), ‖v‖ = 1}` and the fact that **`exp_p` is injective on
it** — "we are able to lift `γ` to `(exp_p|D̃_p)⁻¹ ∘ γ` uniquely in the tangential segment domain".
Namespace `Submission.SegmentDomain`; the module is imported by `Submission.lean`, is sorry-free, and
all four headline declarations are axiom-clean.

### 89.1 The two new theorems

* **`cutTime_le_of_meet`** — *the first structural fact about the cut locus*: if `ξ ≠ η` are unit
  directions at `p` whose rays are both minimising at `m > 0` and meet there
  (`GExp p (m•ξ) = GExp p (m•η)`), then `cutTime p ξ ≤ m`.  In words: **a ray stops being
  minimising no later than the first point where it is met by another minimising geodesic of the same
  length.**  (This is the "half of the structure theorem `σ(v) = min{κ(v), first meeting}`" that is
  provable without any curvature hypothesis.)

  Proof: suppose `m < τ := cutTime p ξ`.  The **sync lemma** (`LensSync.eq_GExp_of_minimising_pair`)
  makes the two rays agree on all of `(m, τ)` (every `t ∈ (m,τ)` is in `minimisingSet p ξ` by
  `CutTime.mem_minimisingSet_iff`).  Pick `t₀ = (m+τ)/2`: there the two rays agree on a
  *neighbourhood*, so their velocities at `t₀` agree
  (`GeodesicExtend.curveVelocity_congr_of_eventuallyEq`).  **Geodesic uniqueness**
  (`GlobalExp.eqOn_Ioo_of_isGeodesicAt_of_eq`, on `Ioo (m-1) (τ+1)`) then promotes the equality of
  the initial data at `t₀` to equality of the two rays on the *whole* interval `(m-1, τ+1)`, hence on
  a neighbourhood of `m`, hence (again by germ-invariance of `curveVelocity`) the two velocities *at*
  `m` coincide — contradicting `XuStep.velocity_ne_of_meet`.

* **`segmentDomain`** — Xu's `D̃_p`, in the form used by the library:
  `segmentDomain hmet p = {v | v = 0 ∨ ∃ hv : v ≠ 0, ‖v‖ < cutTime p (unitOf v hv)}`
  (the *strict* inequality is essential: the flat torus shows that vectors of norm exactly `τ` can be
  reached by two minimising geodesics, so the closed ball is *not* injective).  Basic API:
  `zero_mem_segmentDomain`, `mem_segmentDomain_iff`, `mem_segmentDomain_of_norm_lt`,
  `edist_GExp_eq_norm_of_mem_segmentDomain` (**vectors of `D̃_p` are minimising**:
  `d(p, GExp p v) = ‖v‖`, from `minimisingSet = [0, cutTime]`), and

* **`ball_subset_segmentDomain`** — `InjAtLeast hmet p ρ` implies that the ball of radius `ρ` lies in
  `D̃_p` (`injAtLeast_le_cutTime`), so `D̃_p ⊇` the injectivity ball;

* **`injOn_GExp_segmentDomain`** — **THE SEGMENT-DOMAIN STATEMENT**: `GExp p` is **injective** on
  `D̃_p`.  Proof: two vectors `u ≠ v` of `D̃_p` with the same image are both minimising, so
  `‖u‖ = ‖v‖ = m` (`edist_GExp_eq_norm_of_mem_segmentDomain`); if the two unit directions agreed then
  `u = v`, so they differ and `cutTime_le_of_meet` gives `cutTime p (unitOf u) ≤ m = ‖u‖`, against
  `‖u‖ < cutTime p (unitOf u)` from membership in `D̃_p`.

* **`exists_unique_segmentDomain_vec`** — the *lift* form: every point `y` with
  `d(p,y) < ρ ≤ inj(p)` is `GExp p v` for **exactly one** `v ∈ D̃_p`; and
  **`ball_subset_image_segmentDomain`** — the injectivity ball is contained in `GExp p '' D̃_p`.

### 89.2 What the lift step still needs (precise list)

Xu's first paragraph concludes that the minimal geodesic `γ` from `q` to the cut point `x₀` can be
**lifted** into `D̃_p`.  With `injOn_GExp_segmentDomain` and the curve-lifting lemma proved by the
fleet this session,

> `Commons.Topology.exists_lift_of_injective_open_nonempty`
> (`import Submission.Commons.Topology.exists_lift_of_injective_open_nonempty`) — an injective,
> *open* map on an open set `U` lifts continuous curves with values in `f '' U` to `U`, uniquely
> (hypothesis `[Nonempty X]` and `Set.EqOn`; see the gotcha below),

the two remaining inputs are:

1. **`GExp p` is an open map on `D̃_p`.**  It suffices that `GExp p` be a local diffeomorphism at each
   `v ∈ D̃_p`, and *that* needs: **there are no conjugate points inside `D̃_p`** — classically
   `σ(v) ≤ κ(v)` ("the cut time is at most the first conjugate time"), because at a conjugate vector
   the differential is singular and `RayConj.pi_div_sqrt_le_of_singular_mfderiv` (plus
