## 90. SESSION: **THE `hloc`-FREE TRANSFER LEMMA** (`Submission/KlingenbergTransfer.lean`)

### 90.1 The correction

`KlingenbergLemma.le_two_mul_of_isClosedGeodesicLoop_lower` (session §81) carried the hypothesis

> `hloc : ∀ w : T_pM, ‖w‖ = m → LocallyInjectiveAtGExp hmet p w`  ("`GExp p` is locally injective at
> every vector of norm `m`")

at the **maximal** injectivity radius `m`.  §87.6b observed that this hypothesis is **false**: on `Sⁿ`
with `m = π` every vector of norm `π` maps to the antipode, so `GExp p` is not locally injective at
any of them.  Consequently the old lemma is inapplicable exactly in the case `m ≥ π/√K` — which is
the case where one has *no* cut-point witness and must instead use the diameter bound.  The old
lemma is kept (nothing used it) but is now marked **superseded** in its docstring, pointing at §90.

### 90.2 The corrected statements (both sorry-free and axiom-clean)

* **`KlingenbergTransfer.le_two_mul_of_isClosedGeodesicLoop_lower_of_lt`** — the transfer lemma
  *below the conjugate radius*: with `sec ≤ K` (`0 < K`), `m` the maximal injectivity radius of `p`,
  **`m < π/√K`**, the Klingenberg–Xu input `hXu` (two distinct vectors of norm `m` with the same
  image give a closed geodesic loop through `p` of length `≤ 2m`) and `hℓ` (every closed geodesic
  loop through `p` has length `≥ ℓ`), conclude `ℓ ≤ 2m`.  **No `hloc`**: below `π/√K` local
  injectivity is a *theorem*
  (`RayConj.locallyInjectiveAtGExp_of_norm_lt_pi_div_sqrt`, from Rauch's conjugate-radius bound),
  which is precisely what rules out the second alternative of
  `CutStep3.exists_two_minimising_or_not_locallyInjective` — the very alternative that `hloc` was
  there to kill.

* **`KlingenbergTransfer.pi_div_sqrt_le_of_isClosedGeodesicLoop_lower`** — the **pointwise Klingenberg
  estimate**: with `sec ≤ K`, `m` maximal at `p`, `hXu` as above (at that `m`) and the deep input
  `hdeep` (every closed geodesic loop through `p` has length `≥ 2π/√K`), conclude
  **`π/√K ≤ inj(p)`**.  Proof: if `m < π/√K` the cut-point step produces the two vectors (no `hloc`
  needed), `hXu` gives a loop of length `T ≤ 2m` and `hdeep` gives `2π/√K ≤ T`, so
  `2π/√K ≤ 2m`, i.e. `π/√K ≤ m` — and the case `π/√K ≤ m` is trivial.  (Equivalently: the case
  `m < π/√K` is *impossible* under the two inputs; that is the structure theorem
  `inj = min{conj, ½ℓ}` specialised to the conjugate radius.)

### 90.3 Payoff: `KlingenbergCore` now *consumes* the transfer lemma

`KlingenbergCore.diameter_gt_half_pi_of_klingenberg` (§87.6b) had the two-case argument inlined;
it now calls `pi_div_sqrt_le_of_isClosedGeodesicLoop_lower` and is a handful of lines long.  Its
