## 94. SESSION: **THE κ-SIDE BRIDGE, CURVATURE-FREE** (`Submission/SingularConj.lean`)

This session closes the *other* half of the equivalence "`v` is conjugate to `p` ⟺ `d exp_p` is
singular at `v`", and with it one of the two inputs of `ConjCut.isInvertible_mfderiv_of_mem_segmentDomain`
(§91.2).  New module `Submission.SingularConj.lean` (namespace `Submission.SingularConj`, imported by
`Submission.lean`, sorry-free, axiom-clean — `#print axioms` = `propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound`).

### 94.1 The headline

> **`Submission.SingularConj.singular_imp_conjTime`**
> ```
> theorem singular_imp_conjTime (hmet : IsMetricCompatible cov) (htor : cov.torsion = 0) :
>     Submission.ConjCut.SingularImpConjTime (I := I) hmet htor
> ```
> i.e. for `v ≠ 0` with `¬ (mfderiv (GExp p) v).IsInvertible`, the ray `s ↦ GExp p (s • unitOf v)`
> has a **conjugate time** at `‖v‖`: there is a Jacobi field along the ray, not identically zero,
> vanishing at `0` and at `‖v‖`.  **No curvature hypothesis is used** — in particular no Rauch
> estimate, and no bound on `v`.

Corollary (`singularImpConjTime_of_torsion_free`) and the payoff
**`isInvertible_mfderiv_of_mem_segmentDomain_of_cutConj`**: the invertibility of `d GExp_p` on the
tangential segment domain `D̃_p` now depends on the *single* remaining classical input `σ ≤ κ`
(`Submission.ConjCut.CutConjInequality`).  The chain of §91.2 therefore becomes

  `d GExp_p` invertible on `D̃_p`  ⟸  `CutConjInequality`  +  `injOn_GExp_segmentDomain`
                                   +  `Commons.Topology.exists_lift_of_injective_open_nonempty`.

### 94.2 Why the old proof could not give it, and what replaces Rauch

`Submission/RayConj.pi_div_sqrt_le_of_singular_mfderiv` contains the whole *construction*: the kernel
vector `ξ` of the singular differential, the unit direction `u = ‖v‖⁻¹ • v`, the perturbation
`η = ‖v‖⁻¹ • ξ`, the variation field
`J σ = curveVelocity (fun r ↦ GExp p (σ • (u + r • η))) 0`, and the two identities `J 0 = 0`,
`J ‖v‖ = 0` (the second is the chain rule `ChartGeoAt.curveVelocity_eq_mfderiv_comp` plus the kernel
condition).  Its only *curvature-dependent* step is the **non-triviality** `∃ s ∈ Ioo 0 ‖v‖, J s ≠ 0`,
which it obtains from Rauch via `RauchK.jacobiTensor_injective_of_norm_lt_pi_div_sqrt_of_curvLe` —
and which is exactly what forces `π/√K ≤ ‖v‖` there.

The replacement is curvature-free and purely a statement about `d exp_0 = id`:

* `fderiv_chartGExp_zero` — `fderiv ℝ (extChartAt I p ∘ GExp p) 0 = id`
  (from `mfderiv_GExp_zero` + `mfderiv_extChartAt_self` through `mfderiv_comp`);
* `eq_zero_of_norm_sub_id_lt` — an operator within `1/2` of the identity in operator norm is
  injective (`‖y‖ ≤ ‖Ay‖ + ‖(id - A)y‖ ≤ ‖Ay‖ + ‖y‖/2`);
* **`exists_pos_fderiv_chartGExp_injective`** — `fderiv ℝ (extChartAt I p ∘ GExp p)` is injective on a
  ball around `0` (continuity of `fderiv` from `ContDiffAt.continuousAt_fderiv`);
* **`model_deriv_of_variation`** — if `J σ = 0` then the *model* derivative
  `fderiv ℝ (extChartAt I p ∘ GExp p) (σ • u) (σ • η)` vanishes (chain rule + linearity: the chart
  differential is linear, so it sends `0` to `0`; *no invertibility of the chart differential is
  needed*);
* **`exists_pos_variation_ne_zero`** — for `‖u‖ = 1`, `η ≠ 0` there is `δ > 0` with `J σ ≠ 0` for all
  `σ ∈ (0, δ)`: if `J σ = 0` then `fderiv F (σ•u) (σ•η) = 0`, hence `σ • η = 0` by injectivity of
  `fderiv F (σ•u)`, contradicting `σ > 0`, `η ≠ 0`.

`singular_imp_conjTime` is then `RayConj`'s construction with `exists_pos_variation_ne_zero` in place of
the Rauch step (the non-triviality witness is taken as `min δ ‖v‖ / 2`).

### 94.3 Gotchas (all cost real time; they will recur)

* **`(show TangentSpace I p from v)` is *not* a silent type ascription.**  It elaborates to a
  `let`-term (`have this := v; this`), so *rewriting* through it fails (`rw [GlobalExp.GExp_zero]`
  cannot see the `0`, `simp only [GExp_zero]` reports the lemma as *unused*).  The working idiom is
  `have hg0 : (fun v : E => GExp p (show TangentSpace I p from v)) (0:E) = p := by
  simpa using GlobalExp.GExp_zero …` and then `rw [hg0]`; i.e. **always go through a `have` whose
  statement uses the same `show`-shape**.
* **The model norm and the fibre norm differ.**  `‖(show E from u)‖` (model norm of the coerced
  vector) is *not* `‖u‖` (fibre norm), and `‖(show E from (σ • u))‖ = |σ| * ‖(show E from u)‖`, not
  `σ`.  Any ball condition on the *model* side must be converted with an explicit constant
  (`mu := ‖(show E from u)‖`, then `σ < δ/(mu+1)`).  (`(show E from (σ • u)) = σ • (show E from u)`
  *is* `rfl`, so the scalar action is harmless.)
* **`rw [ContinuousLinearMap.comp_apply]` can fail on `(mfderiv g _ ∘SL mfderiv f _) z`** with
  "pattern not found" + "the target expression is not type-correct under the implicit transparency
  level" — the `TangentSpace` instances of the two `mfderiv`s make the term ill-typed at implicit
  transparency.  Workaround used here: name the two functions with `set`, prove the *value* identity
  `(mfderiv (gE ∘ fE) x) z = (mfderiv gE (fE x)) ((mfderiv fE x) z)` by `rw [h3]; rfl` (it *is* `rfl`),
  then `rw [hval, hmf]` and close with `map_zero`.  Also: `rw [mfderiv_eq_fderiv]` (not `←`) turns the
  `mfderiv` form into the `fderiv` form; the `←` direction silently fails to find `fderiv`.
* `h1.sub tendsto_const_nhds` **does not elaborate** (the constant is undetermined): use
  `h1.sub_const c`.
* `rw [...] at hw ⊢` fails when the rewrite does not apply at `hw`; split into `refine hδsub ?_`
  followed by the rewrites on the goal.
* `h.norm` has target `𝓝 ‖0‖`, not `𝓝 0`: use `simpa using h.norm`.
* `ContDiffAt.continuousAt_fderiv` needs the continuity order `≠ 0` (pass `one_ne_zero`), and
  `ContinuousAt` is a *def*: `simpa only [ContinuousAt, hderiv0] using hcont` is the idiom.
* `Function.Injective` binds its two arguments **implicitly**: `hδ₁inj w hw a b` is wrong (it applies
  the injectivity proof to `a` and `b` as the two point arguments, then `hfin` as the equality); write
  the equality first, `hδ₁inj w hw (h : f a = f b)`.
* `Metric.mem_nhds_iff.mp` gives a *subset* `ball 0 ρ ⊆ s`, so the hypothesis must be `w ∈ ball 0 ρ`
  (`rw [Metric.mem_ball, dist_eq_norm, sub_zero]`), not `‖w‖ < ρ`.
* `exact` is more forgiving than `simpa` for `@Eq (TangentSpace I x) a 0` vs `@Eq (TangentSpace I y) a 0`
  (defeq through `TangentSpace I _ = E`): `simpa` normalises and then complains.

### 94.4 Effect on the plan

`hXu` (§90.5/§91.2) needed `D̃_p`-invertibility of `d GExp_p`, hence `σ ≤ κ`.  That requirement is now
halved: the κ-side bridge is a theorem.  **However** — and this is worth recording — the *other* input
`CutConjInequality` may not in fact be needed for `(K2)` at all: everything in the case `m < π/√K` of
`KlingenbergCore` happens below the conjugate radius, where invertibility is already available from
`ConjCut.isInvertible_mfderiv_GExp_of_norm_lt_pi_div_sqrt` (Rauch, no `σ ≤ κ`).  The remaining question
is whether the *lift* step of `hXu` can be run on the truncated domain
`D̃_p ∩ {‖v‖ < π/√K}`.  This is the cheapest route to `hXu` and should be investigated before any
further investment in the global `σ ≤ κ`.

