## 99.2 How `hgeo` is proved (and why no flow computation is needed)

`σ ↦ fam D X u σ t` is the chart-coordinate curve of the `GExp`-ray from `γ t` in the direction
`ξ = frameVector z₀ (γ t) (u t)`.  Hence `Submission.GeoChartCurve.chartGeodesicEquation_GExp`
(giving `deriv(deriv(…)) s + chrB (…)(deriv … s)(deriv … s) = 0` in the chart `z₀`, at *every* time
`s`) is *exactly* `hgeo` at `s = 0`, after transporting `fam` to the ray through
`UniformInj.GExp_eq_symm_Hfun` and `frameVector_smul'`.  Three `deriv`-congruences
(`Filter.EventuallyEq.deriv_eq` on `Ioo (-δ) δ`, an open neighbourhood of `0`) do the rest.

The **degenerate direction** `u t = 0` is handled separately: then `σ • u t = 0` for *all* `σ`, so
`fam D X u σ t = Hfun D (X t, 0)` is constant in `σ`, giving `psiV = psiA = 0` and a trivial
identity.  (This is the only reason `chartGeodesicEquation_GExp`'s hypothesis `ξ ≠ 0` is not a
problem; note the application — the shortening argument — has `u 0 = u L = 0`.)

## 99.3 How `hgeo'` is proved (the Clairaut chapter)

`hgeo` is an identity in `t`, so differentiate it.  The individual derivatives are:

* `HasDerivAt (fun t' => psiA D X u 0 t') (psiEA D X u 0 t)` — needs
  `deriv_psiA_eq_psiEA` (Clairaut), i.e. `∂_t∂_σ² fam = ∂_σ²∂_t fam`;
* `HasDerivAt (fun t' => fam D X u 0 t') (psiW D X u 0 t)` — by definition of `psiW`;
* `HasDerivAt (fun t' => psiV D X u 0 t') (psiE D X u 0 t)` — needs `deriv_psiV_eq_psiE`;
* the `chrB` term by `ChartCurv.hasDerivAt_chrB_curve`;

and then `HasDerivAt.unique` with the zero function (`hgeo` holds on a neighbourhood of `t`), followed
by `eq_neg_of_add_eq_zero_left`.  **Both** Clairaut instances are `Commons.Analysis.Calculus.deriv_deriv_swap`
(the second one applied to the function `(σ,t) ↦ psiV D X u σ t`, whose `C^∞`-ness is supplied by
`contDiffOn_psiV`, hence by `contDiffOn_deriv_section_fst`).  This is the payoff of the commons lemma
requested at the beginning of the session.

