### 101.1 THE DESIGN PROBLEM SOLVED THIS SESSION
The remaining gap for `ConjCut.CutConjInequality` (σ ≤ κ) is the *assembly*: the second variation of
the energy of the broken-Jacobi-field family, plus the two index-side identities

* (i) `∫_0^t idxAlong γ J (∇J) = 0`  (`J` a Jacobi field with `J 0 = J t = 0`),
* (ii) `∫_0^t bilAlong γ J W (∇J) (∇W) = ⟪∇J t, W t⟫` (the corner pairing).

Both statements involve the **global fields** `J` and `∇J` along the curve.  `Submission/IndexIntrinsic2`
provides the cover machinery (`IsJacobiPiece`/`IsBilPiece` + `integral_idxAlong_eq_zero_of_cover`,
`integral_bilAlong_corner_of_cover`), but it requires the global field `Z = ∇J` as *input*, together
with the per-piece identification `Z t = frameVector x₀ (γ t) (DtQ … t)`.  The obstacle was: **how to
define `∇J` as a global function `ℝ → (t : ℝ) → TangentSpace I (γ t)`?**  A Jacobi field along a curve
is not a global section, and any chart formula is only valid on one piece.

**The solution (this session).**  Write the field in the **chart at its own base point**:

```
covAlong cov V t := frameVector (I := I) (γ t) (γ t)
  (DtQ cov (γ t) (chartCoord (I := I) (γ t) γ) (chartCoords (I := I) (γ t) γ V)
    (deriv (chartCoord (I := I) (γ t) γ)) (deriv (chartCoords (I := I) (γ t) γ V)) t)
```

This is a *global* definition (no cover, no choice).  Its two properties are proved once:

* **`covAlong_eq_cov`** — `covAlong cov V t = cov 𝒱 (γ t) (curveVelocity γ t)` for any globally
  smooth section `𝒱` agreeing with `V` along `γ` near `t`.  (This is the *intrinsic* characterisation:
  it shows that `covAlong` is the covariant derivative, independently of the chart in the definition.)
* **`covAlong_eq_chart`** — the identification with the chart formula `DtQ` in *any* chart, i.e.
  exactly the clause of `IsJacobiPiece`/`IsBilPiece`.

The smoothness input is the predicate `SmoothAlongAt γ V t` (`∃ U ∋ t` open on which the chart-`γ t`
coordinates of `V` are `C^∞` **and** `γ(U)` lies in the chart at `γ t`), produced by
`smoothAlongAt_of_isJacobiAt` (from `IsJacobiAt` + the curve's smoothness) and by
`smoothAlongAt_frameVector` / `BumpField.smoothAlongAt_bumpField` (from a chart formula).

