### 106.4 The strict corner inequality (`XuLift.edist_lt_of_velocity_ne_neg`)

> Let `ξ, η` be unit directions at `p` whose rays meet at time `m > 0` and are minimising on `[0,m]`,
> and suppose the two velocities at the meeting point are **not** opposite.  Then for all
> `τ, s ∈ (0,m)`,  `d (GExp p (τ•ξ)) (GExp p (s•η)) < (m - τ) + (m - s)`.

This is the "the corner is not straight so the broken geodesic is not minimising" statement, in the
quantitative form the `F_{p;q}` argument consumes.  The proof (all with tools already in the library)
is:

1. the two intermediate points are at distance `m - τ` resp. `m - s` from the meeting point
   (`LensSync.edist_eq_sub_of_minimising_ray`), so the triangle inequality gives `≤`;
2. suppose equality.  Then the broken path `B := GExp p (s•η) → x₀ := GExp p (m•ξ) → Q := GExp p (τ•ξ)`
   is a *minimising* broken geodesic;
3. the **no-corner lemma** (`GSField.eq_GExp_of_minimising_broken`), applied with base point `B` and
   direction the velocity of the `η`-ray at `s` (unit speed: `BrokenLength.curveSpeed_GExp_ray`),
   identifies the whole path with a *single* ray: `Q = GExp B (((m-τ)+(m-s)) • w₂(s))`; translating
   along the `η`-ray (`GExpTranslate.GExp_translate`) gives `Q = GExp p ((2m - τ) • η)`;
4. the same translation formula at the base point `x₀ = GExp p (m•η)` gives
   `GExp x₀ ((m-τ) • w₂) = GExp p ((2m-τ)•η) = Q` where `w₂` is the velocity of the `η`-ray at `m`;
5. the reversed `ξ`-ray (`GeodesicLoop.GExp_neg_velocity_of_ray`) gives
   `GExp x₀ ((m-τ) • (-w₁)) = GExp p (τ•ξ) = Q`;
6. so the two vectors `(m-τ)(-w₁)` and `(m-τ)w₂` at `x₀` have the same image and the same norm, and
   the first is in the segment domain of `x₀` (because the reversed `ξ`-ray reaches `p` at time `m`,
   so `cutTime x₀ (-w₁) ≥ m > m - τ`); `SegmentDomain.cutTime_le_of_meet` then forces their unit
   directions to be equal, i.e. `w₁ = -w₂`.  ∎

Note the shape: **the contradiction is again a cut-time contradiction at the meeting point**, and it
uses `σ`-style reasoning rather than any second variation.

### 106.5 The range criterion (`XuLift.mem_image_segmentDomain_of_edist_add_lt`)

> If `d(p,q) = τ`, `τ ≤ m`, `InjAtLeast hmet p m` and `d(q,y) + d(p,y) < (m - τ) + m`, then `y` lies
> in the image of the tangential segment domain of `p` (hence is *not* a cut point of `p`).

This is Xu's `(2.1)`/`F_{p;q}` step: points outside `GExp p '' D̃_p` are at distance `≥ m` from `p`
(`SegmentDomain.ball_subset_image_segmentDomain`), while the triangle inequality through `q` gives
`d(q,y) + d(p,y) ≥ (m - τ) + m` for such points.  In Xu's proof the function `F_{p;q}` is defined on
the *cut locus*; the lemma above replaces the cut locus by its complement
`M ∖ GExp p '' D̃_p`, which is what the project has.

**Corollary for the main argument** (`q` = a point of the `ξ`-ray at time `τ`, `y` = a point of a
minimising geodesic from `q` to `β(s) := GExp p (s•η)`): combining §106.4 with `d(p, β(s)) = s`
gives `d(q,y) + d(p,y) ≤ d(q,β(s)) + s < (m-τ) + m`, so **every** point of `γ_s` lies in
`GExp p '' D̃_p`.  This is Claim A of the plan below.

### 106.6 The key simplification: **openness replaces Arzelà–Ascoli**

Xu's proof of `propertyoflift` needs a *uniform Lipschitz bound* for the lifted curves, obtained from
a positive lower bound for the distance of the lifts to the conjugate locus, and then Arzelà–Ascoli in
`T_pM`.  With the ball form of the IFT (§106.3) this is unnecessary:

* `GExp p` is a **local homeomorphism at every point of `D̃_p`** (and at every non-conjugate point of
  `∂D̃_p`), hence *open* there; together with `injOn_GExp_segmentDomain` this makes
  `GExp p : D̃_p → M` an **open injective map**, i.e. a homeomorphism onto its image;
* therefore the inverse of the local homeomorphism, and the lifting of curves, are **continuous**;
  convergence of the lifts is then obtained from convergence of the curves, with no compactness
  argument in `T_pM` at all.

The remaining compactness input is only the *elementary* one used in Claim B below (a bounded
sequence in `M` has a convergent subsequence and the minimising geodesics from a fixed `q` to
converging endpoints converge to the unique minimising geodesic to the limit).

