* the contradiction: `s • η ∉ ball u r` because
  `‖s • η - m • ξ‖_fibre ≥ m‖ξ - η‖ - δ₀/2 ≥ (15/16) m‖ξ - η‖`, hence the *model* norm is
  `≥ (15/16) m‖ξ-η‖/√Λ > m‖ξ-η‖/(4√Λ) ≥ r`.  ∎

### 107.5 THE REMAINING GAP — AND A CORRECTION TO `LoopLengthLowerBound`

**`Submission.KlingenbergXu.LoopLengthLowerBound hmet K` is FALSE as stated.**  It asserts that
every geodesic loop has length `≥ 2π/√K` under the *sole* hypothesis `sec ≤ K` — with no lower
curvature bound.  Counterexample: the **Berger spheres** `(S³, σ₁² + σ₂² + τ²σ₃²)`, where
`sec ∈ [τ², 4 - 3τ²]` and the Hopf fibres are closed geodesics of length `2πτ`.  At `τ² = 0.3`:
`sec ≤ K := 3.1 < 4` ✓, and the fibre length `2π·0.5477… ≈ 3.4416` is *below* `2π/√3.1 ≈ 3.5685`.
(The pinching `sec ≥ K/4` also fails there, which is exactly the hypothesis the classical theorem
needs.)

`Submission.XuReduction` therefore introduces the corrected classical statement
**`PinchedLoopLengthLowerBound hmet K`** (`sec ∈ [K/4, K]` and `M` simply connected ⟹ every geodesic
loop has length `≥ 2π/√K`), shows it implies the form used by the reduction
(`loopLengthLowerBound_of_pinched`), and reproves `(K2)` from it
(`diameter_gt_half_pi_of_pinchedLoopLengthLowerBound`).  The reduction theorems of
`KlingenbergXu` remain valid as they stand (the hypothesis is simply stronger than necessary); they
must be instantiated *under* the pinch, which in the `(K2)` application is automatic
(`1 ≤ sec ≤ K` with `K ≥ 1` gives `sec ≥ K/4`).

### 107.6 What is still missing: `hdeep` = Klingenberg 1961

`PinchedLoopLengthLowerBound` is Klingenberg 1961 and is the **only** remaining gap of
`KlingenbergBound` (hence of the sphere theorem).  The picture (from §80, corrected and
re-confirmed this session against the classical literature):

* the structure theorem `inj(p) = min{conjε(p), ½·(shortest geodesic loop at p)}` (Xu's Theorem 1.3);
* `conjε(p) ≥ conj(p) ≥ π/√K` by Rauch — **available**;
* so one needs **`ℓ(M) ≥ 2π/√K`**: sharp at `ℂPⁿ`, `ℍPⁿ` and the round sphere (all with
  `ℓ·√K = 2π`), and `ℝPⁿ` (`ℓ·√K = π`, *not* simply connected) shows that simple connectivity is
  essential;
* the **second-variation computation** for a closed geodesic `c` of length `L` with a *periodic*
  parallel normal field `X` and the variation field `V = sin(2πs/L)X` gives
  `I(V,V) = |X|²(2π²/L - ∫ sec·sin²)` — this is `< 0` exactly when `L > 2π/√(min sec)`: **short**
  closed geodesics are *stable*, which is precisely why the naive shortening argument does not
  work and why Berger's structure (Morse theory of the path space `Ω_{p,q}` of the earlier note, and
  the holonomy argument in even dimensions) is needed;
* **even dimensions**: Berger's holonomy argument — the parallel transport of the normal bundle
  `c'^⊥` around a closed geodesic lies in `SO(n-1)`, which for odd `n-1` has the eigenvalue `1`;
  the corresponding **periodic** parallel field `X` gives `I(X,X) = -∫ sec(X,c') < 0`, so *every*
  closed geodesic is unstable;
* **odd dimensions**: Klingenberg 1961 — the Morse theory of the free loop space
  (Palais–Smale condition C, the minimax, the index estimate).  **This is the wall**; it is not in
  Mathlib and is not attempted here.

