## 109. SESSION (continued): THE STABILITY ANALYSIS OF CLOSED GEODESICS, DONE CORRECTLY

This section records the second-variation computations for closed geodesics in the form now needed by
§78.2/§108, with one **correction** to an earlier session note, and with the precise even/odd split.

### 109.1 The three variation computations

Let `c : ℝ → M` be a closed unit-speed geodesic of length `L` with `sec ∈ [K/4, K]`, and let
`X` be a **parallel** normal field (`∇_{c'}X = 0`).

* **(a) the constant mode with a *periodic* `X`.**  If `X(L) = X(0)` (i.e. the normalized parallel
  transport `P_⊥` of the normal bundle `c'^⊥` fixes `X(0)`), then `V := X` is a variation field of
  *closed* curves and `I(V,V) = ∫₀^L (|∇V|² - sec|V|²) = -∫₀^L sec(X,c') < 0` **for every `L`**.
  So `c` is **unstable**, with no hypothesis on its length.
* **(b) the frequency-`k` modes.**  For `X` parallel and `f` a smooth periodic function with
  `f(L) = f(0)`, `V = fX` is periodic iff `f(L)X(L) = f(0)X(0)`; with `X` periodic (case (a)) and
  `f(s) = cos(2πks/L)` one gets `I = (L/2)[(2πk/L)² - \overline{sec}]` where `\overline{sec}` is a
  weighted average of `sec(X,c')`, so `I < 0` forces `L > 2πk/√K` (using `sec ≤ K`); these modes are
  *positive* for short `L`, which is why (a) is the relevant one.
* **(c) the *based* index.**  With `f(s) = sin(πs/L)` (vanishing at both ends, i.e. the variation
  fixes `c(0)`) and `sec ≥ K/4`: `I ≤ (L/2)[(π/L)² - K/4]`, which is negative exactly when
  `L > 2π/√K`.

### 109.2 The correction (important — an earlier note had the model case wrong)

For the equator of the round `S²` (length `L = 2π`, `sec ≡ 1`): with `N` the parallel unit normal
field and `V = fN`, `I(V,V) = ∫₀^{2π}(f'² - f²)`, and expanding the **periodic** `f` in Fourier
series `f = Σ_{k∈ℤ} a_k e^{iks}` gives `I = 2π Σ_{k∈ℤ}(k² - 1)|a_k|²`.  Hence the **free index of the
equator is `1`**, the negative direction being the **constant mode `k = 0`** (this is exactly case
(a) above: `f ≡ 1` and `N` periodic), and the nullity is `2` (the modes `k = ±1`).  This matches
Bott's formula `ind = (2k-1)(n-1)` for the `k`-fold great circle in `ΛS^n` (`k = 1`, `n = 2` ⟹ 1).
An earlier session note (and §80) recorded the index as `0`; that was the error of expanding in the
base `sin(ks)` instead of `e^{iks}` (the `k = 0` mode is absent from `sin(ks)`, `k ≥ 1`).  The
**based** index of the equator is also `1` (the Dirichlet eigenfunction `sin(s/2)` gives
`I = π(1/4 - 1) < 0`), consistently with §109.1(c) (`L = 2π > 2π/√1 = 2π`? — here `L` equals the
threshold, so the negative direction is the borderline `k = 1` of the Dirichlet problem; the
computation `π(k²/4 - 1)` is negative only for `k = 1`, giving index `1`).

### 109.3 The even/odd split, precisely

* **`n` even, `M` orientable** (e.g. any simply connected `M`, which is automatically orientable):
  the parallel transport of `c'^⊥` around a closed geodesic is an element of `SO(n-1)` with `n-1`
  **odd**, hence has the eigenvalue `1`
  (`Commons.LinearAlgebra.Orthogonal.odd_dim_det_one_fixed_vector`, in the commons since this
  session).  By §109.1(a) **every closed geodesic is unstable**.
* **`n` odd**: `P_⊥ ∈ SO(n-1)` with `n-1` even may have no eigenvalue `1`.  In the *failure* case
  all rotation angles `φ_j` of `P_⊥` lie in `[L√K/2, π]` (the general "rotation" variation
  `V(s) = cos(θs)X₁(s) + sin(θs)X₂(s)`, with `θ ≡ (2πk - φ)/L` for periodicity, satisfies
  `I ≤ (L/2)[θ² - K/4]`, so it is *negative* precisely when the holonomy angle is
  `dist(φ, 2πℤ) < L√K/2`; this is exactly the classical statement that the normal holonomy angle
  bounds the length of a stable closed geodesic).  In the failure case the geodesic can be
  **stable**, and no second-variation argument can exclude it: this is the wall (Klingenberg 1961,
  free-loop-space Morse theory).

Both §109.1(a)-(c) and the `φ`-analysis above are *computations within the project's API*
(`Submission.IndexCorner`, `Submission.IndexIntrinsic2`, `Submission.SecondVarGeo`): nothing in them
is formalised yet, but they show precisely which rungs of §108.2 are needed for the `n` **even**
half of the Klingenberg length bound, and why the `n` **odd** half is a different (and much harder)
problem.

