## 110. THE CLASSICAL PROOF OF KLINGENBERG'S INJECTIVITY RADIUS ESTIMATE — located in the literature

This section records the outcome of a literature search (session 18).  It **corrects §78.2** and
pins down, with sources, the exact logical shape of the remaining input
`Submission.Klingenberg.KlingenbergBound cov` = (K2).  Sources read (freely available):

* **U. Abresch, W. T. Meyer**, *Injectivity radius estimates and sphere theorems*, MSRI
  Publications **30** (1997), 1–48, `https://library.slmath.org/books/Book30/files/abresch.pdf`.
  *(Sections 1 and 4 are the key ones; verified by `pdftotext` this session.)*
* **S. Xu**, *On conjugate points and geodesic loops in a complete Riemannian manifold*,
  arXiv:1401.5549 (the project's `XuTrap`).
* Wikipedia, *Sphere theorem* / *Berger's sphere* (statements, and the role of even-dimensionality).

### 110.1 The statements

* **Theorem 1.5 + 1.6 (Klingenberg 1959/1961).** Let `Mⁿ` be compact and simply connected with
  `0 < sec ≤ K`.
  * `n` **even**: `inj(M) = conj(M) ≥ π/√K`.  (No pinching assumption!  Only positivity.)
  * `n` **odd**: the same conclusion `inj(M) = conj(M) ≥ π/√K` requires the **strictly
    1/4-pinched** hypothesis `K/4 < sec` (equivalently `sec ∈ (K/4, K]`).
  * In both cases `conj(M) ≥ π/√K` is Rauch (`Submission.ConjCut.not_isConjugateTime_of_lt_pi_div_sqrt`),
    so the content is **`inj(M) ≥ π/√K`**, equivalently, by Lemma 1.8 below, **the length bound**
    `ℓ(M) ≥ 2π/√K` for closed geodesics.
  * `inj(M) ≤ conj(M)` always (a geodesic is not minimising past its first conjugate point).
  * Sharpness: the Berger spheres `(S³, σ₁²+σ₂²+τ²σ₃²)` have `sec ∈ [τ², 4-3τ²]` and Hopf fibres
    (closed geodesics) of length `2πτ` with `inj ≤ πτ`; for `τ² < 1/3` one has `πτ < π/√(4-3τ²)`,
    so the *un-pinched* odd form is false — this is the counterexample already recorded in
    `Submission.XuReduction`.  (Abresch–Meyer Remark 1.7(ii): the optimal odd pinching constant is
    at least `0.117 > 1/9`, and in dimension 7 worse — Aloff–Wallach.)

### 110.2 Lemma 1.8 — the structure theorem (`inj = min{conj, ½ℓ}`)

> **Lemma (Klingenberg 1959, Lemma 4; Cheeger–Ebin, Lemma 5.6).** For `p ∈ M`,
> `inj(p) = min{conj(p), ½ ℓ(p)}`, where `ℓ(p)` is the infimum of the lengths of the *geodesic
> loops* at `p`; for compact `M`, `inj(M) = min{conj(M), ½ℓ(M)}` with `ℓ(M) = inf_p ℓ(p)`, and if
> `ℓ(M) < 2 conj(M)` the minimising loop is a (velocity-matched) **closed geodesic**.

Both directions of the loop half of this are *already in the project*, pointwise:
`GeodesicLoop.IsGeodesicLoopAt.two_mul_le_injAtLeast` (`inj(p) ≤ ½ℓ(p)`) and
`XuTrap.xuCornerExcluded` + `XuReduction.klingenbergLoopStep` (`ℓ(p) ≤ 2 inj(p)` whenever
`inj(p) < π/√K`).  What the project does **not** have is the compactness statement that the
infimum `ℓ(M)` is attained at a closed geodesic: this is precisely the "closed geodesic of length
`≤ 2 injSup`" item of §78.5, and it is needed to run *both* classical proofs below (in the
literature it is the content of "`inj(M) < conj(M)` ⟹ there is a closed geodesic `c₀` of length
`2 inj(M)`").

### 110.3 The EVEN-dimensional proof (Abresch–Meyer, proof of Theorem 1.5) — **a correction of §78.2**

§78.2 proposed a "farthest point / exactly two segments" argument (Petersen 5.7.12).  The
published argument is different, simpler, and uses Klingenberg's **lifting** observation instead:

1. Assume `inj(M) < conj(M)`, get a closed *unit-speed* geodesic `c₀` of length `L := 2 inj(M)`
   (Lemma 1.8).
2. **Synge.** `n` even and `M` orientable (automatic for simply connected `M`; for even `n` and
   `sec > 0`, Synge's lemma gives simple connectivity anyway): the parallel transport of the
   normal bundle `c₀'^⊥` around `c₀` is an element of `SO(n-1)` with `n-1` **odd**, hence
   (`Commons.LinearAlgebra.Orthogonal.odd_dim_det_one_fixed_vector`) fixes a vector; so there is a
   **closed parallel unit normal field** `v₀` along `c₀`.  *(Needs: global parallel fields, and
   that parallel transport on an oriented manifold is orientation-preserving.)*
3. **Second variation.** The family `c_t(s) = exp_{c₀(s)}(t v₀(s))` (`t ≠ 0` small) consists of
   closed curves with `L(c_t) < L(c₀)` — because the second variation of length along a *parallel*
   field is `I(v₀,v₀) = -∫ sec(v₀,c₀') < 0`, and `sec > 0`.  Note: **no length hypothesis and no
   pinching** — this is exactly why the even case needs only `sec > 0`.
4. **Klingenberg's lifting argument.** Every point of `c_t` is at distance `≤ ½L(c_t) < ½L = inj(M)
   ≤ inj(c_t(0))` from `c_t(0)` (each point of a closed curve is within *half the length* of the
   base point, along the curve).  Hence `c_t` lies in the *normal ball* of `c_t(0)` and lifts
   uniquely under `exp_{c_t(0)}` to `c̃_t` with `c̃_t(0) = 0`; the lifts are equicontinuous
   (this is where `inj(M) < conj(M)` is used), and extend continuously to `t = 0`, giving a lift
   `c̃₀` of `c₀` under `exp_{c₀(0)}` with `c̃₀(0) = 0`.  But `c₀` is a *geodesic* from `c₀(0)`, so its
   lift with `c̃₀(0) = 0` is the *straight line* `s ↦ s·c₀'(0)·`— of norm `L` at `s = 1`, while the
   lifts satisfy `‖c̃_t(s)‖ = d(c_t(0), c_t(s)) ≤ ½L(c_t) < ½L`.  Contradiction.

So the even case is a **complete finite project** with four ingredients: global parallel fields
(this session's target), Synge, the closed-curve second variation, and the lift argument.  It
yields `inj(M) = conj(M) ≥ π/√K` for even-dimensional `M`, hence Klingenberg 1959 and (with
Rauch + the existing diameter reduction) **Berger's even-dimensional sphere theorem**.

### 110.4 The ODD-dimensional proof (Abresch–Meyer, proof of Theorem 1.6)

Synge is unavailable (the normal holonomy `P ∈ SO(n-1)` with `n-1` even may have no fixed vector),
and this is where the free-loop-space argument enters:

1. Assume `inj(M) < conj(M)`; get the closed geodesic `c₀` of length `L₀ = 2 inj(M) < 2conj(M)`.
2. **Long homotopy lemma** (Klingenberg 1962, p. 50; Abresch–Meyer Lemma 4.3).  `M` is simply
   connected, so `c₀` is freely null-homotopic; *every* free null-homotopy of a nontrivial geodesic
   **digon** `c₀` of length `< 2 conj(M)` contains a curve of length `≥ 2 conj(M)`.  *(Proof: the
   curves of length `< 2conj(M)` can be cut in half at the base point and lifted, exactly as in
   step 4 of §110.3; the lift of a geodesic's half is a straight line, which contradicts the digon
   structure.)*  This is the **same lifting technology** as the even case, so the even case's
   ingredients 3–4 are shared.
3. **Minimax (Palais–Smale).**  `E_min(c₀) := inf{ max_t E(c_t) : c a free null-homotopy } ≥
   (2conj(M))²/2`; by the Palais–Smale **Condition C** for the energy on the free loop space, a
   minimising sequence of homotopies subconverges to a closed geodesic `c̄₀` with
   `L(c̄₀) ≥ 2 conj(M) ≥ 2π` and **free Morse index `≤ 1`** (degenerate Morse lemma).
4. **Index bound (elementary, and *not* yet in the project).**  For a closed unit-speed geodesic
   `γ` of length `L` in a manifold with `sec ∈ [δ, 1]`, `δ > 1/4`, **the free index is `≥ n-1` as
   soon as `L > π/√δ`**: take a parallel orthonormal basis `X₁,…,X_{n-1}` of the normal bundle
   (parallel fields exist unconditionally!), set `V_j(s) := sin(πs/L)·X_j(s)` — a *periodic* field
   (it vanishes at both ends, so it is a legitimate variation of closed curves).  Then
   `|V_j| = sin(πs/L)`, `∇V_j = (π/L)cos(πs/L)·X_j`, so
   `I(V_j,V_j) = π²/(2L) - ∫₀^L sin²(πs/L)·sec_j`, with `sec_j ≥ δ`, hence
   `I(V_j,V_j) ≤ (π² - δL²)/(2L) < 0 ⟺ L > π/√δ`.  The `n-1` fields are linearly independent
   (`X_j` independent), so the index is `≥ n-1 ≥ 2`.  Since `L(c̄₀) ≥ 2conj(M) ≥ 2π > π/√δ`
   (as `δ > 1/4`), this contradicts step 3.
5. Contradiction ⟹ `inj(M) = conj(M) ≥ π/√K`.

### 110.5 Why the odd case cannot be replaced by the even-case (second-variation) argument

This was investigated in detail this session and the classical picture is now fully explained.
On a closed geodesic `γ` of length `L` with parallel orthonormal normal fields `X_j` and holonomy
rotation angle `ψ_j ∈ [0,π]` on the `j`-th normal plane, the *closed* (periodic) variation fields
`V(s) = cos(ψ_j s/L)X₁(s) - sin(ψ_j s/L)X₂(s)` are the only *parallel-ish* periodic fields, and

`I(V,V) = ψ_j²/L - ∫₀^L sec(V,γ')`,  so `I(V,V) < 0` **iff** `ψ_j < L·√(average sec)`.

* If `ψ_j = 0` for some `j` (a fixed vector of the holonomy — Synge's situation in even
  dimension) the field is *parallel* and `I = -∫sec < 0` for **every** `L`: no length hypothesis.
* For `ψ_j ≠ 0` the shortening requires `L > ψ_j/√δ ≥ π/√δ` (using `ψ_j ≤ π` and `sec ≥ δ`),
  i.e. only **long** geodesics are seen by this argument.  The Dirichlet field
  `sin(πs/L)X_j` is the borderline case `ψ = π` and gives exactly the same threshold `L > π/√δ`.

Consequently a *short* closed geodesic (`L < π/√δ`) whose holonomy has no small rotation angle can
be **stable** (no negative direction obtainable from these fields), and no purely second-variational
argument excludes it: this is precisely the gap that the minimax in §110.4.3 fills, by producing a
*long* geodesic of index `≤ 1`.  (For `n` odd the holonomy may have no fixed vector; for `n` even
Synge always provides one, which is the whole difference between Theorems 1.5 and 1.6.  The Berger
spheres show the odd statement genuinely needs the pinching: they have short closed geodesics and
their holonomy angles are *large*.)

