### 112.2 The even-dimensional case of Klingenberg's estimate (Klingenberg 1959)

Reading Abresch–Meyer (§1, quoted verbatim in §110) again settles the **exact** logical shape:

> **Theorem 1.5.** Let `Mⁿ` be an even-dimensional **compact** Riemannian manifold with
> `sec > 0` (only positivity!) and suppose `M` is **orientable**.  Then `inj M = conj M ≥ π/√(max K)`.

*Simple connectivity is used in the printed statement only in order to guarantee orientability.*
The proof (Abresch–Meyer, "On the proof of Theorem 1.5") is:

1. `inj M ≤ conj M` (a geodesic is not minimising beyond the first conjugate point); proceed
   indirectly and assume `inj M < conj M`.
2. Lemma 1.8 (**Cheeger–Ebin 5.6**): `inj_M(p) = min{conj M, ½ ℓ_M(p)}` where `ℓ_M(p)` is the minimal
   length of a **nontrivial geodesic loop** through `p`; for compact `M`,
   `inj M = min{conj M, ½ ℓ(M)}` with `ℓ(M)` the minimal length of a nontrivial **closed geodesic**.
   "If `ℓ_M(p₀) < 2 conj M`, the geodesic loop of length `2 inj M` at `p₀` is actually a closed
   geodesic."  In our language, `ℓ_M(p) = 2 inj(p) < 2 conj(p)` is exactly the **cut-point
   dichotomy** of `CutStep3`/`XuTrap`: the closest cut point carries a second minimising geodesic,
   and Xu's Theorem B (`XuTrap.xuCornerExcluded`, **proved** in session 17) says there is **no
   genuine corner** there — the loop is smooth.
3. **Synge**: for `M` orientable and `n` even, the closed geodesic `c₀` carries a **closed parallel
   unit normal field** `v₀` (the holonomy of `c'^⊥` lies in `SO(n-1)` with `n-1` **odd**, hence
   fixes a vector: `Commons.LinearAlgebra.Orthogonal.fixed_vector_compl_of_det_one` /
   `odd_dim_det_one_fixed_vector`, both already in the commons).
4. The second variation of length along `v₀` gives `I(v₀,v₀) = -∫ sec(v₀,c₀') < 0`, so the
   nearby curves `c_t(s) = exp_{c₀(s)}(t v₀(s))` are **closed** and **strictly shorter**.
5. **Klingenberg's lifting argument**: every point of `c_t` is within `½L(c_t) < inj M ≤ inj(c_t(0))`
   of `c_t(0)`, so `c_t` **lifts** under `exp_{c_t(0)}`; the lifts are equicontinuous (this is where
   `inj < conj` is used) and extend to `t = 0`, producing a **continuous** lift `c̃₀` of `c₀` through
   `0`.  But the continuous lift of a **geodesic** through `0` is the straight line
   `s ↦ s c₀'(0)`, contradicting the periodicity `c̃₀(1) = c̃₀(0) = 0`.

### 112.3 The exact role of orientability — and a route to it that avoids orientation theory

Synge's step (3) needs: **the holonomy of `c'^⊥` has determinant `1`**.  For a closed geodesic `c`
the velocity field `γ'` itself is a parallel field (`pieceSol_deriv`), so the holonomy of the full
tangent space fixes `γ'(0)`; hence `det(P|_{γ'^⊥}) = det P`.  So the needed input is `det P = 1`
for the holonomy `P` of the full tangent space at `c(0)`.

**Lemma (to be formalised).**  If `M` is **simply connected** then every holonomy `P` along a
**closed** curve at `p` has `det P = 1`.

*Proof.*  Fix `p`.  Consider the map `F : Ω_pM → ℝ` given by `F c = det(P_c)`, where `Ω_pM` is the
space of continuous closed curves at `p` (uniform topology) and `P_c` is the parallel transport
along `c`.  (i) `F` is **locally constant**: parallel transport depends continuously on the curve —
the linear ODE `y' = -Γ(c(t))(c'(t))·y` has coefficients that converge uniformly when the curve does,
and Gronwall gives uniform convergence of the solutions (`Commons.Analysis.ODE.gronwall_le`);
`det` is continuous from `O(n)` to the discrete set `{±1}`.  (ii) `Ω_pM` is **path-connected**: `M`
simply connected means every loop is homotopic to a constant loop, and a homotopy gives a path in
`Ω_pM`.  (iii) `F(constant loop) = det id = 1`: the transport along a constant curve solves
`y' = -Γ(y)(0)·y = 0`, i.e. it is the identity (one chart, constant curve — note that the cover
producer `exists_geodesicCover` cannot be used here because it asks for a non-vanishing velocity;
build the one-piece cover of the constant curve by hand).  Hence `F ≡ 1`.

This is **elementary** (no orientation theory, no oriented atlases!) and is the cheapest route to
Synge in this library.  The pieces that still have to be written are:
* the "holonomy" bookkeeping: the `GeodesicCover` of a closed curve (`γ a = γ b`), the transport
  `P_c : T_pM → T_pM` as `v ↦ parallelField … v (c N)` (linear ✓ and norm-preserving ✓ by §112.1),
  and the one-piece cover of the constant curve;
* the continuous dependence of the transport on the curve (Gronwall + the chartwise ODE theory);
* path-connectedness of the loop space of a simply connected space (a topological statement).
Note that the *locally-constant* step needs the *uniform* topology on loops and a uniform bound on
the Christoffel coefficients, i.e. a finite chart cover of `M` — all available
(`ChartPieces`, `ChartBounds`).

### 112.4 The two cases at the closest cut point (`ξ = -η` vs `ξ ≠ -η`) — the corner-cutting argument

The classical "the geodesic loop is a closed geodesic" has the following precise content, which is
worth recording because it shows that **step 2 of §112.2 is not the only route to the contradiction**:

* Let `p` have `inj(p) = inj(M) =: m < conj(p)`.  By the cut-point dichotomy there are two distinct
  minimising vectors `ξ ≠ η` of norm `m` with `GExp p ξ = GExp p η = q`, and (Xu) the resulting
  broken loop `σ_ξ * σ_η⁻¹` has **no corner at `q`**.
* **Case `ξ = -η`**: then the loop is smooth at `p` as well, i.e. `c₀ := s ↦ GExp p (s ξ)` is a
  **closed geodesic** of length `2m` — this is step 2 of §112.2 and Synge applies.
* **Case `ξ ≠ -η`**: the loop has a genuine corner at `p`; **cutting that corner** produces a
  one-parameter family `c_t` of *closed* curves with `L(c_t) < 2m` and `c_t → c₀` uniformly.  The
  lifting argument of step 5 then applies **verbatim** (it only needs closed curves shorter than
  `2 inj M`) and gives a contradiction — because the "canonical lift" of `c₀` is
  `s ↦ μ(c₀(s))` (the unique minimising vector from `p` to `c₀(s)`, which is `sξ` for `s < m` and
  `(2m-s)η` for `s > m`): it **jumps** at `s = m`, contradicting the continuity of a limit of
  equicontinuous lifts.

So in **both** cases one gets a contradiction; the classical proof takes Case `ξ = -η` and uses
Synge, and the corner-cutting argument is the tool for the other case.  Either way, the *hard*
infrastructure is the lifting/equicontinuity argument of steps 4–5, which is
**Klingenberg's** original idea and is independent of the parity of `n`.

### 112.5 Why the ODD-dimensional case is unaffected by all of this

For `n` **odd** the normal space `γ'^⊥` has even dimension and `P ∈ SO(n-1)` need **not** have a
fixed vector (equivalently the holonomy angle `ψ` can be non-zero); the only available shortening
uses the "rotation" modes of §109.3, whose index is `ψ²/L - ∫sec`, negative only for
`L > π/√δ`.  So second-variation methods see only *long* closed geodesics, and the odd case genuinely
needs the **free-loop-space minimax** (Klingenberg 1961) — the wall.  This was checked again this
session:

* the **product trick** `M ↦ M × S¹` (or `M × T²`) does *not* help.  The product metric has
  `sec = 0` on all mixed planes, and although the shortening argument only sees planes
  `span{v₀, c'}` with `c'` the geodesic's velocity, a closed geodesic of `M × S¹` may be a
  "helix" or the circle factor itself, for which the candidate normal field has zero curvature
  pairing.  Moreover `inj(M × S¹) = min{inj(M), inj(S¹)} = inj(M)`, so the structure theorem for
  `M × S¹` gives back exactly the unknown quantity `min{conj M, ½ℓ(M)}` — the argument is
  **circular**.  Recording this saves a future session a lot of time.

