### 112.8 ADDENDUM: the transport API that *is* installed; the endomorphism form must be avoided

`Submission/ParallelGlobal` now also contains (all proved, axiom-clean):

* **`transport`** — the parallel transport along a geodesic read at the right endpoint of a cover:
  `transport C htor hN v := parallelField htor C hN v (C.c C.N)`, with the **dependent** codomain
  `TangentSpace I (γ (C.c C.N))`;
* `transport_add`, `transport_smul`, `transport_norm`, `transport_norm_sq` — the transport is
  **linear** and **norm-preserving** (the norm of the datum being the frame-vector norm
  `‖frameVector (C.x 0) (γ (C.c 0)) v‖`).

**Two gotchas found while trying to package the holonomy as an endomorphism of `T_pM`.**

1. The transport **cannot** be declared with codomain `E`: the goal
   `‖transportEnd v‖ = ‖frameVector … v‖` then uses the *declared* `NormedAddCommGroup E` instance,
   while `norm_parallelField` produces the **Riemannian bundle** instance on
   `TangentSpace I (γ (C.c C.N))`.  The two norms are not syntactically the same (even though
   `TangentSpace I p := E` by definition) — the instance is chosen by the *type ascription*.
   **Keep the dependent codomain.**
2. Coercing the endpoint is *not* possible: with `hclosed : γ (C.c C.N) = γ (C.c 0)` the cast
   `hclosed ▸ x` does not typecheck against the expected codomain `E` (Lean asks for the cast's
   result type to be `E`, and `hclosed`'s sides are points of `M`), and adding the ascription
   `(hclosed ▸ x : TangentSpace I (γ (C.c 0)))` produces a term on which **`cases hclosed` fails**
   with *dependent elimination failed*.  The reason is that `TangentSpace I` is only
   *definitionally* constant, so `Eq.rec` does not compute.

**Consequence.**  Do not build the holonomy as an `E → E` map; instead use `transport` (dependent
codomain) and carry the closedness hypothesis `γ (C.c C.N) = γ (C.c 0)` in the statements that need
it.  The facts about a **closed** geodesic that will actually be needed are:

* *the transport of the velocity is the velocity*: `transport C htor hN (chart coordinates of the
  velocity at c 0) = (the chart coordinates of the velocity at c N)`.  This needs the **cross-chart
  bridge** for the chart velocity, i.e. that
  `frameVector (x k) (γ t) (deriv (coordCurve k) t)` is independent of `k` — equivalently that it
  equals `curveVelocity γ t`.  The library lemma `ChartIndep.frameVector_chartVel_eq_curveVelocity`
  provides this but **requires `MDifferentiableAt 𝓘(ℝ) I γ t`**, which `GeodesicCover` does not
  currently carry.  Two ways out: (a) add a field `mdiff : ∀ k < N, ∀ t ∈ Ioo (A k) (B k),
  MDifferentiableAt 𝓘(ℝ) I γ t` to `GeodesicCover` (filled in the producer from `hγ`, once the
  producer knows `Ioo (A k) (B k) ⊆ V` — it does not, so the field must be proved from `C.ode` +
  `C.src` + `contMDiffOn_extChartAt_symm`, which **is** possible); or (b) define the velocity datum
  *intrinsically* as `chartCoords (x 0) γ (curveVelocity γ) (C.c 0)` and prove the cross-chart
  identity for that datum directly from `ChartCoordsTrans.chartCoords_eq_coordChangeL` plus the chain
  rule for the chart transition.
* *the transport is an isometry on `γ'^⊥`*: this needs the velocity to be a fixed vector of the
  transport, i.e. the previous item, plus `transport_norm`.



### 112.9 ADDENDUM (end of session): THE TRANSPORT FIXES THE VELOCITY — the velocity-holonomy is complete

`Submission/ParallelGlobal` gained (all **proved**, axiom-clean):

* **`mdifferentiableAt_gamma_of_mem_room`** — the geodesic `γ` is `MDifferentiableAt` at every point
  of every chart room `Ioo (A k) (B k)` of its cover.  Proof: on the room the chart coordinate curve
  `u = extChartAt (x k) ∘ γ` has a derivative at every point (`C.ode`) whose derivative is again
  differentiable (`C.ode` again), hence `deriv u` is continuous on the room; the fleet lemma
  `Commons.Analysis.Calculus.contDiffAt_one_of_hasDerivAt` then gives `ContDiffAt ℝ 1 u t`, and
  `contMDiffOn_extChartAt_symm` + `ContMDiffAt.comp` + `γ =ᶠ symm ∘ u` give
  `ContMDiffAt 𝓘(ℝ) I 1 γ t`, hence `MDifferentiableAt` (via `.mdifferentiableAt (by decide)`).
* **`frameVector_deriv_coordCurve_eq_curveVelocity`** — the chart-derivative of the geodesic *is* the
  **intrinsic velocity**: `frameVector (x k) (γ t) (deriv (coordCurve k) t) = curveVelocity γ t`
  (the library bridge `ChartIndep.frameVector_chartVel_eq_curveVelocity` + the previous item).
  This is the **cross-chart bridge for the velocity**: the chart velocity is chart-independent.
* **`nextDatum_deriv`** — the datum conversion maps the chart-`x k` coordinates of the velocity at
  `c k` to the chart-`x (k+1)` coordinates of the velocity at `c (k+1)`.
* **`c_mono_lt`** — **strict** monotonicity of the partition (`i < j ≤ N ⟹ c i < c j`);
* **`kOf_last`** — `kOf C hN (C.c C.N) = C.N - 1`;
* **`datumAt_deriv`** — by induction from `nextDatum_deriv`:
  `datumAt htor C (chart-`x 0` velocity at `c 0`) k = chart-`x k` velocity at `c k` for `k < C.N`;
* **`transport_velocity`** (the headline of this session's second half):
  ```
  transport C htor hN (deriv (extChartAt (C.x 0) ∘ γ) (C.c 0)) = curveVelocity γ (C.c C.N)
  ```
  i.e. **the parallel transport along a geodesic fixes its velocity field**.  For a **closed**
  geodesic (`γ (C.c C.N) = γ (C.c 0)`) this says that the holonomy leaves `γ'` invariant, hence
  preserves `γ'^⊥`, so **Synge's step (3) of §112.2 is now reduced to the determinant theorem of
  §112.3 alone**.

Note (again) that no endpoint coercion was needed anywhere: all these statements are identities of
tangent vectors *at the same point* `γ (C.c C.N)`, so the types match — this is the way to avoid the
trap recorded in §112.8.

### 112.10 The precise remaining plan for the even-dimensional case

1. **The determinant theorem** (§112.3): for `M` simply connected, every holonomy `P` along a closed
   curve at `p` has `det P = 1`.  Needed pieces:
   * the holonomy as a *function of the curve*: `parallelField`/`transport` for the one-piece cover of
     an arbitrary closed curve (the awkward case `γ' ≡ 0` — the constant curve — needs a hand-built
     cover, since `exists_geodesicCover` asks for `curveVelocity ≠ 0`);
   * continuous dependence of the transport on the curve (**Gronwall**, requested and in the commons
     as `Commons.Analysis.ODE.gronwall_le`), with the Christoffel coefficients uniformly bounded on a
     finite chart cover of the compact `M` (`ChartBounds`, `ChartPieces` — all available);
   * `IsLocallyConstant` on the space of loops + path-connectedness of the loop space of a simply
     connected space (`SimplementConnecté` ⟹ every loop is homotopic to a constant loop).
2. **Synge** = `transport_velocity` (done) + the determinant theorem + the odd-dimensional linear
   algebra (`Commons.LinearAlgebra.Orthogonal.fixed_vector_compl_of_det_one`, in the commons):
   for `n` even and `M` orientable the normal holonomy lies in `SO(n-1)` with `n-1` odd, hence fixes
   a non-zero normal vector `v₀`.
3. The **closed-curve second variation**: for the family `Φ(σ,t) = GExp (γ t) (σ v₀(t))` with `v₀` a
   closed parallel unit normal field along a closed geodesic `γ`, the second variation of the energy
   is `E''(0) = -2∫₀^L sec(v₀,γ')`; the chart machinery is
   `SecondVarGeo.energy_second_deriv_chart` (which applies because the **σ-curves
   `σ ↦ GExp (γ t) (σ v₀(t))` are geodesics**), and the jets need `∇_{γ'}v₀ = 0`;
   the conclusion is `L(c_t) < L(c₀)` for small `t ≠ 0`.
4. **Klingenberg's lifting argument** (steps 4–5 of §112.2), including the equicontinuity of the
   lifts.  Together with 1–3 this **proves (K2) for even-dimensional `M`** — a complete theorem in its
   own right (Klingenberg 1959 / Berger's even-dimensional sphere theorem).
5. The **odd** case is unchanged: the free-loop-space minimax (§112.5) — the wall.


## 113. THE SHORTENING STEP OF KLINGENBERG'S ESTIMATE, AND THE PRECISE STATUS OF THE LIFTING ARGUMENT

### 113.1 What was proved this session

**`Submission.DerivShorten`** (namespace `Submission.DerivShorten`):

* **`exists_ne_of_deriv_deriv_neg`** — if `f` is differentiable at `0`, `deriv f` has derivative
  `c < 0` at `0`, and `f` is differentiable near `0`, then `∃ σ ≠ 0, f σ < f 0`.
* **`eventually_lt_or_eventually_lt_of_deriv_deriv_neg`** — the *one-sided* form: under the same
  hypotheses, `f σ < f 0` holds for all `σ` in an interval `(0,δ)` (if `deriv f 0 ≤ 0`) or for all
  `σ` in an interval `(-δ,0)` (if `0 < deriv f 0`).

Both are proved by the slope form `hasDerivAt_iff_tendsto_slope`, the mean value theorem
`exists_deriv_eq_slope` and the sign analysis of `div_neg_iff`/`div_pos_iff`; the proofs do **not**
need `deriv f 0 = 0` (the three cases `deriv f 0 < 0`, `= 0`, `> 0` are treated separately, which is
why the conclusion is a *disjunction*).

**`Submission.EnergyShorten`** (namespace `Submission.EnergyShorten`):

* **`exists_energy_lt_of_integral_neg`** — along a `GExp`-ray `γ(t) = GExp p (t • ξ)` (`ξ ≠ 0`) and
  for a field `V` smooth along the ray (`SmoothAlongAt` at every point), if the **index integral**
  `∫_a^b idxAlong γ V (∇V) < 0`, then there is `d > 0` such that the energy
  `E(σ) = ∫_a^b ‖∂_t Φ(σ,·)‖²` of the geodesic variation `Φ(σ,t) = GExp (γ t) (σ • (d • V t))`
  satisfies `E(σ) < E(0)` **for all small `σ` on one side of `0`**.

The proof: `EnergyRayGlobal.energy_second_deriv_ray'` (a new variant of `energy_second_deriv_ray`
that keeps the *eventual differentiability* of the energy — needed for the mean value theorem; its
building block is the new `EnergyCover.energy_second_deriv_cover_eventually'`) gives
`E''(0) = 2 ∫ idxAlong γ (d•V) (∇(d•V))`; by `IndexScale.idxAlong_smul` and
`IndexScale.covAlong_smul` the integrand is **quadratic** in `d`, so this value is
`2 d² ∫ idxAlong γ V (∇V) < 0`; then `DerivShorten.eventually_lt_or_eventually_lt_of_deriv_deriv_neg`
applies.  Both new theorems are axiom-clean (`propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound`).

**The geometric form of the shortening** (also this session, `Submission.EnergyShorten`):

* **`idxAlong_eq_neg_of_parallel`** — for a parallel field, `idxAlong γ V (∇V) t = -⟨R(V,γ')γ',V⟩`.
* **`integral_idxAlong_neg_of_parallel`** — with `γ` `C^∞` (nonvanishing velocity), `V` smooth and
  parallel along `γ`, and a *positive* curvature pairing on `[a,b]`, the index integral is `< 0`
  (uses `ConjEnergy.continuousOn_idxAlong_of_smooth` and the commons lemma
  `Commons.MeasureTheory.IntervalIntegral.integral_pos_of_continuousOn_of_pos`, both available now).
* **`exists_energy_lt_of_positive_curvature`** — the same for a `GExp`-ray (the composition of the
  previous point with the abstract shortening).
* **`exists_curveLength_lt_of_positive_curvature`** — **the length form**: for a *unit-speed*
  `GExp`-ray of length `L > 0` and a parallel field with positive curvature pairing, there is `d > 0`
  such that `curveLength (t ↦ GExp (γ t) (σ • (d • V t))) 0 L < L` for all small `σ` on one side of
  `0`.  The transfer from energy to length is Cauchy–Schwarz
  (`Commons.MeasureTheory.IntervalIntegral.sq_integral_le_mul_integral_sq`, proved by the fleet this
  session): `L(c_σ) = ∫ curveSpeed ≤ √(L · E(σ)) < √(L·L) = L` since `E(σ) < E(0) = L`.  (An
  alternative route avoiding Cauchy–Schwarz: AM–GM, `curveSpeed ≤ (1 + curveSpeed²)/2`, gives
  `L(c_σ) ≤ (L + E(σ))/2 < L`; both need only the continuity of the speed — which
  `EnergyRaySmooth.contMDiffOn_ray_family` provides.)

**What this buys.**  In the intended application `V = v₀` is a *closed parallel unit normal field*
along a unit-speed closed geodesic (`∇v₀ = 0`, `‖v₀‖ = 1`), so
`idxAlong γ v₀ (∇v₀) = -⟨R(v₀,γ')γ',v₀⟩ = -sec(v₀,γ') < 0` when the curvature is positive: the index
integral is *negative*, and the theorem produces the family of shorter curves.  The length form of the statement
(`exists_curveLength_lt_of_positive_curvature`) is therefore available as well:
**the shortening step 4 of §112.2 is DONE** (modulo the three mechanical instantiations
described below).

**What is still missing to *instantiate* it** (both are honest gaps, not obstacles of principle):
1. the field `v₀` must satisfy `SmoothAlongAt γ v₀ s` for every `s`: i.e. *the parallel field along a
   geodesic is `C^∞` along it* — the ingredients are `ParallelGlobal.parallelField_piece`,
   `CoeffSmooth2.contDiffOn_of_jacobiSolution` and `Coeff.contDiffOn_gammaOp_along_Ioo`;
2. `covAlong cov v₀ = 0` for the parallel field (the chart form is `ParallelGlobal.pieceSol_ode`);
3. the **geometric** positivity hypothesis of `exists_curveLength_lt_of_positive_curvature`: from
   `PinchUniform.exists_uniform_lt_curvVec` (uniform strict pinching gives a *uniform positive lower
   bound* `c > 0` on `⟨R(u,v)v,u⟩` over orthonormal pairs: the library form is
   `inner ℝ u u = 1 → inner ℝ v v = 1 → inner ℝ u v = 0 → c < inner ℝ (curvVec cov p u v v) u`) one
   discharges it for `u = V t`, `v = γ'(t)` once `V` is unit and `⊥ γ'`.  Note that then the whole
   shortening is *unconditional* except for the existence of the closed parallel unit normal field —
   which is **Synge** (item 2), i.e. the determinant theorem.

### 113.2 The LIFTING ARGUMENT: a correction, and the S² test case (IMPORTANT for the next session)

§112.2 step 5 and §112.10 item 4 claim that "the lifts `c̃_t` of the shorter curves are
**equicontinuous**".  This session's analysis shows that the naive reading of this is **false**, and
that a future session must not attempt to prove it in that form:

* the lifts that the classical argument uses are the *unique preimages of `c_t(s)` in the ball of
  radius `inj(c_t(0))`* — i.e. the **minimising** vectors (this is forced: the norm of the preimage
  in the injectivity ball equals the distance `d(c_t(0), c_t(s))`, by `InjBall.bijOn_GExp_ball` and
  the `ofReal_norm_eq_edist_of_GExp_eq` bridge);
* **concrete test case (the round `S²`).**  `p` the north pole, `c₀` the great circle of length `2π`,
  `m = π`, and `c_σ` the family of *parallel circles* `t ↦ exp_{c₀(t)}(σ v₀(t))` (`v₀` the normal
  field), which converge uniformly to `c₀` and have `L(c_σ) = 2π cos σ < 2π = 2 inj(S²)`.  The lifts
  `c̃_σ` (preimages under `exp_p`, valued in the ball of radius `π`) converge **pointwise** to
  `σ ↦ 2πt·ξ` for `t ∈ [0,1/2]` and `σ ↦ (2πt - 2π)·ξ` for `t ∈ [1/2,1]` — which **jumps by `2πξ`
  at `t = 1/2`** (the antipode, where `exp_p` is not a local diffeo: `conj(S²) = π`).  Hence the
  convergence is **not uniform** and the family `(c̃_σ)` is **not** equicontinuous (its Lipschitz
  constants blow up like `1/σ`).  Note that `S²` satisfies `inj = conj`, so no contradiction is
  expected there — the *point* of the example is that the *method* (equicontinuity of the lifts)
  cannot work as stated;
* what the classical argument (Abresch–Meyer, proof of Lemma 4.3) actually lifts is **the whole
  family** `c : R × [0,1] → M` as a *continuous map* `c̃ : [-1/2,1/2] × [0,1] → TM` with
  `exp ∘ c̃ = c` and `c̃(0,t) = 0 ∈ T_{c_t(0)}M`: i.e. the **homotopy lifting property of the local
  diffeomorphism `exp : TM → M` for a simply connected source**, not equicontinuity of a family of
  curves.  Two facts are then used:
  * (i) `c̃(s,1) = 0` (the curve `c_1` is constant), hence `c̃(-1/2,t) = c̃(1/2,t)` for all `t`;
  * (ii) for `t = 0` one of the arcs `c₀[-1/2,0]`, `c₀[0,1/2]` lifts to a **radial straight line** of
    length `½L(c₀)`, and the other arc lifts to a curve with the same endpoints and the same length;
    in a *Euclidean* tangent space a curve of length equal to `|w|` between `0` and `w` **is** the
    straight segment, so the two arcs coincide — contradicting that `c₀` is a **nontrivial digon**.
  So the missing infrastructure is: **(a)** the lifting of a continuous map from a simply connected
  domain under `exp` (equivalently: `exp` restricted to the ball of radius `conj` is a **covering
  map** onto its image, or a hand-rolled lifting argument along a grid), and **(b)** the length
  identity "the exponential does not increase the length of the *lift* along an arc shorter than the
  conjugate radius" (this is `∫‖(d exp)_{c̃} c̃'‖ ≥ ∫‖c̃'‖` in the form needed, i.e. a **Rauch I**
  (norm lower bound) statement, for which `Commons.Analysis.ODE.sturm_model_lower_bound_general`
  (session 4) is the analytic input).
* **`S²` also shows that the endpoint of the lifting argument is not "periodicity vs straight line"**
  (§112.2's phrasing): on `S²` the "tent" lift is periodic in the sense `c̃(1) = c̃(0) = 0` and there
  is no contradiction — because `S²` has `inj = conj`; the contradiction in the setting
  `inj < conj` comes from the **jump** of the limit lift at the cut time (the two branches `t·ξ` and
  `(2m-t)·η` have different values at `t = m` whenever `ξ ≠ η`).

### 113.3 What the even-dimensional case now needs (updated list, ordered)

1. **The determinant theorem** (`det P = 1` for the holonomy of a closed curve in a simply connected
   manifold) — §112.3; still the crux.  Status of the possible routes: the "geodesic cone" contraction
   of a closed geodesic to `p` is **not continuous at the cut locus** (the minimising vector jumps),
   so that route is dead; the surviving routes are (a) orientability + the volume-form/sign argument
   (needs "simply connected ⟹ orientable", i.e. the orientation double cover, which Mathlib does not
   have), (b) general parallel transport along curves + homotopy invariance (needs a **smooth**
   null-homotopy, i.e. smooth approximation of maps into a manifold — Mathlib has
   `SmoothApprox` only for maps *into a normed space*), (c) the geodesic-polygon approximation of the
   homotopy (needs continuity of the transport in the endpoints of a geodesic segment).
