## 115. THE DETERMINANT THEOREM `det P = 1`: THE ANALYSIS, THE REDUCTION, AND STAGE 1 (DONE)

### 115.1 The statement and why it is needed

`Submission.ParallelGlobal.transport` gives the holonomy of a closed geodesic as a linear isometry
`P` of `T_pM` fixing `γ'`.  Synge's step of the even-dimensional case of Klingenberg's estimate
(§112.2) needs:

> for `n = dim M` **even**, `P` has a nonzero fixed vector in `γ'^⊥`
> (equivalently `P|_{γ'^⊥}` has eigenvalue `1`; `γ'^⊥` is odd-dimensional).

The classical route is `det P = 1` (then `P ∈ SO(n)` and `Commons.LinearAlgebra.Orthogonal.
fixed_vector_compl_of_det_one` applies: an orthogonal map of an even-dimensional space fixing
`e ≠ 0` with `det = 1` fixes a nonzero vector orthogonal to `e`).

`det P` is well defined without any orientation theory: `P` is an isometry of the single space
`T_pM`, so once an orthonormal basis is chosen, `det P` is basis-independent and `= ±1`.

**Necessity of a topological input.**  On the Möbius band the holonomy of the core circle is
orientation-reversing (`det = -1`).  So `det P = 1` is *not* a chart-level statement; the topology
of `M` (here: `SimplyConnectedSpace M`) must enter.

### 115.2 THE REDUCTION: `det P` is the product of the CHART-TRANSITION SIGNS

Let `c : [0,L] → M` be a closed curve, `c(0) = c(L) = p`, and cover `[0,L]` by subintervals
`[t_k, t_{k+1}]`, each contained in a chart `U_k`, with the first and last chart chosen equal (always
possible since `c` returns to `p`).  Write `Φ_k` for the chart-level parallel transport of piece `k`
(a map between the tangent spaces at `c(t_k)`, `c(t_{k+1})`, given in chart coordinates by the
fundamental solution of `y' = -Γ̂ y`), and choose as basis of `T_{c(t_j)}M` the **coordinate basis of
the chart `U_j` of the piece starting there**.  Then, because the matrix of a composite is the
product of the matrices in a *matching* sequence of bases,

  `[P] = [Φ_m] ⋯ [Φ_1]`,  hence  `det P = ∏_k det [Φ_k]`,

and each factor decomposes as

  `det [Φ_k] = (chart determinant of Φ_k) · (sign of the Jacobian of the chart transition
  U_{k-1} → U_k at the junction c(t_k))`.

The first factor is **positive** — that is exactly §115.3 — so

> **`det P = ∏_k ε_k` with `ε_k ∈ {±1}` the signs of the chart-transition Jacobians at the
> junctions.**

**This is the crux, and it is NOT the chart-level determinant.**  The chart-level part contributes a
positive factor; only the transition signs can make `det P = -1`.  Consequences:

* If the curve is covered by a **single** chart (or by charts whose consecutive transitions all have
  positive Jacobian at the junctions), then `det P = 1`.  On a *trivialised* manifold (e.g. `ℝⁿ`,
  or any manifold with a global frame) this settles `det P = 1` for **all** closed curves.
* On the Möbius band, the two transitions of a two-chart cover of the core circle have signs `+1`
  and `-1`, product `-1` ✓ — consistent.
* For a **simply connected** `M` one must show that the product is `+1` for every closed curve.
  Two routes, both recorded below (§115.5).

### 115.3 STAGE 1, DONE: `Submission/FundSol.lean` — the chart transport has positive determinant

New module (namespace `Submission.FundSol`, imported by `Submission.lean`, axiom-clean):

| name | statement |
| --- | --- |
| `chartOpCoeff` | the operator coefficient `Φ ↦ A t ∘ Φ`, `A t = -Γ̂(t)` |
| `exists_chartOpSol` | existence **and uniqueness** of `Φ' = A(t) ∘ Φ`, `Φ a = 1`, in the Banach space `E →L[ℝ] E` |
| **`fundSol`** | the fundamental solution of the chart parallel equation on `[a,b]` |
| `fundSol_datum`, `fundSol_hasDerivAt` | `Φ a = 1` and the operator ODE |
| `fundSol_vector_hasDerivAt` | `t ↦ Φ t v` solves the **vector** ODE `y' = -Γ̂(t)y` |
| `fundSol_eqOn` | **uniqueness**: any vector solution with datum `v` at `a` equals `t ↦ Φ t v` |
| **`fundSol_injective`** | `Φ t` is injective on `[a,b]` (a linear-ODE solution vanishing at one point vanishes identically) |
| `fundSol_det_ne_zero` | `(Φ t).det ≠ 0` |
| `continuous_linearMap_det` | `f ↦ LinearMap.det f.toLinearMap` is continuous on `E →L[ℝ] E` |
| `fundSol_continuousOn_det` | `t ↦ (Φ t).det` is continuous on `[a,b]` |
| **`fundSol_det_pos`** | **`0 < (Φ t).det` for all `t ∈ [a,b]`** |

The last is the statement that **the chart-level parallel transport is orientation preserving**:
`Φ t` acts on the line `ΛⁿE` by the positive scalar `(Φ t).det`.  Proof: `t ↦ (Φ t).det` is
continuous (the determinant is a continuous function of a continuous linear map — proved through the
matrix in `Module.finBasis`), never vanishes, and equals `1` at `t = a`; the IVT on the connected
interval `[a,b]` closes it.

**Design note that made this cheap**: solving the ODE **in the operator algebra** `E →L[ℝ] E` makes
the linearity of `Φ t` free and gives the ODE directly; solving for one vector at a time would have
required `Classical.choose` and separate linearity proofs, and would only be linear on `[a,b]`
(the chosen functions are junk outside).  Note also that `fundSol` is *not* tied to geodesics: it
applies to any chart curve (`u w : ℝ → E` with only continuity hypotheses), which is what the
curve-level transport of §115.5 will need.

### 115.4 What is now available, and what is missing

Available (all proved, axiom-clean):
* the chart-level transport and its **positive determinant** (§115.3);
* the global parallel field along a **geodesic** and its smoothness/parallelism (`ParallelRoom`);
* the parallel transport along a **geodesic** as a linear isometry (`ParallelGlobal.transport`,
  `transport_velocity`);
* the **uniqueness of the parallel field** on an interval (`ParallelRoom.eqOn_of_parallel`) — the
  chart-free tool that will make the curve-level transport consistent.

Missing, and this is the whole remaining content of `det P = 1`:
1. **the parallel transport along an arbitrary smooth curve** (§115.5, Step A);
2. **the multiplicativity of the transport over a grid** and the resulting
   `det P = ∏ (cell holonomy determinants)` (§115.5, Step B);
3. the **null-homotopy** of the closed curve (from `SimplyConnectedSpace M` via
   `Path.Homotopic`/`Subsingleton (FundamentalGroup M p)`) and the local triviality of the cell
   holonomy (§115.5, Step C).

### 115.5 The plan for `det P = 1` (the three steps, in order)

**Step A — the transport along an arbitrary smooth curve.**
For a `C^∞` curve `γ : ℝ → M` on `[a,b]` with nonvanishing velocity, build
`curveTransport : T_{γ(a)}M → T_{γ(b)}M`:

* **the cover**: a *`SmoothCurveCover`* — the same data as `ParallelGlobal.GeodesicCover`
  (partition `c k`, charts `x k`, rooms `A k < c k < c(k+1) < B k`, target membership) but with the
  geodesic-equation field `ode` **replaced by** `ContDiffOn ℝ ∞ (coordCurve k) (Ioo (A k) (B k))`
  and `ContDiffOn ℝ ∞ (deriv (coordCurve k)) (…)`.  Such a cover exists for *any* `C^∞` curve
  (take `V := curveVelocity` in `SmoothFieldCover.exists_partition_smoothField`, or generalise
  `ChartPieces.exists_chart_pieces` by dropping `hgeo`).
* **the piecewise field**: literally `ParallelRoom`'s construction (`pieceSolR`, `pieceFieldR`, the
  germ lemmas, `covAlong_eq_zero_of_germ`, `parallelFieldR_spec`) with the `ode`-derived smoothness
  replaced by the two `ContDiffOn` hypotheses.  `ParallelRoom` uses the geodesic equation **only**
  in `contDiffOn_coordPair` and `contMDiffOn_gamma_room`; every other lemma is geodesy-free.
* **the transport**: chain the chart transports `fundSol_0, …, fundSol_{m-1}` through the chart
  changes.  The consistency (independence of the cover) follows from
  `ParallelRoom.eqOn_of_parallel` (uniqueness of the parallel field).
* **multiplicativity**: for a concatenation `γ = γ₁ * γ₂`, `curveTransport γ = curveTransport γ₂ ∘
  curveTransport γ₁`.

**Step B — the grid.**
For the homotopy `H : I × I → M` (from Step C), choose a grid so that each cell maps into a chart
(Lebesgue number + `isCompact_elim_finite_subcover`).  Then the holonomy of `∂(I × I)` is the
product of the cell holonomies *with the interior edge transports cancelling*, and since each cell
holonomy is a self-map of a single tangent space, the determinants multiply:
`det P_c = ∏_{cells} det (cell holonomy)`.  (Each cancellation is a genuine equality of linear maps
`T_xM → T_xM`, because both directions give the *same* transport along the same curve by
uniqueness — Step A.  This is the "holonomy is multiplicative over a grid" fact.)

**Step C — the cells are trivial.**
For a cell contained in a chart `U`, the holonomy of its boundary is, in the coordinates of `U`,
the product of four (or three) chart-level fundamental solutions — i.e. a product of maps with
**positive determinant** (§115.3) — hence positive determinant; as a self-map of the odd/even
tangent space it is an isometry (`ParallelGlobal.transport_norm` for the generalisation), so its
determinant is `±1`, hence `+1` ✓.

Then `det P_c = ∏ 1 = 1` ✓, with `c` null-homotopic (from `SimplyConnectedSpace M`), which is the
only place the simple connectivity is used.

### 115.6 The alternative route (orientability), and why the current plan avoids it

Classically one argues: `M` simply connected ⟹ orientable ⟹ there is a nowhere-vanishing `n`-form
`ω`; transporting `ω` along `c` gives `ω_L = f(L)·ω`, with `f` continuous and never zero and
`f(0) = 1`, so `f > 0`, hence `det P > 0`, hence `= 1`.  This is *shorter* than Steps A–C **but it
needs "simply connected ⟹ orientable"**, i.e. the theory of the orientation double cover
(equivalently: that `ΛⁿT*M` is trivial), which Mathlib does not have and which is itself a
covering-space-theory project.  Steps A–C use only the homotopy, which `SimplyConnectedSpace` gives
directly.

### 115.7 Gotchas of this session (the ones that cost time)

* **Solving a linear ODE in the operator algebra** `E →L[ℝ] E` is far easier than solving it
  vector-wise: `Φ a = 1` and linearity are then free, and `E →L[ℝ] E` inherits
  `NormedAddCommGroup/NormedSpace/CompleteSpace` from `E`.  The coefficient is
  `ContinuousLinearMap.compL ℝ E E E (A t)`, whose defining equation is
  `compL ℝ E E E g f = g.comp f` (a `rfl`).
* `HasDerivAt.clm_apply (c) (u) : HasDerivAt (fun y => c y (u y)) (c' (u x) + c x u') x` is the
  product rule that extracts the **vector** ODE from the operator ODE (with `u := fun _ => v`).
* **The coercion `↑f` of a `ContinuousLinearMap` to a function and the `FunLike` application
  `f x` are not syntactically the same**, and this breaks `rw` (it works with `simp`/`simpa`).
  Symptom: `rw [hv]` fails with "pattern `↑(fundSol … t) v` not found in `(fundSol … t) v = …`".
  Workaround: restate the hypothesis with `LinearMap.mem_ker.mp`/`simpa using`.
* `Module.Basis.coord i` (the coordinate functional) is the way to get continuity of the entries of
  `LinearMap.toMatrix`: `LinearMap.toMatrix_apply` plus `rw`/`rfl` (the `@[simps]`-generated lemma
  `Module.Basis.coord_apply` leaves a `↑f` vs `f` mismatch that `simp only` cannot bridge).
* `Submodule.ne_bot_iff (p)` takes the submodule **explicitly**.
* `intermediate_value_Icc'` (rather than `intermediate_value_Icc`) is the one that applies when
  `f a > f b`; for a function that starts positive and is suspected negative, apply it on the
  interval `Icc a t` with the goal `0 ∈ Icc (f t) (f a)`.
* `Icc a t ⊆ Icc a b` is proved by hand (`fun s hs => ⟨hs.1, le_trans hs.2 ht.2⟩`), not by
  `Icc_subset_Icc_right` (which has the other orientation).
* `Basis.coord` is `Module.Basis.coord` (the basis structure lives in the `Module` namespace).
* Development: `Sc3.lean` at the workspace root → `Submission/FundSol.lean`.


