### 116.2 WHAT IS *NOT* TRUE, AND WHY THE GRID ARGUMENT MUST BE USED ON ITS OWN FAMILY

**The τ-partition of a grid cannot be prescribed.**  A tempting simplification — "ask the grid
lemma for a grid whose `t`-partition is exactly the partition of the transport cover of the geodesic"
— is **false in general**: a cell `[s_i, s_{i+1}] × [u_j, u_{j+1}]` with prescribed (possibly long)
τ-extent must map into a single chart, and for `i ≥ 1` nothing forces `F (·, τ)` to stay in a chart
over that τ-interval even when `F (0, ·) = γ` does (take `F (σ, τ) = e^{2πi(1+σ)τ}` on `S¹` with the
prescribed partition coming from `γ`).  Consequently:
* the grid argument must be applied to the grid's **own** τ-partition and chart family, and
* to connect it with the transport cover of the geodesic, **partition independence** is needed
  (§116.3), or the transport must be built from the grid's family (§116.4).

### 116.3 THE REMAINING WORK, ROUTE 1: PARTITION INDEPENDENCE

By §115.2 the intended relation is `det P = (positive) · loopSignOf`, so the sign of the holonomy is
*the* `loopSignOf` of **any** chart-covered loop.  Hence:

> **Theorem (partition independence).**  For a continuous closed curve `γ` at `p` (continuous on
> `[0,1]`, `γ 0 = γ 1`), any two valid chart families `(P, z)`, `(P', z')` give the same
> `loopSignOf`.

* the two *same-partition* comparisons are `loopSignOf_congr` (different charts, same partition) and
  — still to do — the **subdivision lemma**: if `Q` refines `P` (every `P`-junction is a `Q`-junction)
  and `Z k := z (ψ k)` where `ψ k` is the index of the `P`-piece containing the `Q`-piece `k`, then
  `loopSignOf Q γ Z = loopSignOf P γ z`.  Proof: the *new* junctions contribute
  `chartSign (z j) (z j) (·) = 1` (`chartSign_self`), and the surviving factors are exactly the
  `P`-factors; since `ψ (k+1) ∈ {ψ k, ψ k + 1}` (strict monotonicity of both partitions), the
  product splits along the jumps (`Finset.prod_range_add`).
* a **common refinement** (junctions = union of the two junction sets, charts inherited from the
  containing pieces) then gives both `(Q, Z)` (refining `P`) and `(Q, Z')` (refining `P'`), and
  `loopSignOf_congr` closes `loopSignOf Q γ Z = loopSignOf Q γ Z'`.

### 116.4 REMAINING WORK, ROUTE 2: THE TRANSPORT FROM AN ARBITRARY VALID FAMILY

Alternatively, build the parallel transport along `γ` **directly from a given valid chart family
`(P, z)`** (this is what the grid supplies):

* `chartTrans k := fundSol` on the piece `k` (`FundSol.fundSol`), with `u := extChartAt (z k) ∘ γ`,
  `w := deriv u`; `det (chartTrans k) > 0` (`fundSol_det_pos`);
* the junction transition is literally `chartTransition (z k) (z (k+1)) (γ (t (k+1)))`, so
  `chartDet`/`chartSign` are *definitionally* the determinant/sign of the transition;
* the composite `P := (closing transition) ∘ Φ_{L-1} ∘ τ_{L-1} ∘ ⋯ ∘ τ_0 ∘ Φ_0` (a linear map
  `E → E` in the `z 0`-chart at `p`) satisfies, by `LinearMap.det_comp` and the positivity above,
  `sign (det P) = loopSignOf P γ z`;
* the concurrent definition of the parallel field `X` (piecewise
  `X t = frameVector (z k) (γ t) (Φ_k(t) v)`) gives `X (γ b) = P v`, `X` parallel, `‖X‖ = ‖v‖`, and
  `X` fixes the velocity field — exactly the Synge input (a periodic unit normal parallel field).
Both routes are viable; Route 2 avoids §116.3 entirely at the cost of not reusing
`ParallelGlobal.transport`.

