### 126.3 ★ THE NEGATIVE FINDING: THE "EQUICONTINUITY" STEP OF THE EVEN-CASE PROOF IS FALSE AS STATED

Abresch–Meyer's survey proof of Theorem 1.5 (Klingenberg 1959: `M` compact even-dimensional
orientable with `sec > 0` ⟹ `inj M = conj M`) reads:

> "the nearby curves `c_t : R/Z → M`, `s ↦ exp_{c_0(s)} t v_0(s)`, are strictly shorter than `c_0`
> … At this point Klingenberg observes that the image of such a curve `c_t` is contained in the closed
> ball with radius `½L(c_t) < inj M` centered at `c_t(0)`, and thus he can lift `c_t` under
> `exp_{c_t(0)}` … **By assumption, `inj M < conj M`, and thus the curves `c̃_t` define an
> equicontinuous map `c̃ : R/Z × (0, ε) → TM`** … Extending this map continuously to `R/Z × [0, ε)`,
> one obtains a lift `c̃_0` of `c_0` … On the other hand the lift of the geodesic `c_0` must be the
> line `s ↦ s c_0'(0)`, contradicting the periodicity of `c̃_0`."

**The equicontinuity claim in it is false, and the failure is *unavoidable*.**  Explicit computation
(round `S²`, `p_eq = (1,0,0)`, `c_t` = the circle of colatitude `π/2 - t`, `c_0` = the equator of
length `2π`, `inj = conj = π`):

* the minimising lift of the *limit* geodesic `c_0` is forced to have the minimising norms
  `‖c̃_0(s)‖ = d(p_eq, c_0(s)) = min(2πs, 2π(1-s))`, hence **jumps** at `s = 1/2` (from `π·ξ'` to
  `-π·ξ'`); so *no continuous lift with the minimising norms exists* — this part of the argument is
  fine, and it does not use `inj < conj` at all;
* but the *family* `c̃_t` is **not equicontinuous**: with the spherical triangle `N A B` (sides
  `NA = NB = π/2 - t`, angle `2πs` at `N`) the initial bearing of the minimising geodesic from `A` to
  `B_s` satisfies `cos A(s) = tan t · tan(d(s)/2)` with `cos d(s) = cos²t cos 2πs + sin²t`; near
  `s = 1/2` this gives `A² ≈ ε/t²` where `ε = (π - 2t) - d(s) ≈ (π²/t) δ²`, whence the "transition
  region" where `A` swings from `0` to order `1` has width `δ ≈ t^{3/2} → 0`.  So the lifts' modulus
  of continuity degenerates like `t^{-3/2}`, and no uniform limit exists.  Nothing in the computation
  uses `inj = conj` (only `L(c_t) < 2 inj`, which holds for *every* `t > 0`), so the same degeneration
  occurs in the hypothetical `inj M < conj M` situation.

**Consequences for the plan (this supersedes §113.2 and §124.3 items 2–4).**

1. The even case **cannot** be closed by the naive "minimising lifts + equicontinuity" argument.  What
   the published proofs actually use is one of:
   * the **long homotopy lemma** (Abresch–Meyer Lemma 4.3 / Klingenberg 1962 p. 50 / Shankar–Sormani
     arXiv:0709.2331 Theorem 6.2), which lifts **the whole one-parameter family of curves at once**
     (a homotopy-lifting statement for `exp : TM → M`, not a statement about the individual lifts),
     together with the conjugate-radius lift of a curve of length `< conj` — which needs **Rauch I**
     (`‖(d exp) c̃'‖ ≥ ‖c̃'‖` below the conjugate radius, i.e. `d exp` is length-non-decreasing);
   * and then the **minimax** (Palais–Smale condition C + the degenerate Morse lemma) producing a
     closed geodesic `c̄_0` of length `≥ 2 conj M` with index `≤ 1`, contradicted by the **index
     estimate** `ind(c̄_0) ≥ n - 1 ≥ 2` from the Dirichlet test fields `sin(πs/L) X_j`.
2. Note the long homotopy lemma applies to a **nontrivial geodesic digon** of length `< 2 conj M` —
   and the two halves of a closed geodesic `c_0` of length `2 inj(M) < 2 conj(M)` *are* such a digon
   (two distinct minimising geodesics between `c_0(0)` and `c_0(1/2)`).  So the even case can be run
   through **the same** `LHL + minimax + index` route as the odd case; the "Synge shortcut" of §112.2
   is not needed (Synge is still what makes `inj M ≤ conj M` fail in the even case, i.e. what produces
   the short closed geodesic in the first place — but the contradiction then comes from LHL + minimax).
3. The lifting technology proved in §126.1 remains exactly what the *minimising* (injectivity-radius)
   variants of these arguments use — in particular the **parametric lift** (next step).

