## §127 SESSION 27 — ★ THE EVEN-DIMENSIONAL KLINGENBERG ESTIMATE: A CORRECT AND MUCH SIMPLER PROOF FOUND IN THE LITERATURE (this CORRECTS §126.3)

### 127.1 THE FINDING

Session 26 (§126.3) concluded, correctly, that the *equicontinuity step* of Abresch–Meyer's sketch of
Klingenberg 1959 (Theorem 1.5: `M` compact even-dimensional orientable, `sec > 0` ⟹ `inj M = conj M`)
is false as stated.  That conclusion stands, **but the pessimistic corollary drawn from it — that the
even case has to go through the free-loop-space minimax (the wall) — is WRONG**.

The correct proof is in the lecture notes of Zuogiang Wang (USTC, *The Sphere Theorem*,
`staff.ustc.edu.cn/~wangzuoq/Courses/16S-RiemGeom/Notes/Lec22.pdf`).  It uses **compactness instead of
equicontinuity**, and it needs **no minimax at all**.  The classical ingredients are exactly the ones
this project already has:

* **Proposition 1.1 (the structure at the closest cut point).**  Let `q ∈ Cut(p)` with
  `d(p,q) = d(p, Cut(p)) = inj(p)`.  Then either `q` is conjugate to `p` along a minimising geodesic,
  or **any two distinct minimising geodesics from `p` to `q` arrive at `q` with opposite velocities**
  (hence there are exactly two, and they are the two halves of one geodesic).  *Proof*: if `γ`, `σ`
  are two such geodesics with `γ̇(l) ≠ -σ̇(l)`, pick `X ∈ T_qM` with `⟨X, γ̇(l)⟩ < 0` and
  `⟨X, σ̇(l)⟩ < 0` (`q` not conjugate ⟹ `exp_p` is a local diffeo at `lγ̇(0)`, so one may push the
  endpoint out along `exp_q(sX)`); the first variation formula gives two geodesics from `p` to
  `exp_q(sX)` whose lengths are `< l`, i.e. two *distinct* vectors of norm `< l = inj(p)` with the
  same image — contradicting `InjAtLeast p l`.
  **IN THIS PROJECT THIS IS `XuTrap.xuCornerExcluded`** (Xu's Theorem B, arXiv:1401.5549), which is
  *sharper*: it only needs the non-conjugacy along one of the two geodesics, and gives the
  opposite-velocity conclusion for any two minimising vectors of norm `inj(p)` with the same image.
  The non-conjugacy is free below `π/√K` by Rauch
  (`ConjCut.not_isConjugateTime_of_lt_pi_div_sqrt`).

* **The even case.**  Suppose `m := inj(M) = inf_p inj(p) < π/√K`.  Take `p` with `inj(p) = m` and
  `q` a cut point of `p` at distance `m` (`q` not conjugate to `p` since `m < π/√K ≤ conj`).  The
  cut-point step gives two distinct minimising vectors `mξ, mη` (`ξ ≠ η` unit) with the same image
  `q`.  By Proposition 1.1 (at `p`) their velocities at `q` are opposite, `w₁ = -w₂`; applying
  Proposition 1.1 **also at `q`** (legitimate because `inj(q) = m` — see §127.2) gives `η = -ξ`.
  Then the concatenation `p → q → p` is a **smooth closed geodesic `Γ` of length `2m`** (§127.3).
  Since `M` is even-dimensional and orientable, **Synge** gives a periodic parallel unit normal field
  `Z` with `-∫⟨R(Z,Γ')Γ',Z⟩ < 0`, so the closed curves `Γ_σ(t) = exp_{Γ(t)}(σ Z(t))` have
  `L(Γ_σ) < 2m` for small `σ ≠ 0` (this is already a theorem in this project:
  `SyngeWindow.exists_shorter_closed_curve_of_synge`, modulo the normalisation §127.4).
  **The final contradiction is compactness, not equicontinuity** (§127.5).

### 127.2 THE ATTAINMENT OF `inf_p inj(p)` — A CLEAN LIMITING ARGUMENT, NO CONTINUITY OF `inj` NEEDED

The published proof takes for granted that `p ↦ inj(p)` attains its infimum.  We do not need to prove
continuity: the extraction does the job.

Let `m := injSup hmet` (= `sSup {r | InjAllAtLeast hmet r}` = `inf_p inj(p)`), so
`InjAllAtLeast hmet m` and `¬InjAllAtLeast hmet r` for `r > m`.  Choose `p_n` with
`m_n := inj(p_n) < m + 1/(n+1)`.  At each `p_n` the cut-point step
(`RayConj.exists_two_minimising_of_injAtLeast_of_lt_pi_div_sqrt`) gives `u_n ≠ v_n` of norm `m_n`
with a common image, both rays minimising.  Write `u_n = m_n ξ_n`, `v_n = m_n η_n` (`ξ_n ≠ η_n`
unit) and let `q_n` be the common image.  Xu's Theorem B gives `w₁,n = -w₂,n` (velocities at `q_n`).
Pass to a subsequence with `p_n → p` (compactness of `M`), `m_n → m`, `ξ_n → ξ`, `η_n → η`
(compactness of the unit sphere *in a chart at `p`*, using `GExpJoint.contMDiffOn_GExp_frameVector`
for the base-point dependence).  Then:

* `GExp p (mξ) = GExp p (mη) =: q` and both rays are minimising at `m` (limits of equalities);
* **`ξ ≠ η`** — otherwise the limiting relation `w₁ = -w₂` would force
  `curveVelocity (ray ξ, m) = -curveVelocity (ray ξ, m) = 0`, contradicting unit speed;
* `InjAtLeast p m` (from `InjAllAtLeast hmet m`) and **`¬InjAtLeast p r` for every `r > m`**,
  because `mξ ≠ mη` are two distinct vectors of norm `m < r` with the same image.
  In particular `inj(p) = m`: **the infimum is attained, and `m` is the maximal injectivity radius
  at `p`** — with no appeal to continuity of `inj`.

So the point `p` we need is produced by compactness.  (`exists_injAtLeast_max` is then not even
needed for `p`; it is needed only to *phrase* the cut-point step.)

### 127.3 FROM THE FLUSH CONFIGURATION TO A SMOOTH CLOSED GEODESIC

Let `p`, `m > 0`, `ξ`, `η` unit with `ξ ≠ η`, `GExp p (mξ) = GExp p (mη) = q`, `w₁ = -w₂` (velocities
at `q`) and `η = -ξ`.  Put `β(t) := GExp p (t • ξ)` (`t ∈ ℝ`); `β` is a unit-speed geodesic on all of
ℝ (`GExpRayZero.isGeodesicAt_GExp_smul_all`).  Then:

* `β(m) = q` and `β(-m) = GExp p (m • (-ξ)) = GExp p (mη) = q`;
* **flushness** (`GeodesicLoop.GExp_add_two_mul_of_velocity_eq_neg`) gives `β(2m) = p`;
* `β'(-m) = w₁`: indeed the ray of `η = -ξ` is `t ↦ β(-t)`, so its velocity at `m` is `-β'(-m)`,
  and `w₁ = -w₂ = β'(-m)`;
* hence the closed curve `γ(t) := β(t)` for `t ∈ [0,m]`, `β(t - 2m)` for `t ∈ [m, 2m]` is
  **C¹** (the two one-sided velocities at `t = m` are `β'(m) = w₁` and `β'(-m) = w₁`) and
  geodesic on each piece, so it is a **closed unit-speed geodesic of length `2m`**; in particular the
  ray `β` is periodic of period `2m`.

**WARNING (this is the point of the two applications of Xu).**  Neither `w₁ = -w₂` nor `η = -ξ` is
automatic: `η = -ξ` is exactly the statement that `GExp p (-mξ) = q`, which is what makes the
"second branch" close up at `p`; and `β'(m) = β'(-m)` (equivalently `w₁ = -w₂`, which we have) is
exactly what makes the curve C¹ at `t = m`.  **Both** are needed, and they come from Xu's Theorem B
applied at `p` and at `q` respectively.  The application at `q` needs `InjAtLeast q m`, which
§127.2 supplies because `m = injSup` is a *uniform* radius.

