  `RayConj.locallyInjectiveAtGExp_of_norm_lt_pi_div_sqrt` applicable to the first member, which is the
  key to ruling out the degeneracy.

The remaining step of (N4b) is the **compactness/limit** step, and it has exactly one soft spot.  Let
`x n → p`, `u n ≠ v n` at `x n` (norms `< r < π/√K`, common image), and let `ũ n`, `ṽ n` be the
`chartAt p` coordinates of `u n`, `v n`.  Bounded ⟹ a subsequence converges, say `ũ → ã`, `ṽ → b̃`.
Then:

1. **`ã ≠ b̃`** — proved from local injectivity: `u' n := frameVector z₀ (x n) (ũ n) ≠ 0` (after the
   normalisation above), `‖u' n‖ < r < π/√K`, so `locallyInjectiveAtGExp_of_norm_lt_pi_div_sqrt` gives
   `LocallyInjectiveAtGExp hmet (x n) (u' n)`, a neighbourhood of `u' n` on which `GExp (x n)` is
   injective; the companion `v' n` converges to `u' n` **in the fibre norm** (the chart Gram bounds on
   a compact piece give `‖v' n - u' n‖ ≤ √Λ ‖ṽ n - ũ n‖ → 0`), so for large `n` both lie in that
   neighbourhood and have the same image, forcing `v' n = u' n` — contradiction.  **This part is
   already completely reduced to machinery the project has.**
2. **The limit pair has a common image**: `GExp p (frameVector z₀ p ã) = GExp p (frameVector z₀ p b̃)`,
   plus `‖·‖ ≤ r` for both, giving `¬ InjAtLeast hmet p R` for `R > r` — **and this is the soft
   spot.**  Passing the identities `GExp (x n) u n = GExp (x n) v n` to the limit needs **joint
   continuity of `(q, a) ↦ GExp q (frameVector z₀ q a)` in the base point `q`**, and the project only
   has this
   * for `‖a‖ ≤ η` with `η ≤ 1/(2 · flowScale D)` — `GExpJoint.contMDiffOn_GExp_frameVector`, whose
     bound is intrinsic to the chart-flow model (`GExp q (frameVector z₀ q a)` is *defined* by running
     the chart flow `D.α` for the time `flowScale D⁻¹`, and `D.α` is only guaranteed for `|t| ≤ ρ`);
   * for a **fixed** base point and `‖a‖` up to the injectivity radius —
     `GExpBoundary.tendsto_GExp_of_tendsto_of_norm_le` /
     `InjRadiusCut.tendsto_GExp_smul_of_tendsto`.

   Three ways out, in order of preference:
   * **(i) Iterate the ray continuation.**  `GExpGlob.GExp_add_smul` splits a ray of length `≤ r` into
     `⌈r/ρ⌉` chunks of length `≤ ρ`; each chunk is jointly continuous where the chart model applies, so
     the composite is jointly continuous on `Z × {‖a‖ ≤ r}` — this makes the joint continuity available
     for **all** vectors at once and is the reusable form (a self-contained lemma worth having:
     "`(q, a) ↦ GExp q (frameVector z₀ q a)` is continuous on `Z ×ˢ closedBall 0 r`").
   * **(ii) Compare `r` with the phase radius** and choose `r ∈ (m, η)`; this needs `m < η`, i.e. a
     comparison of the global injectivity radius with the chart-flow phase radius — plausible
     (`UniformInj`'s own `η i` is the same kind of quantity) but not available.
   * **(iii) Use the global geodesic flow** (`ChartFlowModel`/`FlowJacobi`), which is the same as (i)
     but via a single chart-flow iteration rather than the `GExp`-level one.

*(i) is the recommended next step.*

(N4b) **THE LIMIT — and the degeneracy is now disposed of.**  The remaining task is to turn the
sequence of (N4a) into a *single* point `p` with `inj(p) = m`.  The naive extraction (pass to a
subsequence `p n → p`, `ξ n → ξ`, `η n → η`) **cannot work as it stands**: in a totally geodesic
degenerate situation (e.g. the round sphere, where *every* unit direction at `p` gives a minimising
ray of length `π`) the two directions `ξ n`, `η n` may both converge to the *same* direction, and then
the limit carries no information at all.

The way out uses the fact that we are in the range `m < π/√K`, so we may work at radii
`r ∈ (m, π/√K)`:

> **Claim.**  For `r ∈ (m, π/√K)`, the set `W r := {p : ¬ InjAtLeast hmet p r}` is **closed**.

*Proof.*  Let `p n ∈ W r` with `p n → p`.  In a chart at `p`, the witness pairs
`u n ≠ v n ∈ T_{p n}M` (same image, norms `< r`) have bounded chart coordinates, hence a subsequence
converges to vectors `u, v ∈ T_pM` with the same image and norms `≤ r`.  If `u = v`, then the chart
coordinates of `u n` and `v n` converge to a common value whose fibre norm is `< r < π/√K`, and
**`RayConj.locallyInjectiveAtGExp_of_norm_lt_pi_div_sqrt`** applied at `(p n, u n)` says `GExp (p n)`
is injective on a neighbourhood of `u n`; since `v n → u n`, for large `n` both are in that
neighbourhood and have the same image, so `v n = u n` — contradiction.  Hence `u ≠ v`, and then
`¬ InjAtLeast hmet p r'` for every `r' > r` (norms `≤ r < r'`).  So every limit point of `W r` lies in
`⋂_{r'>r} W r' ⊆ W r`, i.e. `W r` is closed. ∎

> **Attainment.**  Since `¬ InjAllAtLeast hmet r` for `r > m`, each `W r` is nonempty, and `W` is
> monotone in `r`; the finite-intersection property for the decreasing sequence of nonempty compact
> sets `W (m + 1/k)` (for `k` large enough that `m + 1/k < π/√K`) gives a point
> `p ∈ ⋂_{r > m} W r`, i.e. `¬ InjAtLeast hmet p r` for **every** `r > m`.  With `InjAtLeast hmet p m`
> (from `injAllAtLeast_injSup`) this is exactly `inj(p) = m`.

The two analytic inputs are therefore: (i) the compactness step in a chart at the limit point (the
same machinery as `XuTrap`/`XuMain`: `GExpJoint.contMDiffOn_GExp_frameVector`,
`UniformInj.GExp_eq_symm_Hfun`, `ChartBounds.exists_chartGram_bounds`), and (ii) the local-injectivity
lemma above.  **No continuity of `p ↦ inj(p)` is needed**, and no appeal to the classical (true but
unproved-here) continuity theorem.

(N4) **THE REMAINING PIECE — the extraction of §127.2, and it now comes with a bonus.**  The plan is
as in §127.2: with `m := injSup hmet` (`InjAllAtLeast hmet m`, `¬ InjAllAtLeast hmet r` for `r > m`)
and the assumption `m < π/√K`, take `p_n` with maximal radius `m_n := inj(p_n) ∈ [m, m + 1/(n+1))`,
apply the cut-point step (`RayConj.exists_two_minimising_of_injAtLeast_of_lt_pi_div_sqrt`) at each
`p_n`, and pass to a limit `p_n → p`, `m_n → m`, `ξ_n → ξ`, `η_n → η` (using
`GExpJoint.contMDiffOn_GExp_frameVector` for the base-point dependence).

**The bonus (important).**  The limit configuration gives *two distinct vectors of norm `m` at `p`
with the same image*, hence `¬ InjAtLeast hmet p r` for every `r > m`, and `InjAtLeast hmet p m` from
`InjAllAtLeast hmet m`.  So **the extraction proves the attainment of `inf_p inj(p)`** — i.e. N4
does not need any separate argument that `p ↦ inj(p)` attains its infimum, which is the point the
literature waves at (see §127.2).  Nothing else in the even-case proof needs it: in the case
`m ≥ π/√K` the conclusion `InjAllAtLeast hmet (π/√K)` is immediate.

**`ξ ≠ η` in the limit** is forced: if `ξ = η`, then the limiting Xu relation `w₁ = -w₂` would give
`velocity (ray ξ) m = -velocity (ray ξ) m = 0`, contradicting unit speed.

Filling in N4 turns N2+N3 into the even-dimensional Klingenberg estimate: `InjAllAtLeast hmet (π/√K)`
(hence `∃ p q, ofReal (π/2) < edist p q` for `M` compact even-dimensional orientable with `sec ≤ K`,
`K < 4`, `0 < K`), i.e. **(K2) for even dimensions** — the even-dimensional topological sphere
theorem.  The odd-dimensional case (Klingenberg 1961) still needs the free-loop-space minimax.

(N4') The extraction of §127.2 (packaging `p`, `m = injSup`, `q`, `ξ`, `η`, `w₁`, `w₂`).

