## §128 SESSION 28 — WANG'S LECTURE NOTES READ: THE EXACT CLASSICAL STRUCTURE OF THE EVEN CASE, AND THE SINGLE MISSING BRICK ISOLATED (joint continuity of `GExp` in the base point)

### 128.1 THE SOURCE AND ITS EXACT CONTENT

`staff.ustc.edu.cn/~wangzuoq/Courses/16S-RiemGeom/Notes/Lec22.pdf` (*Lecture 22: the sphere theorem*)
contains, in §1, the complete even-dimensional argument.  Verbatim structure:

* `inj_p(M,g) = sup{r | exp_p is a diffeo on B_r(0)} = d(p, Cut(p))` and
  `inj(M,g) = inf_p d(p, Cut(p))`.
* **Proposition 1.1.**  `p ∈ M`, `q ∈ Cut(p)` with `d(p,q) = d(p,Cut(p))`.  Then either (1) `q` is
  conjugate to `p` along a minimising geodesic, or (2) there are exactly two minimising geodesics
  `γ, σ` from `p` to `q` and `γ̇(l) = -σ̇(l)`, `l = d(p,q)`.  *Proof*: if `q` is not conjugate and
  `γ̇(l) ≠ -σ̇(l)`, push the endpoint out along `exp_q(sX_q)` with `⟨X_q,γ̇(l)⟩ < 0`,
  `⟨X_q,σ̇(l)⟩ < 0`; the first variation formula gives *two* minimising geodesics from `p` to
  `exp_q(sX_q)` of length `< l = inj(p)`, contradicting injectivity.
  **This is exactly `XuTrap.xuCornerExcluded`** (which needs only *one* non-conjugacy hypothesis).
* **Corollary 1.2 (Klingenberg).**  `sec ≤ C` (or `K ≤ C`), `M` compact.  **Either**
  `inj(M) ≥ π/√C`, **or** there is a closed geodesic `γ` of *minimal length among all closed
  geodesics* with `inj(M) = ½L(γ)`.  *Proof.*  Take `p` and `q ∈ Cut(p)` with `d(p,q) = inj(M)`.
  If `q` is conjugate to `p`, `inj(M) = d(p,q) ≥ π/√C` (lecture 20 = our Rauch).  Otherwise
  Prop. 1.1 at `p` gives `σ̇(l) = -τ̇(l)`; **`p` is also a cut point of `q` and by definition
  realises the distance from `q` to `Cut(q)`, so Prop. 1.1 at `q` gives `σ̇(0) = -τ̇(0)`, and `σ ∪ τ`
  is a closed geodesic** `γ` with `inj(M) = ½L(γ)`.  Minimality: if `γ'` were shorter, the two
  antipodal points `p',q'` of `γ'` would give `d(p',q'') ≤ ½L(γ') < ½L(γ) = inj(M)` for the closest
  cut point `q''` of `p'` — contradiction.
* **Theorem 1.3, even case.**  `sec ≤ 1`, `M` compact simply connected (indeed: orientable, even).
  Assume `l = inj(M) < π`.  By Cor. 1.2 there is a closed geodesic `γ` through `p = γ(0)`, `q = γ(l)`
  with `L(γ) = 2l < 2π` and minimal length.  **Synge**: there is `X(t)` parallel along `γ` with
  `X(2l) = X(0) = X_p ∈ γ̇(0)^⊥` and `E''(γ_s) = -∫ R(γ̇,X,γ̇,X) < 0`, so `L(γ_s) < L(γ)` for small
  `s ≠ 0`.  Then `p_s := γ_s(0)`, `q_s :=` **the farthest point of `γ_s` from `p_s`**:
  `d(p_s,q_s) < l = inj(M)`, so there is a **unique** minimising geodesic `σ_s` from `q_s` to `p_s`;
  a subsequence `s_i → 0` has `σ̇_{s_i}(0) → Y_q ∈ T_qM` and `σ = exp_q(tY_q)` is a minimising
  geodesic from `q` to `p`; by the first variation formula at the *farthest* point
  (`0 = d/dt E(σ_{s,t})|_{t=l_s} = -⟨σ̇_s(0), γ̇_s(l_s)⟩`) one has `σ̇(0) ⊥ γ̇(l)`, so `σ` is a
  **third** minimising geodesic from `q` to `p` — contradicting Prop. 1.1 (exactly two).

### 128.2 WHAT THIS SETTLES, AND WHAT IT DOES *NOT* SETTLE

* (a) **The attainment is genuinely used** — twice: `p` must satisfy `d(p, Cut(p)) = inj(M)`, and the
  *second* application of Prop. 1.1 (at `q`) uses the automatic identity
  `inj(q) = d(q,Cut(q))`: indeed `inj(M) ≤ inj(q) ≤ d(q,p) = inj(M)`, so `p` is a closest cut point of
  `q`.  **This is why `KlingenbergEven.eq_neg_of_xuCornerExcluded_at_target` needs
  `hinjall : InjAllAtLeast hmet m`**: that hypothesis is used only to get `InjAtLeast q m` at the
  meeting point, i.e. `inj(q) ≥ m`; combined with the automatic `inj(q) ≤ m` (two distinct vectors of
  norm `m` at `q` with the same image) one has `inj(q) = m` — so the *only* content of `hinjall` at
  `q` is the attainment `m = injSup`.
* (b) **No `hdeep` is needed for the even case**: the contradiction comes from the "third minimising
  geodesic", not from a loop-length bound.  So the even case is *independent* of the
  free-loop-space minimax (the wall).  Confirmed.
* (c) **The final compactness step uses limit arguments on geometrically varying objects**:
  `σ̇_{s_i}(0) → Y_q` (limits of unit tangent vectors at converging base points), `q_s → q`,
  `σ = exp_q(tY_q)`.  Every one of these is an instance of **joint continuity of
  `(q,v) ↦ GExp q v` in the base point `q`**.
* (d) **The iteration `p ↦ q` does not avoid the attainment.**  One might hope to iterate the
  cut-point construction: at `p_0` with maximal radius `l_0 < π/√C`, the cut point `q_0 =: p_1` is at
  distance `l_0`, and one gets the *value-closed* geodesic loop of length `2l_0` at `p_0` **without any
  second application of Prop. 1.1** (this is `GeodesicLoop.isGeodesicLoopAt_of_velocity_eq_neg`);
  moreover `inj(p_1) ≤ l_0` always (the two vectors `l_0 • (-w₁)`, `l_0 • w₁` at `p_1` are distinct
  with the same image `p_0`), with equality **iff** `p_0` is a closest cut point of `p_1`, and in that
  case Prop. 1.1 at `p_1` gives the velocity matching `σ̇(0) = -τ̇(0)` and a **genuine** closed
  geodesic.  But the radii `l_0 ≥ l_1 ≥ l_2 ≥ …` need *not* stabilise, and stabilisation up to a limit
  point is again a compactness/limit argument.  **No shortcut: joint continuity is unavoidable.**
  (The value-closed loop version is *also* useless for Synge: the holonomy around a value-closed loop
  with `β̇(2l) = -τ̇(0) ≠ β̇(0)` does not preserve `β̇(0)^⊥`, so the fixed-vector argument
  `Commons.LinearAlgebra.Orthogonal.fixed_vector_compl_of_det_one` does not apply.)

### 128.3 THE SINGLE MISSING BRICK: JOINT CONTINUITY OF `GExp` IN THE BASE POINT

**Statement to prove** (the form the two applications need — a sequential limit in a chart at the
limit point):

> `theorem tendsto_GExp_frameVector` : for `z₀ : M`, `p ∈ (chartAt H z₀).source`, sequences
> `q : ℕ → M`, `a : ℕ → E` with `q n → p` and `a n → a`,
> `Tendsto (fun n => GExp (q n) (frameVector z₀ (q n) (a n))) atTop (𝓝 (GExp p (frameVector z₀ p a)))`.

(Note `frameVector z₀ (q n) (a n)` is the intrinsic vector whose chart-`z₀` coordinates are `a n`;
`GExp` is `Submission.GlobalExp.GExp`, and `frameVector`/`clm` are the conversions of
`Submission.GExpFlow`.)

**Why it is not available now**: the project has joint smoothness only on
`Z ×ˢ closedBall 0 η` with `η ≤ 1/(2 flowScale D) = D.ρ/4` (`GExpJoint.contMDiffOn_GExp_frameVector`),
i.e. for vectors whose **coordinate norm** is bounded by the *chart-flow time* `D.ρ` of a fixed chart
piece; and at a fixed base point up to the injectivity radius
(`GExpBoundary.tendsto_GExp_of_tendsto_of_norm_le`).  `a n` is arbitrary.

**The proof: CHUNKED CHART-FLOW.**

* *A single chart-flow application reaches coordinate vectors of norm up to `D.ρ`* (better than the
  existing `D.ρ/4`: use `GExp_eq_chartFlow` with `u := τ • w`, `‖w‖ ≤ 1/flowScale D`, `τ ≤ D.ρ`; the
  phase is `(φ q, flowScale D • (τ • w))`).  But `D.ρ` is bounded by the geometry and cannot be made
  large, so a long ray needs several chart pieces.
* **The state recursion.**  Fix the *limit* data: the chart sequence `z 0, …, z N` and compact pieces
  `Z k` with `x k ∈ Z k ⊆ (chartAt H (z k)).source`, and the chunk lengths `τ k > 0` with
  `Σ τ k = 1`, chosen so that the ray `t ↦ GExp p (t • frameVector z₀ p a)` maps `[t_k, t_{k+1}]`
  into `Z k` and the chunk vector `τ k • (velocity)` has small enough chart-`z k` coordinates that
  the phase lies in `(D k).O` (with room).  Define the *states* (chart-`z k` coordinates of the point
  and of the velocity of the ray, normalised so that the parameter runs over `[0,1]`):
  * `st 0 n = ((extChartAt I (z 0)) (q n), a n)`, `st 0 = ((extChartAt I (z 0)) p, a)`;
  * the **chunk step** in chart `z k` with data `D k`, phase velocity `c_k = flowScale (D k)`:
    `(x, w) ↦ D k . α ((x, c_k • (τ k • w)), c_k⁻¹)` — this is a jointly `C^∞` map of `(x, w)`
    (`D k.hjoint`), and by `GExpFlow.GExp_eq_chartFlow` it computes
    `GExp (symm (x)) (frameVector (z k) (symm x) (τ k • w))` together with its velocity at parameter
    `1` (`GExpFlowVelocity.curveVelocity_GExp_chartFlow`), both expressed in chart `z k` coordinates;
  * the **transition** to chart `z (k+1)`: from the output `(x', w') ∈ E × E` of the chunk (position
    and velocity in chart `z k` coordinates) form
    `(x'', w'') = ((extChartAt I (z (k+1))) ((extChartAt I (z k)).symm x'),
                  (trivializationAt E (TangentSpace I) (z (k+1))).continuousLinearMapAt ℝ
                    ((extChartAt I (z k)).symm x')
                    (frameVector (I := I) (z k) ((extChartAt I (z k)).symm x') w'))`.
    This is jointly `C^∞` on the domain where `(extChartAt I (z k)).symm x'` lies in both sources
    (use `contMDiffOn_coordChangeL` + `Trivialization.symmL_apply`/
    `continuousLinearMapAt_apply_of_mem`, cf. `Submission.ChartCoordsTrans.chartCoords_eq_coordChangeL`).
* **The identity.**  For `n` large the fixed chunk data satisfy the phase conditions for the sequence
  as well (by convergence and openness), and then, by induction on `k` using
  `GExpGlob.GExp_add_smul` (the ray is `t ↦ GExp (q n) (t • frameVector (z 0) (q n) (a n))`, unit
  direction after normalising `a n` by its norm),
  `GExp (q n) (frameVector (z 0) (q n) (a n)) = (extChartAt I (z N)).symm ((st N n).1)`,
  and the same for the limit.  Both sides are continuous in `n`
  (`contMDiffOn_extChartAt_symm … |>.continuousAt`), and `st N n → st N` by the induction, giving the
  result.
* **Zero handling**: if `a = 0` the conclusion is `GExp (q n) u n → p` for `‖u n‖ → 0`, which follows
  from `riemannianEDist (q n) (GExp (q n) u n) ≤ ofReal ‖u n‖ → 0`
  (the ray is a curve of length `‖u n‖`) and the description of the manifold topology by
  `EDist.setOf_riemannianEDist_lt_subset_nhds`.  So assume `a ≠ 0` and work with the unit direction
  `a / ‖a‖` and the parameter `‖a‖`.

*Alternative (weaker) formulations that are enough for the applications*: (i) the *minimising* case
(`d(x n, y n) = ‖u n‖` with `‖u n‖ → m`); (ii) the case where all `x n` lie in a fixed compact chart
piece and `‖a n‖` is bounded by the chart-flow reach.  **(ii) is exactly what the existing
`GExpJoint.contMDiffOn_GExp_frameVector` gives, so the *non-trivial* content of the brick is the
passage across chart boundaries.**

